/* Practice bank: ten practice tests of 30 questions in the SAT Reading and
   Writing format, all written for Lexicon from scratch in October 2026
   (passages, options and explanations; names in them are invented). None
   comes from College Board or any other publisher.
   t     the practice test, 1-10; each is 15 words-in-context questions, then
         15 rules questions, and is shown in this order
   sec   wic = words in context (every option is a word from data/words.js, so
         the app shows its meaning), wr = rules and expression
   ex    English summary (always shown); ex_<lang> the same in another language
   why   one English explanation per option, in option order */
var QUESTIONS = [
/* Practice 1 */
{"id":"w1","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"For decades, historians assumed the canal was dug in a single campaign. Newly dated tool marks along its walls ___ that view: they show at least three separate periods of construction, decades apart.","o":["corroborate","embellish","undermine","foretell"],"a":2,"ex":"The colon introduces evidence (three periods) that conflicts with 'a single campaign', so the evidence weakens the old view: undermine.","ex_tr":"İki noktadan sonraki kanıt (üç ayrı inşa dönemi) 'tek seferde kazıldı' görüşüyle çelişiyor; kanıt bu görüşü zayıflatıyor: undermine.","why":["Corroborate means to confirm. Three separate building periods contradict a single campaign, so the marks cannot confirm that view.","Embellish means to decorate or exaggerate. The tool marks do not add detail to the old view; they work against it.","Correct. Undermine means to weaken. Evidence of three separate periods weakens the idea of one campaign.","Foretell means to predict. Dating old tool marks looks back at the past and predicts nothing."]},
{"id":"w2","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The museum's new director is known for being ___ with public money: she renegotiated every contract, canceled two costly exhibitions, and still finished the year under budget.","o":["profligate","ostentatious","frugal","capricious"],"a":2,"ex":"Renegotiating contracts, canceling costly shows and finishing under budget all describe careful spending: frugal.","ex_tr":"Sözleşmeleri yeniden pazarlık etmek, pahalı sergileri iptal etmek ve yılı bütçenin altında bitirmek dikkatli harcamayı anlatır: frugal (tutumlu).","why":["Profligate means recklessly wasteful, the opposite of cutting costs and finishing under budget.","Ostentatious means showy, meant to impress. Nothing in the text is about display.","Correct. Frugal means careful and sparing with money, which is exactly what renegotiating and canceling costly shows shows.","Capricious means changing suddenly without reason. Her actions are consistent and deliberate."]},
{"id":"w3","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the two languages are spoken in neighboring valleys, their grammars are strikingly ___: one marks tense on every verb, while the other does not mark tense at all.","o":["disparate","homogeneous","analogous","redundant"],"a":0,"ex":"'Although ... neighboring valleys' sets up a surprise, and the colon shows the grammars differ completely: disparate.","ex_tr":"'Although' bir beklentiyi bozuyor: vadiler komşu olduğu hâlde iki dilin dilbilgisi birbirinden tamamen farklı: disparate.","why":["Correct. Disparate means fundamentally different: one grammar marks tense everywhere, the other never does.","Homogeneous means all of the same kind, the opposite of what the colon goes on to show.","Analogous means similar in some respect. The colon shows a difference, not a likeness.","Redundant means unnecessary or repeated. The sentence compares the grammars; it does not judge them useless."]},
{"id":"w4","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The engineer's report was admirably ___: in two pages it identified the cause of the leak, the cost of the repair, and the date the work could begin.","o":["verbose","esoteric","nebulous","succinct"],"a":3,"ex":"'In two pages' plus three precise facts describes writing that is short and clear: succinct.","ex_tr":"'İki sayfada' üç net bilgi vermek kısa ve öz yazmaktır: succinct.","why":["Verbose means using too many words, the opposite of covering everything in two pages.","Esoteric means understood only by a few specialists. Nothing suggests the report was hard to follow.","Nebulous means vague. The report gave a precise cause, cost and date.","Correct. Succinct means brief and clearly expressed."]},
{"id":"w5","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Many visitors assume the desert is lifeless, but its plants are remarkably ___: some seeds lie in the sand for years and sprout within days of the first heavy rain.","o":["tranquil","feeble","docile","resilient"],"a":3,"ex":"'But' contrasts 'lifeless' with plants that survive years of drought and spring back after rain: resilient.","ex_tr":"'But' cansız sanılan çölü, yıllarca bekleyip ilk yağmurda filizlenen tohumlarla karşılaştırıyor: resilient (dayanıklı, toparlanabilen).","why":["Tranquil means calm and peaceful. The text is about surviving drought, not about calm.","Feeble means weak, the opposite of surviving years of drought.","Docile means easily controlled or obedient, a word for animals or people, not for survival.","Correct. Resilient means able to survive hardship and recover, like seeds that wait years and then sprout."]},
{"id":"w6","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The committee's decision seemed ___ to the applicants: no criteria had been published, and two nearly identical proposals received opposite verdicts.","o":["unequivocal","judicious","arbitrary","meticulous"],"a":2,"ex":"No published criteria and opposite verdicts for nearly identical proposals mean the decision followed no clear reason: arbitrary.","ex_tr":"Yayımlanmış ölçüt yok ve neredeyse aynı iki başvuruya zıt kararlar verilmiş: karar bir mantığa dayanmıyor: arbitrary (keyfî).","why":["Unequivocal means clear and leaving no doubt. The complaint is about a lack of reasons, not unclear wording.","Judicious means showing good judgment. Missing criteria and inconsistent verdicts suggest the opposite.","Correct. Arbitrary means based on chance or whim rather than a reason or system.","Meticulous means extremely careful. Opposite verdicts for nearly identical proposals are not careful work."]},
{"id":"w7","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"At first the researchers thought the bird's song was a fixed inheritance, but recordings of young birds raised apart showed that the song is ___: it changes depending on which adults the chicks hear.","o":["immutable","innate","malleable","vestigial"],"a":2,"ex":"'But' reverses 'fixed inheritance', and the colon says the song changes with what the chicks hear: malleable.","ex_tr":"'But' 'sabit, kalıtsal' fikrini tersine çeviriyor; iki noktadan sonrası şarkının yavruların duyduklarına göre değiştiğini söylüyor: malleable (şekillendirilebilir).","why":["Immutable means unchangeable, the opposite of a song that changes.","Innate means present from birth. That was the researchers' first idea, which the recordings overturned.","Correct. Malleable means able to be shaped or changed.","Vestigial means left over and no longer useful. The text is about change, not about leftovers."]},
{"id":"w8","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The new irrigation system was meant to ___ the effects of the drought, and it did: harvests fell by only a tenth, compared with a third on farms without it.","o":["exacerbate","disclose","emulate","mitigate"],"a":3,"ex":"Losses of a tenth instead of a third show the system made the drought less harmful: mitigate.","ex_tr":"Kayıp üçte bir yerine onda birde kaldı; sistem kuraklığın etkisini hafifletti: mitigate.","why":["Exacerbate means to make worse. The system reduced the losses.","Disclose means to reveal information. A system does not reveal a drought's effects; it changes them.","Emulate means to imitate. Nothing is being copied.","Correct. Mitigate means to make less severe: losses of a tenth instead of a third."]},
{"id":"w9","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Critics had called the author's first novel ___, but her second book silenced them: reviewers praised its original plot and characters unlike any in recent fiction.","o":["ingenious","insipid","inimitable","lucid"],"a":1,"ex":"'But her second book silenced them' means the critics had said something negative, and praise for originality answers a charge of dullness: insipid.","ex_tr":"'İkinci kitabı eleştirmenleri susturdu' ilk kitap için olumsuz bir şey söylendiğini gösterir; özgünlüğe övgü 'yavan' eleştirisine cevaptır: insipid.","why":["Ingenious means very clever. That is praise, which would need no silencing.","Correct. Insipid means dull and lacking interest; the praise for originality answers exactly that charge.","Inimitable means impossible to copy, again praise, and the opposite of a dull first book.","Lucid means clear and easy to understand, also praise, so it creates no contrast."]},
{"id":"w10","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Because the treaty's wording was ___, each government read it as supporting its own claim to the river, and the dispute dragged on for another decade.","o":["ambiguous","explicit","cogent","provisional"],"a":0,"ex":"Each side read the same words as support for itself, so the wording allowed two meanings: ambiguous.","ex_tr":"Aynı metni iki hükümet de kendi lehine okuyabildi; demek ki ifade birden fazla anlama açıktı: ambiguous.","why":["Correct. Ambiguous means open to more than one interpretation, so each side could read it its own way.","Explicit means stated clearly. A clear text could not support two opposite claims.","Cogent means logical and convincing. That describes an argument, and it does not explain two readings.","Provisional means temporary. The problem is two readings, not how long the treaty would last."]},
{"id":"w11","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The volunteers' ___ effort over three summers paid off: they cataloged every one of the 4,000 headstones in the cemetery, including many that had been hidden under moss.","o":["tenacious","sporadic","frivolous","superficial"],"a":0,"ex":"Three summers of work that recorded every one of 4,000 stones is an effort that never gave up: tenacious.","ex_tr":"Üç yaz boyunca 4.000 mezar taşının hepsini kaydetmek pes etmeyen bir çabadır: tenacious (azimli).","why":["Correct. Tenacious means persistent, refusing to give up.","Sporadic means happening only now and then. Three summers of work that reached every stone was steady.","Frivolous means not serious. Cataloging 4,000 headstones is serious work.","Superficial means shallow. Uncovering stones hidden under moss is thorough, not shallow."]},
{"id":"w12","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"In her memoir, the diplomat is unusually ___ about her own mistakes, describing in detail the misjudgments that cost her country a trade agreement.","o":["evasive","candid","reticent","complacent"],"a":1,"ex":"Describing her own misjudgments 'in detail' shows openness: candid.","ex_tr":"Hatalarını ayrıntılarıyla anlatması açık sözlülüğü gösterir: candid (dürüst, açık sözlü).","why":["Evasive means avoiding direct answers, the opposite of describing mistakes in detail.","Correct. Candid means honest and frank, as describing her own misjudgments in detail is.","Reticent means reluctant to reveal things, again the opposite.","Complacent means self-satisfied and unaware of problems. She openly examines her mistakes."]},
{"id":"w13","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"The study's sample of 40 students is too small to support such ___ conclusions; the authors claim their findings apply to every school in the country.","o":["broad","tentative","esoteric","nuanced"],"a":0,"ex":"A claim about every school in the country is very wide, and 40 students cannot carry it: broad.","ex_tr":"Bulguların ülkedeki her okul için geçerli olduğunu iddia etmek çok geniş kapsamlı bir sonuçtur; 40 öğrenci buna yetmez: broad.","why":["Correct. Broad means wide in scope, as a claim about every school in the country is.","Tentative means hesitant and uncertain. The authors make a confident claim about every school.","Esoteric means understood by few. The problem is the size of the claim, not who can understand it.","Nuanced means showing subtle distinctions. A claim that applies everywhere ignores distinctions."]},
{"id":"w14","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Once among the most ___ figures in the city's politics, the former mayor now rarely appears in public, and many younger residents do not recognize her name.","o":["peripheral","pivotal","obscure","reticent"],"a":1,"ex":"'Once ... now' builds a contrast: someone forgotten today was once at the center of the city's politics: pivotal.","ex_tr":"'Once ... now' bir karşıtlık kuruyor: bugün unutulmuş biri bir zamanlar şehir siyasetinin merkezindeydi: pivotal (kilit, çok önemli).","why":["Peripheral means minor, on the edges. 'Once ... now' needs a contrast with being forgotten.","Correct. Pivotal means of central importance, which contrasts with being forgotten now.","Obscure means little known. That describes her now, not then.","Reticent means reserved and quiet. It describes a personality and sets up no contrast with fame."]},
{"id":"w15","t":1,"sec":"wic","p":"Rather than ___ the old factory, the town converted it into a library, keeping the brick walls and the iron roof beams that residents had known for a century.","o":["salvage","obliterate","replenish","venerate"],"a":1,"ex":"'Rather than' sets the rejected choice against what the town did, which was keep the building: obliterate.","ex_tr":"'Rather than' reddedilen seçeneği yapılanla karşılaştırır: kasaba binayı yıkmak yerine duvarlarını ve kirişlerini koruyarak kütüphaneye çevirdi: obliterate (yerle bir etmek).","why":["Salvage means to rescue from loss. Converting the building and keeping its walls is salvaging it, so 'rather than' would make no sense.","Correct. Obliterate means to destroy completely, which 'rather than' sets against keeping the walls and beams.","Replenish means to fill up again. A building is not refilled.","Venerate means to respect deeply. Keeping the walls shows respect, so 'rather than venerate' contradicts the sentence."]},
{"id":"r1","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The glassblower heated the rod until it glowed ___ then she shaped the bowl with a wet wooden paddle.","o":["orange: and","orange,","orange","orange;"],"a":3,"ex":"Two independent clauses: join them with a semicolon. A comma alone makes a comma splice, and 'then' cannot join clauses.","ex_tr":"İki tam cümle noktalı virgülle bağlanır. Tek virgül 'comma splice' olur; 'then' bağlaç değildir.","why":["A colon followed by 'and' is never right; joining with 'and' needs a comma, not a colon.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice; 'then' is not a joining word.","With no punctuation, the two sentences run together.","Correct. Both halves are complete sentences, and a semicolon can join two complete sentences."]},
{"id":"r2","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The recipe calls for only three ___ flour, water, and salt.","o":["ingredients:","ingredients;","ingredients, including:","ingredients"],"a":0,"ex":"A complete clause ('The recipe calls for only three ingredients') introduces a list, so use a colon.","ex_tr":"Tam bir cümleden sonra iki nokta bir listeyi tanıtır. 'including'den sonra iki nokta konmaz.","why":["Correct. A colon after a complete sentence introduces a list.","A semicolon needs a complete sentence on both sides; 'flour, water, and salt' is not one.","A colon never follows 'including'.","With no punctuation, the list runs straight into 'ingredients'."]},
{"id":"r3","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"In 2019, Ana ___ completed the longest timber footbridge in the region.","o":["Molina; a self-taught engineer,","Molina, a self-taught engineer","Molina a self-taught engineer,","Molina, a self-taught engineer,"],"a":3,"ex":"An appositive (extra information renaming a noun) needs a comma on both sides.","ex_tr":"'a self-taught engineer' ek bilgidir (appositive); iki virgülle açılıp kapanmalıdır.","why":["A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'In 2019, Ana Molina' is not one.","The extra information is opened with a comma but never closed.","The extra information is closed with a comma but never opened.","Correct. 'A self-taught engineer' is extra information about Ana Molina, opened and closed by a pair of commas."]},
{"id":"r4","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The documentary features ___ whose recordings of whale songs have reached millions of listeners.","o":["oceanographer Lena Hart,","oceanographer, Lena Hart,","oceanographer, Lena Hart","oceanographer Lena Hart"],"a":0,"ex":"Title + name takes no comma; a 'whose' clause after a name adds extra information, so a comma comes first.","ex_tr":"Unvan + isim ('oceanographer Lena Hart') virgülsüz yazılır; isimden sonra gelen 'whose' cümleciği ek bilgidir, önüne virgül gelir.","why":["Correct. A title and a name ('oceanographer Lena Hart') take no comma, and the comma before 'whose' sets off the extra information.","A comma between 'oceanographer' and the name splits a title from the name it belongs to.","The same wrong comma, and no comma before the extra-information 'whose' clause.","The name already identifies her, so the 'whose' clause is extra information and needs a comma before it."]},
{"id":"r5","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The stack of old maps in the library's basement ___ been scanned by a team of volunteers.","o":["have","has","are","were"],"a":1,"ex":"Cross out the phrase 'of old maps in the library's basement': the subject is 'stack', singular, so 'has'.","ex_tr":"Edat öbeğini ('of old maps ...') çıkar: özne 'stack', tekil: has.","why":["'Have' agrees with 'maps', but 'maps' sits inside the phrase 'of old maps'; it is not the subject.","Correct. The subject is 'stack', which is singular.","'Are' is plural, and 'are been' is not a verb form.","'Were' is plural, and 'were been' is not a verb form."]},
{"id":"r6","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"Since the bike lanes opened in 2021, the number of cyclists on the avenue ___.","o":["has doubled","doubled","had doubled","will double"],"a":0,"ex":"'Since' + a past starting point describes something continuing up to now: present perfect, 'has doubled'.","ex_tr":"'Since 2021' geçmişten bugüne süren bir durumu gösterir: present perfect, has doubled.","why":["Correct. 'Since 2021' marks a change running from the past up to now, which takes the present perfect.","The simple past states a finished event at a past time and clashes with 'since'.","The past perfect needs a second, later past event to come before, and there is none.","The future does not fit a change that has already happened."]},
{"id":"r7","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"Hoping to reach the summit before the storm, ___","o":["the storm forced the climbers to leave camp at three in the morning.","camp was left by the climbers at three in the morning.","the climbers left camp at three in the morning.","three in the morning was when the climbers left camp."],"a":2,"ex":"An opening modifier describes whatever comes right after the comma. Only people can hope, so 'the climbers' must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade ('Hoping to reach the summit') virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi anlatır; umut eden tırmanıcılardır.","why":["This makes 'the storm' the one hoping to reach the summit.","This makes 'camp' the one hoping to reach the summit.","Correct. The climbers are the ones hoping, and they come right after the comma.","This makes 'three in the morning' the one hoping to reach the summit."]},
{"id":"r8","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"Bamboo can grow almost a meter in a single day. ___ a cleared hillside can be green again within one season.","o":["In contrast,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","As a result,"],"a":3,"ex":"Fast growth (cause) leads to a quickly green hillside (effect): 'As a result'.","ex_tr":"Hızlı büyüme sebep, yamacın bir mevsimde yeşermesi sonuç: As a result.","why":["'In contrast' signals an opposite idea, and the sentences agree.","'Nevertheless' signals something surprising despite the first sentence, but the second sentence follows from it.","'For instance' introduces an example of the first claim; the hillside is a consequence of fast growth, not an example of growing a meter a day.","Correct. Fast growth is the cause; a hillside turning green within a season is the result."]},
{"id":"r9","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The first edition of the atlas sold only a few hundred copies. ___ the revised edition, published ten years later, became a standard reference in schools.","o":["Similarly,","By contrast,","Therefore,","In other words,"],"a":1,"ex":"A few hundred copies versus a standard reference: the sentences contrast, so 'By contrast'.","ex_tr":"Birkaç yüz kopya satan ilk baskı ile okullarda standart kaynak olan yeni baskı zıt: By contrast.","why":["'Similarly' would mean the second edition also sold poorly.","Correct. Weak sales of the first edition are set against the revised edition's success.","'Therefore' would mean poor sales caused the later success.","'In other words' restates the first sentence, but the second says something new and opposite."]},
{"id":"r10","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"After months of debate, the city council finally announced ___ decision to rebuild the old pier.","o":["it's","their","its","they're"],"a":2,"ex":"A collective noun like 'council' is singular, and the possessive is 'its' (no apostrophe).","ex_tr":"'Council' tekil bir topluluk ismidir: its. 'It's' = it is.","why":["'It's' means 'it is', which makes no sense before 'decision'.","'Their' is plural, but 'council' is a single group.","Correct. 'Council' is one body, so the singular possessive 'its'.","'They're' means 'they are', which makes no sense here."]},
{"id":"r11","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The training program teaches volunteers to read tide charts, ___ and to radio the harbor in an emergency.","o":["how secure knots are tied,","tying secure knots,","to tie secure knots,","secure knots being tied,"],"a":2,"ex":"Items in a list must be parallel: to read ..., to tie ..., and to radio ....","ex_tr":"Listedeki öğeler aynı biçimde olmalı: to read ..., to tie ..., and to radio ....","why":["A 'how' clause does not match 'to read' and 'to radio'.","'Tying' breaks the 'to + verb' pattern of the other two items.","Correct. All three items share the same form: to read, to tie, to radio.","A passive '-ing' phrase does not match the other two items."]},
{"id":"r12","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's oldest bakery ___ still uses the wood-fired oven its founders built.","o":[": open since 1887 —","— open since 1887,",", open since 1887 —","— open since 1887 —"],"a":3,"ex":"A mid-sentence interruption opens and closes with the same mark: dash ... dash, or comma ... comma.","ex_tr":"Cümle ortasındaki ara söz aynı işaretle açılıp kapanır: tire ... tire.","why":["A colon must follow a complete sentence and cannot open an interruption.","It opens with a dash but closes with a comma; the marks must match.","It opens with a comma but closes with a dash; the marks must match.","Correct. An interruption opened with a dash is closed with a dash."]},
{"id":"r13","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The two ___ findings were published together in a single issue of the journal.","o":["laboratories'","laboratory's","laboratories","laboratorie's"],"a":0,"ex":"Plural ending in -s + possession: add only an apostrophe, 'laboratories''.","ex_tr":"İki laboratuvar (çoğul) + iyelik: laboratories'.","why":["Correct. Two laboratories own the findings: plural, then an apostrophe after the s.","'Laboratory's' is the possessive of one laboratory, but there are two.","'Laboratories' is plural but shows no possession, and the findings belong to them.","'Laboratorie's' is not a real form."]},
{"id":"r14","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"The village relies on a single spring, ___ supplies water to more than two hundred homes.","o":["this","it","which","they"],"a":2,"ex":"'Which' after a comma adds extra information; 'it' or 'this' would make a comma splice.","ex_tr":"Virgülden sonra 'which' ek bilgi cümleciği başlatır; 'it' veya 'this' comma splice yaratır.","why":["'This' also starts a new sentence after a comma: a comma splice.","'It' starts a new complete sentence after a comma: a comma splice.","Correct. After a comma, 'which' begins a clause of extra information about the spring.","'They' is plural for a single spring, and it also makes a comma splice."]},
{"id":"r15","t":1,"sec":"wr","p":"Either the coach or the players ___ responsible for putting the equipment away after practice.","o":["is","are","was","has been"],"a":1,"ex":"With either ... or, the verb agrees with the subject closer to it: 'players', plural, so 'are'.","ex_tr":"'Either ... or'da fiil yakındaki özneye uyar: 'players' çoğul: are.","why":["'Is' is singular; it would agree with 'coach', but 'players' is nearer.","Correct. With 'either ... or', the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players', which is plural.","'Was' is singular, and the sentence describes a habit in the present.","'Has been' is singular, so it cannot agree with 'players'."]},
/* Practice 2 */
{"id":"p2w1","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"After a decade of high prices persuaded farmers across the province to plant saffron, the ___ of the spice that followed pushed wholesale prices down by more than half in a single season.","o":["dearth","glut","depletion","veneration"],"a":1,"ex":"High prices drew farmers across the province into saffron, so the harvest that followed was more than buyers wanted, and too much supply drives prices down: glut.","ex_tr":"Yüksek fiyatlar yüzünden ilin dört bir yanındaki çiftçiler safran ekince piyasaya ihtiyaçtan çok daha fazla safran çıktı; arz fazlası fiyatları düşürür: glut (arz fazlası, aşırı bolluk).","why":["Dearth means a shortage. A shortage would push prices up, and a province full of new saffron farms would not create one.","Correct. A glut is an excessive supply: with farmers across the province growing saffron, there was more than buyers wanted, so prices fell by half.","Depletion is a reduction in supply. More farmers planting saffron adds to the supply, and a smaller supply would raise prices, not halve them.","Veneration is deep respect. Respect for a spice says nothing about how much of it there is and would not explain prices falling by half."]},
{"id":"p2w2","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Doctors cannot yet repair the nerve damage caused by the rare disease, but a new drug appears to ___ its progress: in a two-year trial, patients taking the drug lost no further feeling in their hands, while those given a placebo continued to decline.","o":["gauge","exacerbate","precipitate","arrest"],"a":3,"ex":"Patients on the drug 'lost no further feeling' while the others kept declining, so the drug halts the disease's progress: arrest.","ex_tr":"İlacı kullanan hastalarda his kaybı durdu, plasebo alanlarda ise devam etti; yani ilaç hastalığın ilerlemesini durduruyor: arrest (bir süreci durdurmak).","why":["To gauge is to measure. A drug that only measured the disease would not explain why patients taking it stopped losing feeling.","To exacerbate is to make something worse, but patients on the drug did better than those on the placebo, not worse.","To precipitate is to make something happen sooner or more suddenly. The drug did not speed up the decline; it stopped it.","Correct. To arrest a process is to halt it, and patients on the drug lost no further feeling while the others kept getting worse."]},
{"id":"p2w3","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"For months the city's transit director insisted that the new late-night fare was harmless, but after ridership on the night buses fell by a third, she had to ___ that the fare was driving passengers away, and she cut it in half.","o":["refute","foretell","concede","stipulate"],"a":2,"ex":"'Insisted ... harmless, but' sets up a reversal: the drop in ridership forced her to admit, unwillingly, that the fare was the problem: concede.","ex_tr":"'Insisted ... but' bir geri adımı haber veriyor: yolcu sayısı üçte bir azalınca müdür, ücretin yolcuları kaçırdığını istemeye istemeye kabul etmek zorunda kaldı: concede (istemeyerek kabul etmek).","why":["To refute is to prove a claim false. Denying that the fare drove passengers away clashes with the fall in ridership and with her decision to cut the fare.","To foretell is to predict. Ridership had already fallen by a third, so she was describing something that had happened, not predicting it.","Correct. To concede is to admit something, often unwillingly. After insisting the fare was harmless, she was forced by the falling ridership to admit it was not.","To stipulate is to state a requirement or condition, as a contract does. She was admitting a fact about the fare, not laying down a rule."]},
{"id":"p2w4","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Most physics textbooks simply state that ice floats because it is less dense than liquid water. Zhao Ying's short guide instead sets out to ___ this fact, showing step by step how water molecules lock into an open, roomy pattern as they freeze, so that ice takes up more space than the water it formed from.","o":["condense","postulate","elucidate","mystify"],"a":2,"ex":"'Instead' contrasts simply stating a fact with 'showing step by step how' it works, which is explaining it: elucidate.","ex_tr":"'Instead', bir bilgiyi yalnızca söylemekle onun nasıl gerçekleştiğini 'adım adım' göstermeyi karşılaştırıyor; rehber bu bilgiyi açıklıyor: elucidate (açıklamak, aydınlatmak).","why":["To condense is to shorten. The textbooks give the fact in one line; the guide expands it into a step-by-step account.","To postulate is to propose an idea that has not been proven. The fact is already stated in textbooks; the guide explains why it is true.","Correct. To elucidate is to make clear or explain, which is what showing step by step why ice floats does.","To mystify is to puzzle or confuse. A step-by-step explanation does the opposite."]},
{"id":"p2w5","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Satellite photographs taken over three decades show how the city's new housing developments slowly began to ___ on the wetland to the south, claiming a few hectares each year until only a narrow strip of marsh remained along the river.","o":["dwindle","digress","intercede","encroach"],"a":3,"ex":"'Slowly' and 'claiming a few hectares each year' describe a gradual takeover of the wetland: encroach.","ex_tr":"'Slowly' ve 'her yıl birkaç hektarı ele geçirerek' ifadeleri, sulak alanın yavaş yavaş işgal edildiğini anlatıyor: encroach (bir yerin sınırlarını aşarak orayı yavaş yavaş ele geçirmek).","why":["To dwindle is to shrink gradually. It was the marsh that shrank; the housing developments grew.","To digress is to stray from the main topic in speech or writing, which has nothing to do with buildings spreading into a marsh.","To intercede is to step in on someone's behalf in a dispute. Housing developments do not plead for anyone; they spread over the marsh.","Correct. To encroach is to take over something gradually, as the developments did, a few hectares at a time."]},
{"id":"p2w6","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Sound archivist Daniel Osei spent three years searching flea markets and attic sales before he managed to ___ the only surviving recording of the singer, a scratched disc that collectors had long assumed was lost.","o":["unearth","efface","bequeath","decipher"],"a":0,"ex":"Three years of searching for a disc that collectors 'had long assumed was lost' ended with finding it: unearth.","ex_tr":"Osei, koleksiyoncuların uzun zamandır kayıp sandığı plağı üç yıl aradıktan sonra buldu: unearth (gizli ya da kayıp bir şeyi arayarak bulup ortaya çıkarmak).","why":["Correct. To unearth is to discover something hidden or lost by searching, exactly what three years of hunting for a 'lost' disc achieved.","To efface is to erase, the last thing an archivist who searched for three years would want to do to the only surviving recording.","To bequeath is to hand something down, usually in a will. He was searching for the disc, not giving it away.","To decipher is to decode something hard to read or understand. The text is about a long search for a disc thought to be lost, so the point is finding it, not decoding it."]},
{"id":"p2w7","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"A tiny shrimp found only in a few flooded limestone caves gives off a blue flash every time it is touched, yet each flash is so ___ that for years no one could see how it begins and ends; researchers finally recorded one, frame by frame, with a camera that takes a thousand frames per second.","o":["abiding","sporadic","conspicuous","fleeting"],"a":3,"ex":"Only a camera taking a thousand frames per second could show the flash from start to finish, so each one lasts a tiny fraction of a second: fleeting.","ex_tr":"Işığın başını ve sonunu ancak saniyede bin kare çeken bir kamera gösterebildiğine göre her parlama saniyenin çok küçük bir kısmı kadar sürüyor: fleeting (çok kısa süren, gelip geçici).","why":["Abiding means lasting a long time. A long-lasting flash would be easy to study and would not need such a fast camera.","Sporadic means happening only now and then, but the shrimp flashes every time it is touched. The fast camera solved a problem of duration, not of frequency.","Conspicuous means easily noticed. Being easy to see would not hide how a flash begins and ends; its short length did.","Correct. Fleeting means lasting a very short time, which is why only a camera recording a thousand frames per second could show a flash from start to finish."]},
{"id":"p2w8","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Because so few residents returned the forms, the first survey of the town's reading habits proved ___, and the researchers could not say whether library use had risen or fallen since the previous decade.","o":["inconclusive","irrefutable","exhaustive","seminal"],"a":0,"ex":"With so few forms returned, the researchers 'could not say whether library use had risen or fallen', so the survey reached no definite result: inconclusive.","ex_tr":"Çok az form geri geldiği için araştırmacılar kütüphane kullanımının arttığını mı yoksa azaldığını mı söyleyemedi; anket kesin bir sonuç vermedi: inconclusive (sonuçsuz, kesin bir sonuca ulaşmayan).","why":["Correct. Inconclusive means not leading to a definite result, and the survey could not show whether library use rose or fell.","Irrefutable means impossible to disprove. A survey that cannot tell whether library use rose or fell proves nothing at all.","Exhaustive means thorough, covering everything. So few returned forms make the survey thin, not thorough.","Seminal means original and influential on later work. The survey failed to answer its own question, so nothing suggests it shaped later research."]},
{"id":"p2w9","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Nurses at the clinic argued that their pay was no longer ___ with their duties: since the clinic merged with a nearby hospital, they had taken on the work of two departments, yet their wages had not risen in six years.","o":["incongruous","fraught","laden","commensurate"],"a":3,"ex":"They took on the work of two departments with no raise in six years, so their pay no longer matched the size of their job: commensurate (proportional). Watch the 'no longer': pay 'no longer incongruous' with the duties would mean it now fits them.","ex_tr":"Birleşmeden sonra iki bölümün işini yapan ama altı yıldır zam almayan hemşirelerin maaşı, yaptıkları işle artık orantılı değil: commensurate (orantılı, denk). 'No longer' ifadesine dikkat: 'no longer incongruous' maaşın artık işe uyduğu anlamına gelirdi.","why":["Incongruous means out of keeping with something. Pay 'no longer incongruous' with the duties would mean pay now matches the work, the opposite of the nurses' complaint.","Fraught means filled with problems or tension. The complaint is that pay has not kept up with the work, not that pay is full of problems.","Laden means loaded or weighed down. Pay cannot be loaded with duties; the complaint is about whether pay matches the amount of work.","Correct. Commensurate means proportional: the nurses took on the work of two departments while their wages stayed the same, so pay no longer matched the duties."]},
{"id":"p2w10","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Earlier biographies portrayed the poet either as a selfless mentor or as a jealous rival, but Ngozi Eze's new study offers a more ___ portrait, showing how his generosity toward young writers coexisted with an envy of their success that he never fully overcame.","o":["superficial","idyllic","nuanced","melodramatic"],"a":2,"ex":"'Either ... or' describes simple, one-sided pictures; the new study shows both traits at once, capturing subtle distinctions: nuanced.","ex_tr":"'Either ... or' tek yönlü, basit portreleri anlatıyor; yeni çalışma ise cömertliği ve kıskançlığı bir arada göstererek ince farkları yakalıyor: nuanced (incelikli).","why":["Superficial means shallow. Showing how two opposite traits existed side by side goes deeper than the earlier books, not less deep.","Idyllic means peaceful and picture-perfect. A portrait that includes an envy the poet never overcame is not a perfect one.","Correct. Nuanced means showing subtle distinctions, as a portrait does that keeps both generosity and envy in view instead of choosing one.","Melodramatic means showing exaggerated emotion. The study is set against one-sided books for its balance, not for its drama."]},
{"id":"p2w11","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"The sensor's glass casing, which cracked in cold-chamber tests at forty degrees below zero, would ___ its use on the polar expedition, so the engineers rebuilt it in a steel shell before the team set out.","o":["preclude","stimulate","validate","facilitate"],"a":0,"ex":"Glass that cracked in polar-level cold would make the sensor unusable on the expedition, which is why the engineers rebuilt it: preclude.","ex_tr":"Eksi kırk derecede çatlayan bir cam kılıf, sensörün kutupta kullanılmasını imkânsız kılar; mühendislerin onu çelik bir kabuğun içinde yeniden yapmasının sebebi de bu: preclude (engellemek, imkânsız kılmak).","why":["Correct. To preclude is to make impossible: a casing that cracks in polar cold rules out use near the pole, so the engineers had to rebuild it.","To stimulate is to encourage growth or activity. A weakness in the casing would not encourage anyone to use the sensor.","To validate is to confirm that something is sound. Cracking in the cold shows the sensor was unfit for the trip, the opposite of confirming its use.","To facilitate is to make easier. A casing that cracks in the cold makes polar use harder, which is why it had to be rebuilt."]},
{"id":"p2w12","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Rather than spraying the whole orchard, the growers made ___ use of pesticide, treating only the trees where their traps had caught pests; the approach cut costs by half and left the bees in nearby fields unharmed.","o":["haphazard","profligate","judicious","gratuitous"],"a":2,"ex":"'Rather than spraying the whole orchard', they sprayed only where the traps showed pests, a choice based on sound judgment: judicious.","ex_tr":"Bütün bahçeyi ilaçlamak yerine yalnızca tuzaklarda zararlı yakalanan ağaçları ilaçladılar; bu, sağduyulu bir karar: judicious (akıllıca, sağduyulu).","why":["Haphazard means random, without a plan. The growers chose trees by what their traps caught, which is a deliberate system.","Profligate means recklessly wasteful, the opposite of spraying only the trees that needed it and cutting costs by half.","Correct. Judicious means showing good judgment: they used the traps to decide where spraying was needed and sprayed nowhere else.","Gratuitous means unnecessary. They sprayed only where pests were actually found, so every treatment was needed."]},
{"id":"p2w13","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Students' typing speeds rose quickly during the first six weeks of the course and then began to ___; most had reached about forty words per minute by week six and were still typing at that same speed when the term ended in December.","o":["wane","plateau","oscillate","rebound"],"a":1,"ex":"Speeds rose, then stayed at 'that same speed' until the term ended, so they reached a level and held it: plateau.","ex_tr":"Yazma hızları ilk altı haftada çabucak arttı, sonra dönem sonuna kadar aynı seviyede kaldı: plateau (belli bir düzeye ulaşıp orada sabit kalmak).","why":["To wane is to decrease. The speeds did not fall; they stayed at about forty words per minute.","Correct. To plateau is to reach a level and then stay there, as the speeds did at forty words per minute.","To oscillate is to swing back and forth. The speeds held at one level rather than rising and falling.","To rebound is to recover after a drop. The speeds never dropped; they rose and then held steady."]},
{"id":"p2w14","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Historians have long treated the merchant's account of his travels as ___, since it describes cities he could not have reached in the time he claims and copies whole passages, word for word, from an earlier traveler's book.","o":["dubious","authoritative","idiosyncratic","candid"],"a":0,"ex":"'Since' introduces two reasons for doubt, impossible journeys and copied passages, so historians do not trust the account: dubious.","ex_tr":"'Since' iki şüphe sebebi sıralıyor: imkânsız yolculuklar ve başka bir kitaptan kelimesi kelimesine kopyalanmış bölümler. Tarihçiler bu anlatıma güvenmiyor: dubious (şüpheli, güvenilmez).","why":["Correct. Dubious means not to be trusted, and impossible journeys plus copied passages give historians good reason for doubt.","Authoritative means reliable and respected. Impossible travel times and copied passages are reasons to distrust the account, not to rely on it.","Idiosyncratic means distinctive and peculiar to one person. An account copied word for word from another writer is the opposite of personal, and the 'since' clause lists reasons for doubt.","Candid means honest and frank. Claiming impossible journeys and copying another book are signs the merchant was not honest."]},
{"id":"p2w15","t":2,"sec":"wic","p":"Conservators examining the 600-year-old fresco were relieved to find only ___ flaking, a few small spots where the paint had just begun to lift from the plaster; they secured the spots with a thin adhesive before the damage could spread.","o":["rampant","incipient","pervasive","vestigial"],"a":1,"ex":"The sentence explains the blank itself: 'small spots where the paint had just begun to lift' is damage in its first stage: incipient.","ex_tr":"Cümle, boşluğun anlamını kendisi veriyor: boyanın sıvadan 'yeni yeni kalkmaya başladığı' birkaç küçük nokta, yani başlangıç aşamasındaki bir hasar: incipient (başlangıç hâlindeki, yeni beliren).","why":["Rampant means spreading unchecked. The conservators were relieved because the damage had not yet spread.","Correct. Incipient means just beginning to develop, like paint that 'had just begun to lift'.","Pervasive means present everywhere. The flaking was limited to a few small spots.","Vestigial describes a small leftover of something that was once larger. This flaking was just beginning, not what remained of earlier damage."]},
{"id":"p2r1","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"Linguist Rana Haddad recorded more than two hundred hours of speech in the mountain ___ she has transcribed only a small fraction of it so far.","o":["villages,","villages","villages, but","villages but,"],"a":2,"ex":"Two complete sentences joined by a coordinating conjunction ('but') take a comma before the conjunction.","ex_tr":"İki tam cümle 'but' gibi bir bağlaçla birleşirken bağlaçtan önce virgül konur. Tek başına virgül 'comma splice', hiç işaret koymamak ise 'run-on' hatasıdır.","why":["A comma alone cannot join two complete sentences; this is a comma splice.","With no punctuation and no conjunction, the two complete sentences run together.","Correct. Both halves are complete sentences, and a comma plus 'but' joins them.","The comma belongs before 'but', not after it; a comma after the conjunction cuts it off from the clause it introduces."]},
{"id":"p2r2","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The tasks on the lifeguard certification exam ___ swimming 300 meters without stopping, treading water for two minutes, and lifting a five-kilogram brick from the bottom of the pool.","o":["include","include:","include;","include,"],"a":0,"ex":"No punctuation goes between a verb and its object: 'include' leads straight into the list.","ex_tr":"Fiil ile nesnesi arasına noktalama işareti girmez: 'include'dan sonra liste doğrudan gelir. İki nokta yalnızca tam bir cümleden sonra kullanılır.","why":["Correct. 'Include' is a verb, and its object, the list, follows it directly with no punctuation.","A colon must follow a complete sentence. 'The tasks on the lifeguard certification exam include' is not complete without its list, so the colon cuts the verb off from its object.","A semicolon joins two complete sentences, and neither the words before it nor the list after it is a complete sentence.","A comma never separates a verb from its object; nothing should come between 'include' and the list."]},
{"id":"p2r3","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's old railway ___ reopened last spring as a covered market where farmers sell fruit, cheese, and honey every Saturday.","o":["station, closed to trains since 1985","station closed to trains since 1985,","station; closed to trains since 1985,","station, closed to trains since 1985,"],"a":3,"ex":"A phrase that adds extra information in the middle of a sentence needs a comma on both sides: 'station, closed to trains since 1985, reopened'.","ex_tr":"Cümlenin ortasına giren ek bilgi ('closed to trains since 1985') iki virgül arasına alınır: biri açar, biri kapatır.","why":["The extra information is opened with a comma but never closed, so it runs straight into the verb 'reopened'.","The comma after '1985' closes extra information that was never opened, and it separates the subject from its verb.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'The town's old railway station' is not one.","Correct. 'Closed to trains since 1985' adds extra information about the station, so a pair of commas opens and closes it."]},
{"id":"p2r4","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"In a lecture on desert farming, ___ described how ancient growers collected winter rain in underground cisterns and used it to water their crops through the dry summer.","o":["hydrologist, Nilay Ekinci","hydrologist, Nilay Ekinci,","hydrologist Nilay Ekinci","hydrologist Nilay Ekinci,"],"a":2,"ex":"A title before a name ('hydrologist Nilay Ekinci') takes no comma, and no comma comes between a subject and its verb.","ex_tr":"İsmin önündeki unvan ('hydrologist') isimle virgülsüz yazılır; özne ile fiil ('described') arasına da virgül girmez.","why":["A comma between a title and the name it describes splits one unit in two.","These commas turn the name into extra information, leaving 'hydrologist' alone, with no article, as the subject. The title and the name belong together.","Correct. 'Hydrologist' is a title attached to the name, so no comma separates them, and no comma comes between the subject and 'described'.","A single comma between the subject and its verb, 'described', is never correct."]},
{"id":"p2r5","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The sound of the waves breaking on the rocks below the old lighthouse ___ the keepers fall asleep on calm summer nights.","o":["help","were helping","have helped","helps"],"a":3,"ex":"Skip the phrase 'of the waves breaking on the rocks below the old lighthouse': the subject is 'sound', singular, so 'helps'.","ex_tr":"Araya giren öbeği ('of the waves ... lighthouse') atla: özne 'sound', tekil: helps.","why":["'Help' is plural. It seems to agree with 'waves' or 'rocks', but those nouns sit inside the phrase that describes the subject.","'Were' is plural, so it cannot agree with 'sound'; the sentence also describes a regular habit, not one moment in the past.","'Have helped' is plural, so it cannot agree with the singular subject 'sound'.","Correct. The subject is 'sound', which is singular, so the verb is 'helps'."]},
{"id":"p2r6","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The oldest bridge in the city, unlike the steel spans built across the river in the 1900s, still rests on ___ original oak pilings.","o":["their","it's","its","they're"],"a":2,"ex":"The pilings belong to the bridge, not to the nearby 'spans': singular possessive, 'its'.","ex_tr":"Kazıklar köprüye ait; araya giren çoğul 'spans' kelimesine aldanma. 'Bridge' tekil olduğu için: its.","why":["'Their' is plural; it would point to 'spans', but the pilings belong to the bridge, the subject of the sentence.","'It's' means 'it is', which makes no sense before 'original oak pilings'.","Correct. The pronoun refers to 'bridge', which is singular, and 'its' is the singular possessive.","'They're' means 'they are'; it is plural and does not show possession."]},
{"id":"p2r7","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"In 1928, a farmer who was digging a well near the village ___ a clay jar filled with copper coins, which now sit in a glass case at the regional museum.","o":["has found","found","had been finding","finds"],"a":1,"ex":"A specific past date ('In 1928') and one finished action call for the simple past: 'found'.","ex_tr":"Belirli bir geçmiş tarih ('In 1928') ve bitmiş tek bir eylem var: simple past, found. Present perfect belirli bir geçmiş tarihle kullanılmaz.","why":["The present perfect links the past to now and cannot be used with a specific past date such as 1928.","Correct. 'In 1928' fixes a single, finished event in the past, which takes the simple past.","The past perfect progressive describes an ongoing action before another past event; a single discovery in 1928 needs the simple past.","The present tense clashes with 'In 1928' and with the past 'was digging' in the same sentence."]},
{"id":"p2r8","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The Antarctic krill is only about as long as a little finger, yet ___ one of the most important animals in the Southern Ocean: whales, seals, and penguins all depend on it.","o":["it's","its","its'","it"],"a":0,"ex":"The clause after 'yet' needs a subject and a verb, 'it is', shortened to 'it's'. 'Its' without an apostrophe only shows possession.","ex_tr":"'Yet'ten sonraki cümlede özne ve fiil gerekiyor: 'it is', kısaca it's. Kesme işaretsiz 'its' yalnızca iyelik bildirir ('onun').","why":["Correct. 'It's' is short for 'it is', giving the clause after 'yet' both a subject and a verb.","'Its' is possessive, as in 'its shell'; the clause after 'yet' needs a verb, which only 'it is' provides.","The form its' (an apostrophe after the s) does not exist in English.","Without 'is', the clause after 'yet' has no verb: 'it one of the most important animals' is not a sentence."]},
{"id":"p2r9","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"While sorting through seeds stored in an abbey cellar, ___","o":["a variety of barley that had not been grown for three centuries was found by the botanist.","the botanist's find was a variety of barley that had not been grown for three centuries.","three centuries had passed since anyone grew the barley that the botanist found.","the botanist found a variety of barley that had not been grown for three centuries."],"a":3,"ex":"An opening '-ing' phrase describes whoever comes right after the comma. Only the botanist can sort seeds, so 'the botanist' must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki 'While sorting ...' ifadesi virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi anlatır. Tohumları ayıklayan botanikçi olduğu için 'the botanist' virgülden hemen sonra gelmeli.","why":["This puts 'a variety of barley' right after the comma, so the barley seems to be sorting the seeds.","The subject here is 'find', not the botanist; a find cannot sort seeds, and the possessive 'botanist's' does not fix that.","This makes 'three centuries' the thing sorting through the seeds.","Correct. The botanist is the one sorting the seeds, and 'the botanist' comes right after the comma."]},
{"id":"p2r10","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The paper mill that opened on the riverbank in 1852 ___ more than three hundred workers at its peak, most of them from farming families in the surrounding hills.","o":["employed","which employed","having employed","employing"],"a":0,"ex":"'That opened on the riverbank in 1852' only describes the subject; the sentence still needs one main verb: 'employed'.","ex_tr":"'That opened on the riverbank in 1852' yalnızca özneyi niteleyen bir yan cümlecik; cümlenin hâlâ bir ana fiile ihtiyacı var: employed.","why":["Correct. 'That opened ... in 1852' describes the mill, and 'employed' is the main verb the sentence needs.","'Which' starts a second describing clause, so the sentence still has no main verb.","'Having employed' cannot serve as a main verb either, so the sentence remains a fragment.","An '-ing' form alone cannot be a main verb, so the sentence is left as a fragment."]},
{"id":"p2r11","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The organizers of the weavers' festival hope both to celebrate the region's long tradition of carpet making and ___ young people to learn the craft before the last master weavers retire.","o":["they encourage","encouraging","to encourage","the encouragement of"],"a":2,"ex":"'Both ... and' needs matching halves: 'to celebrate' ... 'to encourage'.","ex_tr":"'Both ... and' yapısında iki taraf aynı biçimde olmalı: to celebrate ... to encourage.","why":["A full clause, 'they encourage', does not match the 'to' verb after 'both'.","'Encouraging' does not match 'to celebrate'; the two halves of 'both ... and' must have the same form.","Correct. 'Both to celebrate ... and to encourage' pairs two matching 'to' verbs.","A noun phrase does not match 'to celebrate', and 'hope ... the encouragement of young people to learn' is not grammatical."]},
{"id":"p2r12","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"In an interview, potter Selin Yildiz described the glaze on her best-known bowl ___ a color she spent eleven years trying to reproduce.","o":["as \"the blue of a cold morning\"","as, \"the blue of a cold morning,\"","as: \"the blue of a cold morning,\"","as \"the blue of a cold morning,\""],"a":3,"ex":"The quotation is part of the sentence after 'as', so no mark comes before it; a comma after it sets off the description that follows.","ex_tr":"Alıntı, 'as' kelimesinden sonra cümlenin bir parçası olarak geliyor; bu yüzden önüne işaret konmaz. Alıntıdan sonra gelen ek açıklama ('a color ...') virgülle ayrılır; virgül tırnağın içine yazılır.","why":["Without a comma after the quotation, it runs straight into 'a color she spent eleven years trying to reproduce', which is extra information and must be set off.","A comma between 'as' and the words it introduces splits the phrase; a quotation that is part of the sentence takes no comma before it.","A colon must follow a complete sentence, and 'described the glaze on her best-known bowl as' is not complete.","Correct. The quotation blends into the sentence after 'as', so nothing comes before it, and the comma inside the closing quotation mark sets off the description that follows."]},
{"id":"p2r13","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"Street trees shade the pavement beneath them, lowering its surface temperature on summer afternoons by several degrees. ___ their roots and leaves hold back rainwater that would otherwise rush into storm drains during heavy downpours.","o":["However,","In addition,","For example,","Therefore,"],"a":1,"ex":"The second sentence adds a separate benefit of street trees to the first: 'In addition'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle, sokak ağaçlarının ilkine ek olarak başka bir faydasını sayıyor: In addition.","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but both sentences describe benefits of street trees.","Correct. Holding back rainwater is a second, separate benefit, added to the cooling described in the first sentence.","'For example' would make holding back rainwater an example of cooling the pavement, but it is a different benefit.","'Therefore' would mean that shading the pavement causes the trees to hold back rainwater, which the text does not claim."]},
{"id":"p2r14","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"The first glider built by the students flew for only forty seconds before one of its wings snapped. ___ the team rebuilt the wing from thin layers of bamboo, and the second glider stayed in the air for nearly six minutes.","o":["Subsequently,","Previously,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,"],"a":0,"ex":"First the wing snapped; after that, the team rebuilt it: 'Subsequently' (afterward).","ex_tr":"Önce kanat kırıldı, ardından ekip onu yeniden yaptı; olaylar zaman sırasıyla ilerliyor: Subsequently (daha sonra).","why":["Correct. The team rebuilt the wing after it snapped, so the second sentence comes later in time.","'Previously' would place the rebuild before the first flight, but a wing has to snap before it can be rebuilt.","'Meanwhile' means at the same time, but the rebuild came after the forty-second flight, not during it.","'Similarly' introduces a matching point, but rebuilding the wing is the next step in a sequence, not a similar event."]},
{"id":"p2r15","t":2,"sec":"wr","p":"Desert tortoises can go a very long time without drinking. ___ some survive an entire year on the water stored in their bodies and in the plants they eat.","o":["However,","In fact,","Instead,","Otherwise,"],"a":1,"ex":"The second sentence strengthens the first with a striking detail, a whole year without drinking: 'In fact'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle, ilk cümleyi daha çarpıcı bir bilgiyle pekiştiriyor (tam bir yıl susuz kalmak): In fact.","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but surviving a year without drinking supports the first sentence rather than opposing it.","Correct. 'In fact' introduces a stronger, more striking version of the first claim: not just a long time, but a whole year.","'Instead' introduces a replacement for something rejected, and the first sentence is not rejected.","'Otherwise' means 'if that were not so', but the second sentence builds on the first rather than imagining the opposite."]},
/* Practice 3 */
{"id":"p3w1","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"An old museum label claimed that the bronze mirror had belonged to a queen. Curators have now removed that statement because they could not ___ it; no inscription, burial record, or written source connects the mirror to any royal owner.","o":["refute","substantiate","embellish","excavate"],"a":1,"ex":"The curators removed the claim because no inscription or record connects the mirror to a royal owner, so they had no facts to support it: substantiate.","ex_tr":"Küratörler ifadeyi kaldırdı, çünkü aynayı bir kraliçeye bağlayan hiçbir yazıt ya da kayıt yok; iddiayı kanıtlayamadılar: substantiate (kanıtlamak, kanıtla desteklemek).","why":["Refute means to prove false. Curators drop a claim they cannot support, not one they cannot disprove, and missing records show a lack of proof, not proof that the claim is false.","Correct. Substantiate means to support a claim with facts; with no inscription or record linking the mirror to a queen, the curators had nothing to support the label.","Embellish means to add decorative or exaggerated detail. Being unable to add detail to a statement is no reason to remove it.","Excavate means to dig up buried objects. The mirror is already in the museum, and a statement on a label cannot be dug up."]},
{"id":"p3w2","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"The sculptor Hana Sato carves her figures from river ice and displays them outdoors, where they melt within a few hours. She says the ___ nature of the pieces is the point, since a visitor who arrives the next morning will find only a puddle.","o":["enduring","ephemeral","tactile","symmetric"],"a":1,"ex":"Figures that melt within hours and leave only a puddle by the next morning last a very short time: ephemeral.","ex_tr":"Birkaç saat içinde eriyen ve ertesi sabah geriye yalnızca bir su birikintisi bırakan heykeller çok kısa ömürlüdür: ephemeral (geçici, kısa ömürlü).","why":["Enduring means lasting a long time, the opposite of figures that melt within a few hours.","Correct. Ephemeral means lasting a very short time, like ice figures that are gone by the next morning.","Tactile means related to touch. The text never mentions touching the figures; its point is how quickly they disappear.","Symmetric means balanced on both sides. Nothing in the text describes the shape of the figures, and symmetry would not explain the puddle."]},
{"id":"p3w3","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Free online courses often enroll thousands of students, but ___ is severe: of the 12,000 people who signed up for one introductory statistics course, fewer than 900 were still submitting work in the final week.","o":["influx","scrutiny","attrition","stigma"],"a":2,"ex":"'But' turns from large enrollments to the numbers after the colon: most students drop out as the weeks pass, a gradual loss of participants: attrition.","ex_tr":"'But' yüksek kayıt sayısından iki noktadan sonraki rakamlara geçiyor: 12.000 öğrenciden son haftaya 900'den azı kalmış. Katılımcıların zamanla azalması: attrition (zamanla azalma, kayıp).","why":["Influx means a sudden arrival of large numbers. The big enrollment is only the starting point; the colon describes students leaving, not arriving.","Scrutiny means close, critical examination. Nobody in the text is examining the course; the numbers show students dropping out.","Correct. Attrition means a gradual reduction through departures: 12,000 students shrank to fewer than 900 by the final week.","Stigma means strong social disapproval. The text says nothing about disapproval; it reports how many students left."]},
{"id":"p3w4","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"After a childhood infection clears, the virus that causes chickenpox can remain ___ in nerve cells for decades, producing no symptoms at all until age or stress allows it to become active again as shingles.","o":["latent","rampant","virulent","conspicuous"],"a":0,"ex":"The virus stays in the body for decades without symptoms and becomes active only under certain conditions (age or stress): latent.","ex_tr":"Virüs onlarca yıl hiçbir belirti vermeden sinir hücrelerinde kalıyor ve ancak yaş ya da stres gibi koşullarda yeniden etkinleşiyor: latent (gizli, uykuda).","why":["Correct. Latent means present but needing particular conditions to become active, exactly as the virus waits until age or stress reactivates it.","Rampant means spreading unchecked. The virus stays quietly in nerve cells and becomes active only later, under particular conditions.","Virulent means extremely severe or harmful. A virus that produces no symptoms for decades is doing no visible harm during that time.","Conspicuous means easily noticed. A virus that produces no symptoms at all is hidden, not noticeable."]},
{"id":"p3w5","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"In her letters, the novelist Aylin Demir was witty and talkative, sometimes filling ten pages to describe a single dinner party. In person, however, she was famously ___, often answering an interviewer's long question with a nod or a single word.","o":["loquacious","affable","taciturn","erudite"],"a":2,"ex":"'However' contrasts the talkative letters with her manner in person, and answering with a nod or a single word shows she said very little: taciturn.","ex_tr":"'However' mektuplarındaki konuşkanlığı yüz yüze davranışıyla karşılaştırıyor; uzun sorulara baş sallayarak ya da tek kelimeyle cevap vermek çok az konuştuğunu gösterir: taciturn (az konuşan, suskun).","why":["Loquacious means talkative. That describes her letters, but 'however' signals that in person she was different.","Affable means friendly and easy to talk to. Answering long questions with a single word makes her hard to talk to.","Correct. Taciturn means habitually quiet and uncommunicative, like answering long questions with a nod or one word.","Erudite means deeply learned. A nod or a single word reveals nothing about her knowledge, and 'however' needs a contrast with being talkative."]},
{"id":"p3w6","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Because the Salt Fair and the Blessing of the Boats fall in the same week in the fishing town of Selvira, visitors often ___ the two, describing them as a single celebration. Local historians point out that the events began two centuries apart and honor entirely different traditions.","o":["juxtapose","conflate","disparage","scrutinize"],"a":1,"ex":"Visitors describe two separate events as one, and historians correct them, so the visitors wrongly treat two things as the same: conflate.","ex_tr":"Ziyaretçiler iki ayrı etkinliği tek bir kutlama sanıyor; tarihçiler ise ikisinin farklı olduğunu söylüyor. İki farklı şeyi yanlışlıkla aynı saymak: conflate.","why":["Juxtapose means to place things side by side for contrast. Visitors who call the events one celebration are blurring them together, not contrasting them.","Correct. Conflate means to wrongly treat two things as the same, as visitors do when they call two separate festivals one celebration.","Disparage means to belittle. The visitors' mistake is about telling the events apart, not about speaking badly of them.","Scrutinize means to examine closely. Careful examination would reveal the differences the historians point out, not hide them."]},
{"id":"p3w7","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"In the mountain village of Belcoran, farmers treat the return of the swallows as a ___ of spring, since the birds usually appear about a week before the snow melts and the planting season begins.","o":["wake","relic","harbinger","paradox"],"a":2,"ex":"The swallows arrive about a week before the snow melts, so their return is a sign that spring is coming: harbinger.","ex_tr":"Kırlangıçlar karlar erimeden yaklaşık bir hafta önce geliyor; dönüşleri baharın yaklaştığının işaretidir: harbinger (haberci, müjdeci).","why":["Wake means the period following an event. The birds come before the snow melts, not after spring has arrived.","A relic is an object surviving from an earlier time. The swallows point forward to the coming season, not back to the past.","Correct. A harbinger is a sign of something approaching, as birds that arrive a week before the thaw announce spring.","A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself. Birds arriving just before spring is a reliable pattern, not a contradiction."]},
{"id":"p3w8","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Whereas her first album drew only on Chinese folk songs, the composer Wen Li's new record is strikingly ___, blending Turkish folk rhythms, Brazilian guitar, and electronic sound with a traditional Chinese lute melody.","o":["homogeneous","rudimentary","esoteric","eclectic"],"a":3,"ex":"'Whereas' sets the first album, which drew on a single tradition, against the new record, which mixes styles from several traditions: eclectic.","ex_tr":"'Whereas' tek bir kaynaktan beslenen ilk albümü, farklı geleneklerden tarzları harmanlayan yeni albümle karşılaştırıyor: eclectic (farklı kaynaklardan derlenmiş).","why":["Homogeneous means all of the same kind. That fits the first album, but 'whereas' sets the new record against it.","Rudimentary means basic. Combining four distinct traditions in one record is the opposite of basic.","Esoteric means understood only by a few specialists. The text is about how many styles are mixed, not about how hard the music is to understand.","Correct. Eclectic means drawn from varied sources or styles, like a record blending Turkish, Brazilian, electronic and Chinese music."]},
{"id":"p3w9","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Planners in one small town expected chaos when they removed every traffic light from its busiest crossing, so the result seemed ___ to them: accidents fell by half, apparently because drivers, no longer told when to go, slowed down and watched one another more carefully.","o":["counterintuitive","inconsequential","intuitive","redundant"],"a":0,"ex":"The planners expected chaos, but accidents fell by half, the opposite of what they assumed would happen: counterintuitive.","ex_tr":"Planlamacılar kaos bekliyordu, ama kazalar yarı yarıya azaldı; sonuç beklentilerine ters düştü: counterintuitive (sezgiye aykırı).","why":["Correct. Counterintuitive means contrary to what intuition would expect: the planners predicted chaos and got fewer accidents.","Inconsequential means unimportant. Cutting accidents in half is a major result.","Intuitive means easily understood without reasoning. The planners expected chaos, so a drop in accidents went against their instincts.","Redundant means not needed or more than necessary. The result was surprising news to the planners, not something unneeded."]},
{"id":"p3w10","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the volcano above the town of Ardenza has not erupted in eighty years, scientists at its observatory remain ___, measuring ground swelling and gas levels every day because past eruptions there began with only a few days of warning.","o":["complacent","vigilant","dormant","nostalgic"],"a":1,"ex":"'Although' sets eighty quiet years against daily measurements made because eruptions come with little warning, so the scientists keep careful watch: vigilant.","ex_tr":"'Although' seksen yıllık sessizlikle her gün yapılan ölçümler arasında karşıtlık kuruyor; geçmişteki püskürmeler yalnızca birkaç gün önceden belirti verdiği için bilim insanları sürekli tetikte: vigilant (uyanık, tetikte).","why":["Complacent means unconcerned because overly content. Eighty quiet years might invite that, but 'although' and the daily measurements show the scientists stay alert.","Correct. Vigilant means keeping careful watch for possible danger, as measuring swelling and gas every day does.","Dormant means temporarily inactive. It could describe the quiet volcano, but scientists who take measurements every day are far from inactive.","Nostalgic means longing for the past. The scientists are watching for a future eruption, not remembering old times."]},
{"id":"p3w11","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Interest on an unpaid loan continues to ___ month after month, which is why a modest debt ignored for several years can end up far larger than the amount originally borrowed.","o":["dwindle","plateau","oscillate","accrue"],"a":3,"ex":"A modest debt growing far larger than the amount borrowed shows that the interest keeps building up over time: accrue.","ex_tr":"Küçük bir borcun birkaç yılda borç alınan paradan çok daha büyük hâle gelebilmesi, faizin her ay birikerek arttığını gösterir: accrue (birikmek, zamanla artmak).","why":["Dwindle means to gradually shrink. A debt that grows far larger than the amount borrowed shows the interest increasing, not shrinking.","Plateau means to reach a level and stay there. A debt that keeps getting larger has not leveled off.","Oscillate means to swing back and forth. The text describes steady growth in one direction, not rising and falling.","Correct. Accrue means to accumulate over time, which is how interest makes a modest debt grow."]},
{"id":"p3w12","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the mountaineering team led by Ravi Menon never reached the summit, historians describe its decision to turn back as ___, because the climbers gave up their attempt in order to carry an injured porter safely down the mountain.","o":["ignominious","frivolous","arbitrary","laudable"],"a":3,"ex":"The team failed to reach the summit, but it turned back to save an injured porter, an act that deserves praise despite the failure: laudable.","ex_tr":"Ekip zirveye ulaşamadı ama yaralı bir hamalı güvenle aşağı taşımak için geri döndü; başarısız olsa da övgüyü hak eden bir davranış: laudable (övgüye değer).","why":["Ignominious means shameful. Failing to reach the summit might seem embarrassing, but turning back to save an injured porter is honorable, not disgraceful.","Frivolous means silly and not serious. Carrying an injured porter down a mountain is a serious, responsible act.","Arbitrary means based on chance rather than reason. The team had a clear reason to turn back, an injured porter who needed help.","Correct. Laudable describes behavior that deserves praise even without success, like giving up a climb to save an injured porter."]},
{"id":"p3w13","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"For three centuries the river port of Halveth was the busiest market in the region, but its importance began to ___ after 1870, when a new railway bypassed the town and merchants moved their trade to stations along the line.","o":["wane","rebound","prevail","culminate"],"a":0,"ex":"'But' turns from the port's long success to a railway that drew the merchants away, so the port's importance decreased: wane.","ex_tr":"'But' limanın üç yüzyıllık başarısından tüccarları başka yere çeken demiryoluna geçiyor; limanın önemi azalmaya başladı: wane (azalmak, zayıflamak).","why":["Correct. Wane means to decrease in vigor or extent, as the port's importance did once merchants left for the railway.","Rebound means to recover after a decline. The port had been at its peak, and the railway started a decline rather than ending one.","Prevail means to gain control or influence. Losing its merchants to the railway reduced the port's influence.","Culminate means to reach a final high point after developing. The railway marked the start of a decline, not the peak of growth."]},
{"id":"p3w14","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Residents of the hill village had rejected two earlier plans for a wind farm, objecting to the noise, but they proved ___ to a third design that placed the turbines farther from their homes, approving it by a wide margin.","o":["averse","impervious","indifferent","amenable"],"a":3,"ex":"'But' contrasts the two rejected plans with the third, which residents approved by a wide margin, so they were open to it: amenable.","ex_tr":"'But' reddedilen iki planı, köylülerin büyük bir çoğunlukla onayladığı üçüncü tasarımla karşılaştırıyor; köylüler yeni öneriye açıktı: amenable (öneriye açık, uzlaşmaya yatkın).","why":["Averse means strongly opposed. That describes their response to the first two plans, but they approved the third by a wide margin.","Impervious means not letting liquid through, or, said of people, unaffected. The residents were won over by the new design, not unaffected by it.","Indifferent means uninterested. People who vote for a plan by a wide margin clearly care about the outcome.","Correct. Amenable means open and responsive to suggestion, as residents were when they approved the revised design."]},
{"id":"p3w15","t":3,"sec":"wic","p":"Rather than listing every measurement from the three-year study, the summary for policymakers presents only its most ___ findings, such as the sharp rise in river temperatures, so that busy readers can grasp the main points in a few minutes.","o":["extraneous","tentative","salient","obscure"],"a":2,"ex":"The summary skips most measurements and keeps only the findings that let readers grasp 'the main points' quickly, the most important ones: salient.","ex_tr":"Özet her ölçümü sıralamak yerine okurların ana noktaları birkaç dakikada kavramasını sağlayacak bulguları seçiyor, yani en önemlilerini: salient (en göze çarpan, en önemli).","why":["Extraneous means irrelevant. A summary meant to convey the main points would leave out irrelevant findings, not keep only those.","Tentative means uncertain. The summary picks findings by importance, and the sharp rise in temperatures is presented as a clear result.","Correct. Salient means most noticeable or important, the findings a summary keeps so that readers grasp the main points.","Obscure means unclear or hard to understand. Busy readers need findings they can grasp in a few minutes, not puzzling ones."]},
{"id":"p3r1","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"The original timber deck of the Kessler Bridge rotted within twenty ___ the engineers who rebuilt the bridge in 1932 chose steel-reinforced concrete instead.","o":["years; therefore,","years, therefore,","years therefore,","years therefore"],"a":0,"ex":"Two complete sentences linked by 'therefore' need a semicolon before it and a comma after it; 'therefore' is not a conjunction, so a comma alone cannot join them.","ex_tr":"'Therefore' bağlaç değildir. İki tam cümleyi bağlarken önüne noktalı virgül, arkasına virgül konur: '; therefore,'.","why":["Correct. Both halves are complete sentences; the semicolon joins them, and 'therefore' is followed by a comma.","A comma before 'therefore' joins two complete sentences with only a comma, a comma splice; 'therefore' is not a joining word.","With no punctuation before 'therefore', the two complete sentences run together.","With no punctuation before or after 'therefore', the two complete sentences run together."]},
{"id":"p3r2","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Archaeologists believe the ancient port of Kelmara was abandoned for a simple ___ silt carried down by the river had made the harbor too shallow for loaded ships.","o":["reason:","reason,","reason","reason; because"],"a":0,"ex":"The first part is a complete sentence, and the second clause explains it by saying what the simple reason was, so a colon introduces it.","ex_tr":"İlk kısım tam bir cümle; ikinci cümle ise 'a simple reason' ifadesindeki sebebin ne olduğunu açıklıyor. Böyle bir açıklamadan önce iki nokta (:) konur.","why":["Correct. A colon after a complete sentence can introduce a second clause that explains it, here by stating the simple reason.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together.","A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, and 'because silt ... had made the harbor too shallow' is a dependent clause."]},
{"id":"p3r3","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Lake ___ holds roughly a fifth of the world's unfrozen surface fresh water, more than the five Great Lakes of North America hold combined.","o":["Baikal the deepest lake on Earth,","Baikal, the deepest lake on Earth","Baikal, the deepest lake on Earth,","Baikal — the deepest lake on Earth,"],"a":2,"ex":"'The deepest lake on Earth' renames Lake Baikal and adds extra information, so it needs a comma before it and a comma after it.","ex_tr":"'the deepest lake on Earth' Baikal Gölü hakkında ek bilgidir (appositive); iki virgül arasına alınır.","why":["The appositive is closed with a comma but never opened.","The appositive is opened with a comma but never closed, so it runs straight into the verb 'holds'.","Correct. The appositive 'the deepest lake on Earth' is extra information, opened and closed by a pair of commas.","An interruption opened with a dash must be closed with a dash; a dash and a comma do not make a pair."]},
{"id":"p3r4","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"The tall sandstone towers that wind and sand have carved along the eastern edge of the Kaya ___ attract thousands of climbers every spring.","o":["Plateau:","Plateau,","Plateau;","Plateau"],"a":3,"ex":"Everything from 'The tall sandstone towers' to 'Kaya Plateau' is the subject of 'attract', and no punctuation goes between a subject and its verb.","ex_tr":"'The tall sandstone towers ... Kaya Plateau' öznenin tamamı; özne ile fiil ('attract') arasına noktalama işareti konmaz.","why":["A colon must follow a complete sentence, but the words before it are only a subject with no main verb, and the colon splits it from 'attract'.","A single comma cuts the subject off from its verb, 'attract'; the 'that' clause is essential and is not set off.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, but the words before it are only a subject with no main verb ('have carved' belongs to the 'that' clause).","Correct. The subject runs from 'The tall sandstone towers' to 'Plateau', and nothing should separate it from its verb, 'attract'."]},
{"id":"p3r5","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Each spring, the panel of judges for the regional science fair ___ one project for a national prize, and its decision is posted online the same evening.","o":["selects","select","are selecting","have selected"],"a":0,"ex":"The subject is 'panel', a collective noun acting as one body (note 'its decision'), so the verb is singular: selects.","ex_tr":"Özne 'panel' (tek bir kurul); 'of judges' sadece onu niteliyor. Cümledeki 'its decision' ifadesi de tekilliği gösteriyor: selects.","why":["Correct. The subject is 'panel', one body acting together, so it takes the singular verb 'selects', matching 'its decision'.","'Select' is plural; it agrees with 'judges', which sits inside the phrase 'of judges' and is not the subject.","'Are selecting' is plural, but the panel is one body, as 'its decision' shows.","'Have selected' is plural, and it also describes a finished action rather than what happens each spring."]},
{"id":"p3r6","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"My classmate Zehra had volunteered at the city archive before, so when a box of old maps arrived, the head archivist invited ___ to catalog them over the summer.","o":["she and I","her and me","her and I","she and me"],"a":1,"ex":"Both people are objects of 'invited', so both pronouns take the object case: her and me. Test each alone: 'invited her', 'invited me'.","ex_tr":"İki kişi de 'invited' fiilinin nesnesi; iki zamir de nesne hâlinde olmalı: her and me ('invited her', 'invited me').","why":["'She' and 'I' are subject pronouns; no one would write 'the archivist invited she' or 'invited I'.","Correct. Both pronouns are objects of 'invited', so both are in the object case: 'invited her' and 'invited me'.","'Her' is right, but 'I' is a subject pronoun; no one would write 'the archivist invited I'.","'Me' is right, but 'she' is a subject pronoun; no one would write 'the archivist invited she'."]},
{"id":"p3r7","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"When a honeybee finds a rich patch of flowers, it returns to the hive and performs a waggle dance for its nestmates. The angle of the dance ___ the direction of the flowers relative to the sun, and the length of its straight run signals how far away they are.","o":["indicates","indicated","had indicated","will have indicated"],"a":0,"ex":"The passage describes what bees always do, a general truth, and the other verbs ('finds', 'returns', 'signals') are in the present tense, so: indicates.","ex_tr":"Metin arıların her zaman yaptığı bir şeyi, yani genel bir gerçeği anlatıyor; diğer fiiller de geniş zamanda ('finds', 'returns', 'signals'): indicates.","why":["Correct. A general truth about how bees behave takes the present tense, matching 'finds', 'returns' and 'signals'.","The past tense would describe one finished event, but the passage describes what always happens, and 'signals' in the same sentence is present.","The past perfect places an action before another past event, but the passage states a general truth in the present, like 'signals'.","The future perfect describes something completed by a future time, which does not fit a description of how bees always behave."]},
{"id":"p3r8","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"The award went to Tomas Ruiz, a young engineer ___ low-cost water filter is now used in more than two hundred villages.","o":["who's","whose","who","whom"],"a":1,"ex":"The filter belongs to the engineer, so the possessive 'whose' is needed; 'who's' means 'who is'.","ex_tr":"Filtre mühendise ait, bu yüzden iyelik bildiren 'whose' gerekir. 'Who's' = 'who is'.","why":["'Who's' is a contraction of 'who is'; 'a young engineer who is low-cost water filter' makes no sense.","Correct. 'Whose' shows possession: the water filter belongs to the engineer.","'Who' cannot show possession, so nothing links the engineer to the filter.","'Whom' is an object pronoun and cannot show that the filter belongs to the engineer."]},
{"id":"p3r9","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Wood ash, once thrown away by village bakeries, can produce glossy green and brown glazes for pottery. A potter and glaze chemist, ___","o":["glazes made from the ash of local trees were tested by Leyla Arslan for a decade.","a decade was spent by Leyla Arslan testing glazes made from the ash of local trees.","Leyla Arslan's decade was spent testing glazes made from the ash of local trees.","Leyla Arslan spent a decade testing glazes made from the ash of local trees."],"a":3,"ex":"'A potter and glaze chemist' describes a person, so that person must come right after the comma: Leyla Arslan.","ex_tr":"'A potter and glaze chemist' bir kişiyi tanımlıyor; virgülden hemen sonra o kişi gelmeli: Leyla Arslan.","why":["Dangling modifier: this puts 'glazes' right after the comma, making the glazes the potter and glaze chemist.","Dangling modifier: the opening phrase describes whatever follows the comma, so this makes 'a decade' the potter and glaze chemist.","Dangling modifier: the subject is 'decade' ('Leyla Arslan's' only modifies it), so the opening phrase describes a decade.","Correct. The opening phrase describes Leyla Arslan, and her name comes right after the comma as the subject, so the modifier is placed correctly."]},
{"id":"p3r10","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Caravanserais, roadside inns built along trade routes roughly a day's journey apart, ___ merchants and their camels a safe place to rest for the night.","o":["having offered","offering","to offer","offered"],"a":3,"ex":"The subject 'Caravanserais' needs a main verb; the phrase between the commas only describes it. 'Offered' is the only finite verb.","ex_tr":"Öznesi 'Caravanserais' olan cümlenin çekimli bir ana fiile ihtiyacı var; virgüller arasındaki kısım yalnızca ek bilgi. Çekimli tek fiil: offered.","why":["'Having offered' is a participle phrase, not a main verb, so the sentence still has no finite verb.","'Offering' is an -ing form that cannot be a main verb by itself, so the sentence becomes a fragment.","'To offer' is an infinitive, which cannot serve as the main verb, so the sentence is left incomplete.","Correct. 'Offered' is a finite verb, giving the subject 'Caravanserais' the main verb the sentence needs."]},
{"id":"p3r11","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"To prepare the old violin for its first concert in decades, the restorer reglued its cracked top, replaced two worn pegs, and ___","o":["a new bridge was fitted.","fitted a new bridge.","fitting a new bridge.","to fit a new bridge."],"a":1,"ex":"The list of what the restorer did must stay parallel: reglued ..., replaced ..., and fitted ....","ex_tr":"Restoratörün yaptıkları aynı yapıda sıralanmalı: reglued ..., replaced ..., and fitted ....","why":["A passive clause with its own subject, 'a new bridge was fitted', breaks the pattern of past-tense verbs sharing the subject 'the restorer'.","Correct. 'Fitted' is a past-tense verb like 'reglued' and 'replaced', so all three items match.","'Fitting' is an -ing form and does not match 'reglued' and 'replaced'.","'To fit' is an infinitive and does not match 'reglued' and 'replaced'."]},
{"id":"p3r12","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"For six straight days, heavy rain fell across the Tervan ___ causing the river to rise above its banks in three towns.","o":["Valley, and","Valley","Valley;","Valley,"],"a":3,"ex":"'Causing the river to rise ...' is a supplementary phrase that adds the result of the rain, so a comma sets it off from the main clause.","ex_tr":"'causing the river to rise ...' yağmurun sonucunu anlatan ek bir ifadedir; ana cümleden virgülle ayrılır.","why":["'And' would join 'fell' to 'causing', two verbs in different forms; joining them would need a matching verb such as 'caused'.","Without a comma, 'causing' seems to attach to 'Valley', as if the valley made the river rise; the closing supplement must be set off.","A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, and 'causing the river to rise above its banks' is only a phrase.","Correct. A comma sets off the closing '-ing' phrase, which adds the result of the whole main clause."]},
{"id":"p3r13","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Many plants in dry regions store water in unusual ways. ___ the baobab tree can hold thousands of liters of water in its swollen trunk and draw on that supply during the long dry season.","o":["However,","For example,","Therefore,","In contrast,"],"a":1,"ex":"The baobab is one case of a plant storing water in an unusual way, so the transition introduces an example: 'For example'.","ex_tr":"Baobab, suyu alışılmadık bir yolla depolayan bitkilere bir örnektir: For example.","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but the second sentence supports the first instead of opposing it.","Correct. The baobab, which stores water in its trunk, is a specific example of a dry-region plant storing water in an unusual way.","'Therefore' signals a result, but the baobab's trunk is an instance of the first claim, not a consequence of it.","'In contrast' signals a difference, but the baobab does exactly what the first sentence describes."]},
{"id":"p3r14","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's old cinema has stiff wooden seats, a leaking roof, and only one screen. ___ residents voted to keep it open because generations of local families have watched their first films there.","o":["For example,","As a result,","Even so,","Similarly,"],"a":2,"ex":"The cinema's faults would be reasons to close it, yet residents kept it open; the transition must accept those faults and then go against them: 'Even so'.","ex_tr":"Sinemanın kusurları onu kapatmak için birer sebep, ama halk yine de açık kalmasına karar verdi. Kusurları kabul edip yine de ters yönde bir kararı bildiren geçiş ifadesi: Even so (yine de).","why":["'For example' would introduce an example of the cinema's faults, but the vote to keep it open is not a fault.","'As a result' would mean the stiff seats and leaking roof caused residents to keep the cinema open, which is illogical.","Correct. 'Even so' accepts the faults just listed and then states a decision made in spite of them.","'Similarly' signals a point like the first one, but a vote to keep the cinema works against its list of faults."]},
{"id":"p3r15","t":3,"sec":"wr","p":"Most bats hunt insects by echolocation, sending out high-pitched calls and listening for the echoes. ___ many fruit bats rely mainly on their large eyes and keen sense of smell to find ripe fruit.","o":["Consequently,","For instance,","By contrast,","Likewise,"],"a":2,"ex":"Echolocation versus eyes and smell: the second sentence describes a different way of finding food, so 'By contrast'.","ex_tr":"Yankıyla avlanma ile göz ve koku duyusuna dayanma karşılaştırılıyor; ikinci cümle farklı bir yöntemi anlatıyor: By contrast.","why":["'Consequently' signals a result, but other bats' use of echolocation does not cause fruit bats to rely on their eyes.","'For instance' would make fruit bats an example of bats that hunt insects by echolocation, but they find fruit with sight and smell.","Correct. Fruit bats that rely on sight and smell are set against the many bats that hunt by echolocation.","'Likewise' signals a similarity, but fruit bats use a different method from the echolocating bats."]},
/* Practice 4 */
{"id":"p4w1","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"When engineer Kofi Adjei warned in 2009 that a planned footbridge would sway dangerously in strong crosswinds, city officials dismissed his concerns as exaggerated. Measurements taken since the bridge opened now ___ Adjei: on windy days, its deck moves nearly twice as far as the original design allowed.","o":["refute","vindicate","mystify","disparage"],"a":1,"ex":"Officials dismissed Adjei's warning, but the measurements show the bridge swaying just as he predicted, so they prove him right after others doubted him: vindicate.","ex_tr":"Yetkililer Adjei'nin uyarısını abartılı bulup dikkate almamıştı; ölçümler ise köprünün tam da onun dediği gibi sallandığını gösteriyor. Yani ölçümler onu haklı çıkarıyor: vindicate (haklı çıkarmak).","why":["Refute means to prove wrong. The measurements show the deck swaying just as Adjei warned, so they support him rather than disprove him.","Correct. Vindicate means to prove someone right after others doubted them: officials dismissed Adjei, and the measurements confirm his warning.","Mystify means to puzzle. Adjei predicted the swaying, so measurements showing it would hardly puzzle him.","Disparage means to belittle. The measurements back up Adjei's warning; they do nothing to make him look unimportant."]},
{"id":"p4w2","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Only a handful of tax lists and letters survive from Ondira, a trading kingdom that flourished about 2,500 years ago. This ___ of written records has forced historians to piece together the kingdom's commerce from other evidence, such as broken pottery, coins, and the stone foundations of its warehouses.","o":["plethora","veneration","repository","paucity"],"a":3,"ex":"'Only a handful' of documents survive, and this shortage is what forces historians to turn to pottery, coins and foundations: paucity.","ex_tr":"Sadece bir avuç belge günümüze ulaşmış; bu kıtlık tarihçileri kırık çanak çömlek, sikke ve temel kalıntıları gibi başka kanıtlara yöneltiyor: paucity (azlık, kıtlık).","why":["Plethora means an overabundance. Only a handful of documents survive, which is the opposite of too many.","Veneration means deep respect. Respect for records would not push historians toward other evidence; the scarcity of records does.","A repository is a place where things are stored. A store of records would help historians; it would not force them to rely on pottery and coins.","Correct. Paucity means too little of something; it restates 'only a handful' and explains why historians need other evidence."]},
{"id":"p4w3","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Painter Ines Duarte brushes her portraits onto warm slabs of slate using nothing but water. Visitors to her studio photograph each image the moment it is finished, because her pictures are ___; within about ten minutes, the water evaporates and the stone is blank again.","o":["abiding","exquisite","conspicuous","evanescent"],"a":3,"ex":"The part after the semicolon explains the blank: the water evaporates and each picture is gone within ten minutes, so the images fade quickly: evanescent.","ex_tr":"Noktalı virgülden sonraki kısım boşluğu açıklıyor: su on dakika içinde buharlaşıyor ve taş yeniden bomboş kalıyor. Yani resimler çabucak yok oluyor: evanescent (çabuk kaybolan, uçup giden).","why":["Abiding means lasting a long time, the opposite of a picture that vanishes within ten minutes.","Exquisite means extremely beautiful and delicate. The portraits may be lovely, but the reason given for photographing them at once is that they disappear, not that they are beautiful.","Conspicuous means easily noticed. Visibility is not the issue; visitors must photograph the pictures fast because they vanish.","Correct. Evanescent means quickly fading, like a water picture that is gone within ten minutes."]},
{"id":"p4w4","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"In its first month, a new bakery sold twice as many pastries as its owners had expected, largely because a popular food blogger had praised it. The owners chose to ___ from that unusual month, predicting similar sales for years to come, and quickly opened three more shops; two of them closed within a year.","o":["digress","diverge","extrapolate","abstain"],"a":2,"ex":"The owners used one month's figures to predict sales for years ahead, which is what extrapolate means; the closed shops show the forecast was a mistake.","ex_tr":"Sahipler tek bir ayın satışlarından yola çıkarak önümüzdeki yılların satışlarını tahmin ettiler: extrapolate (eldeki bilgilere dayanarak ileriye dönük tahmin yapmak). Kapanan dükkânlar bu tahminin yanlış olduğunu gösteriyor.","why":["Digress means to stray from the main topic of a talk or text. The owners were not wandering off a subject; they were forecasting future sales.","Diverge means to separate and go in different directions. The owners predicted similar sales, so their forecast followed that month instead of departing from it.","Correct. Extrapolate means to predict what will happen from information already known, as the owners did with one month's sales.","Abstain means to deliberately not do something. The owners acted on that month's figures; they did not hold back."]},
{"id":"p4w5","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"When a startup announced a phone battery that could fully charge in thirty seconds, many engineers were ___. They pointed out that the company had released no test results and that charging a battery so quickly would almost certainly cause it to overheat.","o":["sanguine","indifferent","skeptical","reticent"],"a":2,"ex":"The engineers point to missing test results and a likely overheating problem, so they doubt the claim: skeptical.","ex_tr":"Mühendisler hiçbir test sonucunun yayımlanmadığını ve pilin aşırı ısınacağını söylüyor; yani iddiadan şüphe ediyorlar: skeptical (şüpheci).","why":["Sanguine means optimistic. Engineers who list reasons the battery would fail are doubtful, not hopeful.","Indifferent means uninterested. The engineers cared enough to examine the claim and point out its problems.","Correct. Skeptical means not easily convinced: the engineers doubt the claim because there are no test results and such fast charging would cause overheating.","Reticent means reluctant to speak. The engineers spoke up and pointed out the claim's weaknesses."]},
{"id":"p4w6","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Long before field guides used photographs, illustrator Meral Tunc spent years drawing the wild tulips of the Anatolian highlands. Her drawings ___ the tiny differences between nearly identical species so precisely that botanists still consult them today when identifying specimens.","o":["embellish","delineate","overlook","efface"],"a":1,"ex":"Botanists still rely on the drawings because they show small differences 'so precisely', which is what delineate (to describe or portray precisely) means.","ex_tr":"Botanikçiler bu çizimlere hâlâ başvuruyor, çünkü çizimler neredeyse aynı türler arasındaki küçük farkları 'so precisely', yani son derece kesin biçimde gösteriyor: delineate (ayrıntılarıyla ve kesin olarak betimlemek).","why":["Embellish means to decorate or add invented detail. Drawings with invented detail could not be trusted for identifying real specimens.","Correct. Delineate means to describe or portray precisely, which is why botanists can still identify species from the drawings.","Overlook means to fail to notice. Drawings that missed the differences would be useless for telling nearly identical species apart.","Efface means to erase. The drawings make the differences clear; they do not wipe them out."]},
{"id":"p4w7","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Wood swells in damp air and shrinks in dry air, and no varnish can fully stop the change. Experienced furniture makers therefore regard this movement as ___ rather than as a flaw in a particular board, and they fasten every tabletop to its frame loosely enough that the wood can swell and shrink without cracking.","o":["anomalous","inherent","superficial","inconsequential"],"a":1,"ex":"All wood moves, no varnish stops it, and makers treat the movement as something other than 'a flaw in a particular board', so it is a natural part of the material: inherent.","ex_tr":"Her tahta nemli havada şişer, kuru havada büzülür; hiçbir vernik bunu tamamen durduramaz ve bu, tek bir tahtanın kusuru değildir. Yani bu hareket ahşabın doğasında vardır: inherent (doğasında bulunan).","why":["Anomalous means irregular or unusual. All wood swells and shrinks this way, and 'rather than as a flaw in a particular board' rules out treating the movement as an oddity.","Correct. Inherent means existing as a natural, inseparable part: every board moves, and no varnish can stop it.","Superficial means only at the surface. The whole board swells and shrinks, and no coat of varnish on its surface can stop it, so the change runs through the wood.","Inconsequential means unimportant. Makers who build every table to allow for the movement clearly treat it as important."]},
{"id":"p4w8","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"The Daran fern, which grows in a single mountain valley, sheds a few fronds every week, and a small brown beetle feeds only on those dead fronds. By clearing them away, the beetle keeps mold from spreading to the fern's healthy leaves. Because each species benefits from the other, ecologists describe the pair as a clear case of ___.","o":["symbiosis","depletion","antagonism","synthesis"],"a":0,"ex":"The beetle gets food and the fern stays free of mold; 'each species benefits from the other' is the definition of symbiosis.","ex_tr":"Böcek besin buluyor, eğrelti otu da küften korunuyor; iki tür de birbirinden yarar görüyor: symbiosis (iki canlının karşılıklı yarar sağladığı yakın ilişki, ortak yaşam).","why":["Correct. Symbiosis is a close relationship between two organisms that benefits both, exactly what the beetle and the fern share.","Depletion means a reduction in something. The beetle removes dead fronds, but the passage's point is that both species gain, not that something is used up.","Antagonism means hostility between opposing sides. The beetle and the fern help each other rather than fight.","Synthesis means combining ideas into a coherent whole. The passage describes two living species helping each other, not ideas being combined."]},
{"id":"p4w9","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"For decades, the hundreds of folk songs collected in the hill villages of one remote province had never been sorted in any consistent way. Musicologist Chen Jiayi changed that: her ___ places each of more than 900 songs in a category based on its rhythm, its purpose, and the instruments that accompany it.","o":["bibliography","taxonomy","synopsis","notation"],"a":1,"ex":"Songs that had 'never been sorted' are now each placed in a category by rhythm, purpose and instruments, which is a system of classification: taxonomy.","ex_tr":"Daha önce hiç düzenli biçimde sınıflandırılmamış şarkılar artık ritim, amaç ve eşlik eden çalgılara göre kategorilere ayrılıyor. Bu bir sınıflandırma sistemidir: taxonomy.","why":["A bibliography is a list of scholarly sources. Chen sorted the songs themselves into categories; she did not list sources about them.","Correct. A taxonomy is a system of classification, which is what placing every song in a category creates.","A synopsis is a brief summary. Placing 900 songs into categories is classification, not summarizing.","Notation is a system of written symbols, such as the signs used to write music down. Writing songs down does not place them in categories by rhythm and purpose."]},
{"id":"p4w10","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"In many cities, families once bought milk and meat almost every day, because fresh food spoiled within a day or two in a warm kitchen. The ___ of affordable household refrigerators changed that routine, since food could now keep for a week and one large shopping trip could replace several small ones.","o":["stigma","dearth","advent","notion"],"a":2,"ex":"The routine changed because food 'could now keep for a week', which means refrigerators had arrived in people's homes: advent (arrival).","ex_tr":"Yiyecekler artık bir hafta bozulmadan kalabildiği için alışveriş düzeni değişti; yani buzdolapları evlere girmişti: advent (gelişi, ortaya çıkışı).","why":["Stigma means strong social disapproval. Disapproval of refrigerators would not make food last longer.","Dearth means a shortage. A shortage of refrigerators would have left food spoiling quickly, not let it keep for a week.","Correct. Advent means the arrival of a notable thing: once refrigerators arrived, food kept for a week and shopping habits changed.","A notion is only an idea. The idea of a refrigerator cannot keep food fresh; actual machines in homes did."]},
{"id":"p4w11","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Halil Demir, the merchant at the center of Sevgi Gunay's novel about a nineteenth-century port town, is the richest man in his neighborhood, yet he is famously ___. He heats only one room of his large house, wears the same mended coat every winter, and argues with the baker over a single coin.","o":["affluent","garrulous","benevolent","parsimonious"],"a":3,"ex":"'Yet' sets his wealth against his habits, and the examples (one heated room, a mended coat, arguing over a coin) show he hates spending money: parsimonious.","ex_tr":"'Yet' adamın zenginliğiyle davranışları arasında bir karşıtlık kuruyor. Evinde yalnızca bir odayı ısıtması, her kış aynı yamalı paltoyu giymesi ve tek bir bozuk para için fırıncıyla tartışması, para harcamak istemediğini gösteriyor: parsimonious (cimri, eli sıkı).","why":["Affluent means wealthy. He is wealthy, but 'yet' needs something that contrasts with being the richest man, and the examples describe stinginess, not wealth.","Garrulous means excessively talkative. Arguing over a coin is about money, and the other examples have nothing to do with talking.","Benevolent means kind and generous. Nothing in his habits is generous; he argues with the baker over a single coin.","Correct. Parsimonious means very unwilling to spend money, as heating one room and arguing over a single coin show."]},
{"id":"p4w12","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"On summer mornings, a thick fog often settles over the harbor at Port Elsby, delaying the first ferries of the day. As the sun warms the air, though, the fog begins to ___, thinning into scattered wisps, and by ten o'clock passengers can usually see the far shore clearly.","o":["dissipate","coalesce","persist","undulate"],"a":0,"ex":"The fog thins 'into scattered wisps' until the far shore is visible, so it scatters and disappears: dissipate.","ex_tr":"Sis dağınık tutamlara ayrılarak inceliyor ve karşı kıyı görünür hâle geliyor; yani sis dağılıp kayboluyor: dissipate (dağılmak, yok olmak).","why":["Correct. Dissipate means to scatter or disappear, as the fog does when it thins into wisps and the shore comes into view.","Coalesce means to come together into one whole. The fog is breaking into scattered wisps, the opposite of coming together.","Persist means to continue. 'Though' signals a change from the thick morning fog, and by ten the shore is clear, so the fog does not last.","Undulate means to move up and down like waves. Fog that only rippled would still hide the shore; the clear view shows the fog is disappearing."]},
{"id":"p4w13","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Astronomers led by Lin Ruoxi called their detection of water vapor around a distant planet ___, explaining that the signal was faint and that the finding would stand only if a second telescope recorded the same pattern.","o":["tentative","momentous","unequivocal","exhaustive"],"a":0,"ex":"The astronomers stress that the signal is faint and that the result still needs a second telescope to confirm it, so they present it as uncertain: tentative.","ex_tr":"Sinyal zayıf ve sonucun geçerli sayılması için ikinci bir teleskopla doğrulanması gerekiyor; yani bulgu henüz kesin değil: tentative (kesin olmayan, geçici).","why":["Correct. Tentative means not certain or provisional: the signal is faint and the finding depends on confirmation.","Momentous means of great importance. The finding might be important, but the explanation the astronomers give is about uncertainty, not significance.","Unequivocal means clear and leaving no doubt. A faint signal that still needs confirming leaves plenty of doubt.","Exhaustive means thorough, covering every possibility. One faint signal awaiting a second check is the opposite of a complete study."]},
{"id":"p4w14","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"A sailor's letter says the town's great fire of 1712 began at the docks, while a merchant's diary insists that it started in the market square. Rather than dismiss either source, historian Nesrin Toprak has tried to ___ the two accounts, arguing that two separate fires broke out that night and later merged.","o":["conflate","disseminate","denounce","reconcile"],"a":3,"ex":"Toprak keeps both sources and explains how both can be true (two fires that later merged), which makes the conflicting accounts consistent: reconcile.","ex_tr":"Toprak iki kaynaktan hiçbirini reddetmiyor; iki ayrı yangının sonradan birleştiğini öne sürerek çelişen anlatımları bağdaştırıyor: reconcile (bağdaştırmak, uzlaştırmak).","why":["Conflate means to wrongly treat two things as the same. Toprak does the opposite: she argues that there were two separate fires.","Disseminate means to spread information widely. Toprak is working out how the accounts fit together, not spreading them to the public.","Denounce means to publicly condemn. 'Rather than dismiss either source' shows she takes both accounts seriously instead of condemning them.","Correct. Reconcile means to make consistent: two fires starting in two places let both conflicting accounts be true."]},
{"id":"p4w15","t":4,"sec":"wic","p":"Turnout in the last council election in the town of Kerrowan fell to just 18 percent. Surveys suggested that the main cause was not anger at the candidates but ___, since most residents who stayed home said they did not much care who won.","o":["apathy","consternation","antipathy","deference"],"a":0,"ex":"Residents stayed home because they 'did not much care who won', which is a lack of interest: apathy. 'Not anger' rules out a strong negative feeling.","ex_tr":"Evde kalanların çoğu kimin kazandığını pek umursamadığını söylüyor; bu ilgisizliktir: apathy (ilgisizlik, kayıtsızlık). 'Not anger' ifadesi öfke gibi güçlü olumsuz duyguları eliyor.","why":["Correct. Apathy means lack of interest or concern, exactly what residents who 'did not much care who won' describe.","Consternation means sudden anxiety or shock. Residents who did not care who won were not alarmed.","Antipathy means strong dislike. 'Not anger at the candidates' rules out a strong negative feeling, and residents said they simply did not care.","Deference means respectful submission to others' views. Staying home out of not caring shows no particular respect for anyone's judgment."]},
{"id":"p4r1","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Sea otters often wrap themselves in long strands of ___ they use the seaweed as an anchor so that they do not drift away while sleeping.","o":["kelp: and","kelp,","kelp","kelp;"],"a":3,"ex":"Both halves are complete sentences, so a semicolon is needed; the second starts with 'they', which makes a comma tempting, but a comma alone would make a comma splice.","ex_tr":"İki taraf da tam cümle ve ikincisi 'they' ile başlıyor. İki tam cümle noktalı virgülle bağlanır; tek başına virgül 'comma splice' olur.","why":["A colon is not followed by 'and' when joining two sentences; with 'and', the right mark would be a comma.","A comma alone cannot join two complete sentences; 'kelp, they use' is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together.","Correct. A semicolon can join two complete sentences, and 'they use the seaweed as an anchor' is a complete sentence."]},
{"id":"p4r2","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"The museum's new exhibit shows how early scribes recorded information on three kinds of ___ clay tablets, papyrus scrolls, and thin strips of bamboo.","o":["surfaces, such as:","surfaces;","surfaces:","surfaces"],"a":2,"ex":"'The museum's new exhibit shows how early scribes recorded information on three kinds of surfaces' is a complete sentence that introduces a list, so a colon is right.","ex_tr":"İki noktadan önceki kısım tam bir cümle; tam cümleden sonra gelen listeyi iki nokta tanıtır. 'such as' ifadesinden sonra iki nokta konmaz.","why":["A colon never comes right after 'such as'; those words already introduce the examples.","A semicolon needs a complete sentence on both sides, and the list after it is not a sentence.","Correct. A colon after a complete sentence introduces the list of three surfaces.","A complete sentence that introduces a list needs a colon before the list; with no mark, 'surfaces' runs straight into 'clay tablets'."]},
{"id":"p4r3","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Ilyas Karimov's first ___ was rejected by eleven publishers before a small press finally accepted it.","o":["novel — a story about three generations of fishermen —","novel — a story about three generations of fishermen,","novel, a story about three generations of fishermen —","novel — a story about three generations of fishermen"],"a":0,"ex":"'A story about three generations of fishermen' is an interruption that can be lifted out; one opened with a dash must be closed with a dash.","ex_tr":"'a story about three generations of fishermen' çıkarılabilen bir ara sözdür. Tireyle açılan ara söz yine tireyle kapanmalıdır.","why":["Correct. The extra description is opened and closed with matching dashes, and removing it leaves a complete sentence.","It opens with a dash but closes with a comma; the two marks around an interruption must match.","It opens with a comma but closes with a dash; the two marks around an interruption must match.","The dash opens the interruption but nothing closes it, so 'fishermen was rejected' wrongly runs the extra phrase into the main verb."]},
{"id":"p4r4","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"To learn how reef octopuses choose their prey, marine biologist Rosa Mendez spent two years ___ the animals' hunting trips with underwater cameras.","o":["recording;","recording,","recording:","recording"],"a":3,"ex":"'The animals' hunting trips' is the object of 'recording', and no punctuation goes between a verb and its object.","ex_tr":"'the animals' hunting trips', 'recording' fiilinin nesnesidir. Fiil ile nesnesinin arasına noktalama işareti konmaz.","why":["A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'the animals' hunting trips with underwater cameras' is not one.","A comma wrongly separates the verb 'recording' from its object.","A colon cannot come between a verb and its object; it would cut 'recording' off from 'the animals' hunting trips', which is what she recorded, not a list or an explanation.","Correct. The verb 'recording' leads straight into its object, 'the animals' hunting trips', with no punctuation."]},
{"id":"p4r5","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Among the objects that divers recovered from the sunken trading ship ___ a bronze compass, two silver cups, and a box of glass beads.","o":["was","were","has been","is"],"a":1,"ex":"The sentence is inverted: the subject comes after the verb and is 'a bronze compass, two silver cups, and a box of glass beads', a plural compound subject, so 'were'.","ex_tr":"Cümle devrik: özne fiilden sonra geliyor. Özne 'a bronze compass, two silver cups, and a box of glass beads'; 'and' ile bağlanmış üç öğe çoğuldur: were.","why":["'Was' is singular, but the subject after the verb is three items joined by 'and'.","Correct. The subject, which follows the verb, is three items joined by 'and', so it takes the plural 'were'.","'Has been' is singular, and the subject that follows the verb is plural.","'Is' is singular, and the subject that follows the verb is plural."]},
{"id":"p4r6","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Last spring, the city honored the volunteers ___ had spent three years restoring the painted ceiling of the old train station.","o":["which","who","whom","whose"],"a":1,"ex":"The relative pronoun refers to people (the volunteers) and is the subject of 'had spent', so 'who'.","ex_tr":"Zamir insanları ('volunteers') gösteriyor ve 'had spent' fiilinin öznesi: who. 'Which' insanlar için kullanılmaz; 'whom' ise nesne hâlidir.","why":["'Which' refers to things, not to people such as volunteers.","Correct. 'Who' refers to people and acts as the subject of 'had spent'.","'Whom' is the object form, but here the pronoun is the subject of 'had spent'.","'Whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun, as in 'whose work'; here a verb follows it directly."]},
{"id":"p4r7","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Each morning in the spring of 1903, the young astronomer Liang Wei climbed to the observatory roof, ___ the positions of the brightest stars, and mailed his notes to the university before noon.","o":["recorded","records","will record","has recorded"],"a":0,"ex":"The other verbs, 'climbed' and 'mailed', are in the simple past, and the events took place in 1903, so 'recorded'.","ex_tr":"Diğer fiiller ('climbed', 'mailed') geçmiş zamanda ve olaylar 1903'te geçiyor; boşluktaki fiil de geçmiş zamanda olmalı: recorded.","why":["Correct. 'Recorded' is in the simple past, matching 'climbed' and 'mailed' and the year 1903.","'Records' is present tense, but the other actions in the series are past and happened in 1903.","'Will record' is future tense, which cannot describe mornings in 1903.","'Has recorded' is present perfect, which does not fit a finished time like 1903 or match 'climbed' and 'mailed'."]},
{"id":"p4r8","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"A new gallery at the town's folk museum displays ___ toys from the nineteenth century, including carved wooden boats, cloth dolls, and painted spinning tops.","o":["childrens","childrens'","children's","children"],"a":2,"ex":"'Children' is already plural and does not end in -s, so its plural possessive adds 's: children's.","ex_tr":"'Children' zaten çoğuldur ve -s ile bitmez; çoğul iyelik için 's eklenir: children's.","why":["'Childrens' is not a word, and it shows no possession.","'Childrens'' adds an s that the plural does not have; 'children' is already plural.","Correct. 'Children' is an irregular plural, so its possessive is formed by adding 's.","'Children' without an apostrophe shows no possession; standard English describes toys made for children with the possessive 'children's'."]},
{"id":"p4r9","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Having interviewed more than two hundred former dockworkers, ___","o":["the historian's book gives a detailed account of how the port changed after its cranes were automated.","a detailed account of how the port changed after its cranes were automated was written by the historian.","the historian wrote a detailed account of how the port changed after its cranes were automated.","the port's history after its cranes were automated was described in detail by the historian."],"a":2,"ex":"The opening phrase describes whoever did the interviewing, and that person must come right after the comma: 'the historian'.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade ('Having interviewed ...') görüşmeleri yapan kişiyi anlatır; virgülden hemen sonra o kişi gelmelidir: the historian.","why":["This makes 'the historian's book' the one that interviewed the dockworkers; the subject is the book, not the historian.","This makes 'a detailed account' the one that interviewed the dockworkers; an account cannot interview anyone.","Correct. The historian did the interviewing, and the historian comes right after the comma.","This makes 'the port's history' the one that interviewed the dockworkers."]},
{"id":"p4r10","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Engineers fitted the city's oldest water pipes with acoustic sensors ___ leaks before they could damage the roads above.","o":["detects","to detect","detected","have detected"],"a":1,"ex":"The sentence already has its main verb, 'fitted'; 'to detect' adds a phrase that explains the purpose of the sensors.","ex_tr":"Cümlenin ana fiili zaten var ('fitted'). 'To detect' sensörlerin amacını bildiren bir ifadedir; çekimli bir fiil cümleyi bozar.","why":["'Detects' adds a second main verb with no joining word, and it is singular for the plural 'sensors'.","Correct. 'To detect' begins a phrase of purpose, explaining why the sensors were installed.","'Detected' adds a second main verb with no joining word, so the sentence no longer holds together.","'Have detected' is also a second main verb with no joining word, which breaks the sentence."]},
{"id":"p4r11","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"Because they feed mostly on hard seeds, the finches of Sorel Island have beaks that are noticeably thicker than ___ finches living on the nearby mainland.","o":["those","that of","those of","the"],"a":2,"ex":"The comparison is between beaks and beaks. 'Those' stands for the plural 'beaks', so 'those of finches' is right.","ex_tr":"Karşılaştırma gagalar ile gagalar arasında. 'Beaks' çoğul olduğu için onun yerini 'those of' tutar.","why":["'Those finches' compares the island birds' beaks with whole birds, an illogical comparison.","'That' is singular, but it has to stand for the plural 'beaks'.","Correct. 'Those' stands for 'beaks', so the island finches' beaks are compared with the beaks of mainland finches.","'The finches' compares beaks with birds, an illogical comparison."]},
{"id":"p4r12","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"While the field team was ___ the water in the river warmed by nearly two degrees, a change that the scientists later traced to a factory upstream.","o":["testing,","testing","testing;","testing:"],"a":0,"ex":"'While the field team was testing' is a dependent clause before the main clause, so a comma closes it; without one, the reader takes 'the water' as what was being tested.","ex_tr":"'While the field team was testing' bir yan cümledir; ana cümleden önce gelince virgülle ayrılır. Virgül olmazsa 'testing the water' diye yanlış okunur.","why":["Correct. A comma separates the opening dependent clause from the main clause, 'the water in the river warmed'.","Without a comma, the reader first takes 'the water' as what the team was testing, and the main clause loses its subject.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'While the field team was testing' is not one.","A colon must follow a complete sentence, and 'While the field team was testing' is not one."]},
{"id":"p4r13","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"In the experiment, every group of tomato seedlings had the same soil, the same amount of water, and the same temperature; only the hours of light differed. ___ light was the single variable that the researchers changed.","o":["However,","In other words,","For example,","Similarly,"],"a":1,"ex":"The second sentence says the same thing as the first more briefly: everything was equal except light. That is a restatement: 'In other words'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle birincide söyleneni kısaca tekrar ediyor: ışık dışında her şey aynıydı. Aynı fikri başka sözlerle söylemek: In other words.","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but the second sentence agrees with the first.","Correct. The second sentence restates the first: if only the light differed, light was the single variable changed.","'For example' introduces an example, but the second sentence sums up the first rather than illustrating it.","'Similarly' adds a new, comparable point, but the second sentence repeats the same point in other words."]},
{"id":"p4r14","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"The coastal city of Kalvara requires every new office building to collect rainwater for flushing toilets. ___ nearby Dalmor, which faces the same summer droughts, adopted an identical rule in 2021.","o":["Similarly,","Instead,","For instance,","Nevertheless,"],"a":0,"ex":"Dalmor adopted 'an identical rule' because of 'the same' droughts, so the second sentence describes something alike: 'Similarly'.","ex_tr":"Dalmor aynı kuraklık nedeniyle aynı kuralı benimsedi; ikinci cümle birinciye benzer bir durumu anlatıyor: Similarly.","why":["Correct. Dalmor faces the same droughts and adopted an identical rule, so the two cities act alike.","'Instead' signals a replacement, but Dalmor did the same thing as Kalvara, not something in its place.","'For instance' introduces an example of the previous claim, but Dalmor is a second city, not an example of Kalvara's rule.","'Nevertheless' signals something surprising in spite of the first sentence, but Dalmor's rule follows the same pattern."]},
{"id":"p4r15","t":4,"sec":"wr","p":"A late frost in April killed most of the blossoms in the valley's apricot orchards. ___ that summer's harvest was less than a third of its usual size, and the price of dried apricots nearly doubled.","o":["Likewise,","Nevertheless,","For example,","Consequently,"],"a":3,"ex":"The frost killed the blossoms (cause), so the harvest was small and prices rose (result): 'Consequently'.","ex_tr":"Don çiçekleri öldürdü (sebep); bu yüzden hasat küçüldü ve fiyatlar arttı (sonuç): Consequently.","why":["'Likewise' signals a similar, separate point, but the second sentence describes what the frost caused.","'Nevertheless' signals something surprising despite the first sentence, but a small harvest is exactly what a frost would cause.","'For example' introduces an example, but the small harvest is a result of the frost, not an example of it.","Correct. The small harvest and higher prices are the result of the frost killing the blossoms."]},
/* Practice 5 */
{"id":"p5w1","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Records show that the playwright Tomas Varga wrote more than forty comedies for a provincial court theater in the 1680s, yet only three are ___; the others are known solely from titles in the theater's account books, their scripts presumably lost in a fire.","o":["illegible","defunct","extant","obsolete"],"a":2,"ex":"'Yet only three' sets these plays against the others, which survive only as titles because their scripts were lost: the three still exist, so extant.","ex_tr":"'Yet only three' bu üç oyunu, metinleri kaybolup yalnızca adları bilinen diğerleriyle karşılaştırıyor; demek ki bu üçü hâlâ mevcut: extant (günümüze ulaşmış).","why":["Illegible means impossible to read. The contrast is between scripts that survive and scripts lost in a fire, not between readable and unreadable ones.","Defunct means no longer existing. That describes the lost plays, not the three that the sentence sets apart from them.","Correct. Extant means still in existence: three scripts survive, while the rest were lost and are known only by their titles.","Obsolete means outdated or no longer used. The point is which scripts survived the fire, not whether the comedies are still in fashion."]},
{"id":"p5w2","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"When its textile mills closed in the 1970s, the river town of Halden Falls saw its population ___ year after year, from about 12,000 residents in 1970 to barely 3,000 by 2010, and two of its three schools shut their doors.","o":["dwindle","rebound","plateau","oscillate"],"a":0,"ex":"After the mills closed, the population fell 'year after year', from 12,000 to 3,000: it gradually shrank, so dwindle.","ex_tr":"Fabrikalar kapandıktan sonra kasabanın nüfusu yıldan yıla ('year after year') 12.000'den 3.000'e düşmüş; yani nüfus yavaş yavaş erimiş: dwindle (giderek azalmak).","why":["Correct. Dwindle means to gradually diminish, as a population that falls year after year from 12,000 to 3,000 does.","Rebound means to recover after a decline. The figures show only a fall, from about 12,000 to barely 3,000, with no recovery.","Plateau means to reach a level and stay there. A fall from 12,000 to 3,000 is not a population holding steady.","Oscillate means to swing back and forth. The population moved in one direction only, down, year after year."]},
{"id":"p5w3","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Before repairing a torn silk banner, conservator Han Xiu photographed every thread under magnification and logged the exact position of more than 2,000 loose fragments; her ___ records later allowed other researchers to check each step of the restoration.","o":["haphazard","meticulous","nebulous","superficial"],"a":1,"ex":"Photographing every thread and logging the exact position of more than 2,000 fragments shows great attention to detail: meticulous.","ex_tr":"Her ipliği büyüteç altında fotoğraflamak ve 2.000'den fazla parçanın yerini tek tek kaydetmek, ayrıntılara çok dikkat etmek demektir: meticulous (titiz).","why":["Haphazard means random, without a pattern. Logging the exact position of every fragment is the opposite of random.","Correct. Meticulous means showing great attention to detail, like recording the exact position of more than 2,000 fragments.","Nebulous means vague. Records precise enough to let others check each step of the work are not vague.","Superficial means shallow, not thorough. Examining every single thread under magnification goes far below the surface."]},
{"id":"p5w4","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Unlike the permanent lakes at the edge of the basin, the shallow pools that form on the salt flat after spring storms are ___, usually drying out within two or three weeks. Even so, several species of tiny shrimp manage to hatch, grow and lay eggs in them before the water is gone.","o":["transient","desolate","perennial","tranquil"],"a":0,"ex":"'Unlike the permanent lakes' and 'usually drying out within two or three weeks' both describe pools that last only briefly: transient.","ex_tr":"'Unlike the permanent lakes' (kalıcı göllerin aksine) ve 'usually drying out within two or three weeks' (genellikle iki üç haftada kuruyan) ifadeleri kısa ömürlü gölcükleri anlatıyor: transient (geçici).","why":["Correct. Transient means lasting only a short time, like pools that dry out within two or three weeks.","Desolate means empty and bleak. Shrimp hatch and grow in the pools, and the contrast with the permanent lakes is about how long the pools last.","Perennial means lasting a long time. That describes the permanent lakes the pools are contrasted with, not pools that dry out in weeks.","Tranquil means calm and peaceful. 'Unlike the permanent lakes' and 'drying out' point to how briefly the pools exist, not to calm."]},
{"id":"p5w5","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"The objects buried with a merchant at the ancient site of Belvara are strikingly ___: alongside local clay pots lay glass beads from the coast, a bronze mirror made hundreds of kilometers to the east, and silver coins minted by at least four different kingdoms.","o":["homogeneous","pristine","heterogeneous","rudimentary"],"a":2,"ex":"The colon lists objects of many kinds from many places (local pots, coastal beads, an eastern mirror, coins from four kingdoms): heterogeneous.","ex_tr":"İki noktadan sonra farklı yerlerden gelen, farklı türde eşyalar sıralanıyor: yerel çömlekler, kıyıdan gelen boncuklar, doğuda yapılmış bir ayna, dört ayrı krallığın sikkeleri. Bu eşyalar çok çeşitli: heterogeneous (farklı türlerden oluşan).","why":["Homogeneous means all of the same kind. Pots, beads, a mirror and coins from many different places are the opposite.","Pristine means perfectly clean and unspoiled. The list after the colon is about what the objects are and where they came from, not their condition.","Correct. Heterogeneous means made up of different kinds: local pots, coastal beads, an eastern mirror and coins from four kingdoms.","Rudimentary means basic. Minted coins and a bronze mirror are not simple objects, and the list stresses variety, not simplicity."]},
{"id":"p5w6","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"For nearly a century, ships entering the harbor at Port Elling were guided by a keeper's oil lamp. The automated electric light installed in 1961 was meant to ___ the lamp, not merely assist it, and within two years the old lamp was switched off for good.","o":["augment","supersede","replenish","salvage"],"a":1,"ex":"The new light was meant to do more than assist the lamp, and the lamp was then 'switched off for good': the newer technology replaced the older one, so supersede.","ex_tr":"Yeni otomatik elektrikli ışık yalnızca lambaya yardımcı olmak için değil ('not merely assist it'), onun yerini almak için kuruldu; iki yıl içinde eski lamba tamamen söndürüldü: supersede (yerini almak).","why":["Augment means to increase or add to. 'Not merely assist it' rules out simply adding to the lamp, and the lamp was then switched off.","Correct. Supersede means to replace something older: the new light took over, and the old lamp was switched off for good.","Replenish means to fill up again. An electric light does not refill an oil lamp, and the lamp was retired, not refilled.","Salvage means to rescue the usable part of something. The old lamp was switched off for good, not rescued."]},
{"id":"p5w7","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Although historian Beatriz Solano's grandfather fought in the siege of Valmora, her account of the siege is notably ___, weighing the records of both armies with equal care and never letting family stories outweigh the documents.","o":["nostalgic","melodramatic","dispassionate","facetious"],"a":2,"ex":"'Although' her grandfather fought in the siege, she weighs both sides equally and puts documents above family stories: her account is not swayed by personal feeling, so dispassionate.","ex_tr":"'Although' bir zıtlık kuruyor: dedesi o kuşatmada savaşmış olsa da tarihçi iki ordunun kayıtlarını eşit özenle değerlendiriyor ve aile hikâyelerinin belgelerin önüne geçmesine izin vermiyor: dispassionate (tarafsız, duygularına kapılmayan).","why":["Nostalgic means longing fondly for the past. 'Although' sets up a contrast with her family tie, so her account must be the opposite of sentimental.","Melodramatic means showing far stronger emotion than the situation calls for. Weighing records with equal care is calm and balanced.","Correct. Dispassionate means impartial and not swayed by emotion: she treats both armies' records equally despite her grandfather's part in the siege.","Facetious means joking about a serious matter. Nothing suggests humor; the sentence is about fairness despite a personal connection."]},
{"id":"p5w8","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Letters by the composer Ferit Ozkaya reveal a striking ___ toward his best-known symphony: in one 1958 letter he called it the finest thing he had ever written, yet in another, written that same month, he asked an orchestra to stop performing it.","o":["contempt","reverence","indifference","ambivalence"],"a":3,"ex":"The colon gives two opposite reactions to the same work in the same month, high praise and a request to stop performing it: mixed, conflicting feelings, so ambivalence.","ex_tr":"İki noktadan sonra aynı esere aynı ay içinde iki zıt tepki var: büyük övgü ve eserin çalınmasını durdurma isteği. Bu, çelişkili duygular demektir: ambivalence (duygu karmaşası, ikircikli tutum).","why":["Contempt means regarding something as worthless. It fits his request to stop the performances but not his calling the symphony the finest thing he had ever written.","Reverence means deep respect. It fits his praise but not his request, that same month, to stop the performances.","Indifference means a lack of interest. Calling a work his finest and then trying to halt it shows strong feeling, not a lack of it.","Correct. Ambivalence means mixed, conflicting feelings: in the same month he praised the symphony and tried to stop its performances."]},
{"id":"p5w9","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Researchers at the provincial agricultural college had bred a wheat that survived long droughts, but few farmers had heard of it. To ___ the findings, the college began mailing a free monthly bulletin to every registered farm in the province.","o":["corroborate","disseminate","scrutinize","efface"],"a":1,"ex":"Few farmers knew about the wheat, so the college mailed a bulletin to every farm: its aim was to spread the research widely, so disseminate.","ex_tr":"Çiftçilerin çoğu yeni buğday çeşidinden habersizdi; tarım okulu her çiftliğe ücretsiz bülten göndererek araştırma sonuçlarını geniş kitlelere yaymak istedi: disseminate (yaymak).","why":["Corroborate means to confirm with evidence. The problem was that farmers had not heard of the wheat, and mailing bulletins spreads news; it does not test the findings.","Correct. Disseminate means to spread information widely, which is what a free bulletin mailed to every farm does.","Scrutinize means to examine closely. A bulletin sent to farmers passes the results on; it does not examine them.","Efface means to erase. The college wanted farmers to learn about the wheat, not to wipe out the findings."]},
{"id":"p5w10","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Players complained that the board game's first rulebook was hopelessly ___, with forty pages of exceptions and cross-references that even experienced gamers struggled to follow. The second edition replaced those forty pages with six built around a single principle.","o":["lucid","succinct","convoluted","frivolous"],"a":2,"ex":"Forty pages of exceptions and cross-references that 'even experienced gamers struggled to follow' describe rules that are too complicated to follow: convoluted.","ex_tr":"Deneyimli oyuncuların bile takip etmekte zorlandığı kırk sayfalık istisnalar ve çapraz göndermeler, aşırı karmaşık kurallar demektir: convoluted (karmaşık, dolambaçlı).","why":["Lucid means clear and easy to understand, the opposite of rules that even experienced gamers struggled to follow.","Succinct means brief and clearly expressed. Forty pages of exceptions are neither brief nor clear.","Correct. Convoluted means extremely complex and difficult to follow, like forty pages of exceptions and cross-references.","Frivolous means silly and not serious. The complaint is that the rules were hard to follow, not that they were silly."]},
{"id":"p5w11","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"The city had promised the Eastbank neighborhood a new park in return for its support of the highway expansion, so residents were furious when officials tried to ___ on the agreement, claiming that the land was now needed for a parking garage.","o":["concur","renege","expound","ruminate"],"a":1,"ex":"The city promised a park, then claimed the land for a garage, and residents were 'furious': the officials were trying to break their promise, so renege.","ex_tr":"Belediye park sözü vermişti; şimdi araziyi otopark için istiyor ve mahalle sakinleri öfkeli ('furious'). Yetkililer anlaşmadan dönmeye çalışıyor: renege (sözünden dönmek).","why":["Concur means to agree. Officials agreeing on the deal would not make residents furious or free the land for a garage.","Correct. Renege means to fail to keep a promise or agreement: the officials tried to back out of the promised park.","Expound means to explain in detail. Residents were angry because the promise was being broken, not explained.","Ruminate means to think deeply over time. Claiming the land for a garage is an action that breaks the deal, not quiet reflection."]},
{"id":"p5w12","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"The seeds of a desert shrub studied by botanist Chidi Nwosu have a coat so ___ that water cannot reach the embryo inside. In the wild, only a brush fire or tumbling gravel cracks the coat, so gardeners growing the shrub nick each seed with a file before planting it.","o":["dormant","pliable","vestigial","impervious"],"a":3,"ex":"'So ___ that water cannot reach the embryo' calls for a coat that lets no water through: impervious.","ex_tr":"'So ___ that water cannot reach the embryo' (o kadar ... ki su embriyoya ulaşamıyor) yapısı, suyu hiç geçirmeyen bir kabuk ister: impervious (su geçirmez).","why":["Dormant means temporarily inactive. A resting seed can be dormant, but 'so ___ that water cannot reach the embryo' needs a word for a coat that blocks water.","Pliable means bending easily. A coat that only fire, gravel or a file can crack is hard, not flexible.","Vestigial describes a small leftover trace of something larger. The coat is a working barrier that keeps water out, not a leftover.","Correct. Impervious means not allowing liquid to pass through, so water cannot reach the embryo until the coat is cracked."]},
{"id":"p5w13","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"The council's approval of the new bus route through Millbrook is ___; the line will become permanent only if a six-month trial shows that it carries at least 2,000 passengers a day.","o":["contingent","arbitrary","immutable","unequivocal"],"a":0,"ex":"The line becomes permanent 'only if' a six-month trial shows it carrying at least 2,000 passengers a day, so the approval depends on a later result: contingent.","ex_tr":"Hat ancak altı aylık denemede günde en az 2.000 yolcu taşıdığı görülürse ('only if') kalıcı olacak; yani onay gelecekteki bir sonuca bağlı: contingent (bir şarta bağlı).","why":["Correct. Contingent means depending on something in the future: the route stays only if the trial reaches 2,000 passengers a day.","Arbitrary means based on chance rather than reason. This approval follows a stated rule, a set number of daily passengers.","Immutable means unchangeable. The approval can still fall through if the trial falls short of 2,000 passengers.","Unequivocal means clear and definite, leaving no doubt. An approval that holds only if a trial succeeds is not yet definite."]},
{"id":"p5w14","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the two founders of the design studio had disagreed sharply for years about whether to expand, their parting in 2022 was surprisingly ___; they divided the clients fairly, and each still recommends the other to customers who want a different style.","o":["amicable","acrimonious","clandestine","ignominious"],"a":0,"ex":"'Although' and 'surprisingly' set up a contrast with years of sharp disagreement, and the details (a fair split, mutual recommendations) show friendliness: amicable.","ex_tr":"'Although' ve 'surprisingly', yıllarca süren sert anlaşmazlıkla bir zıtlık kuruyor; müşterileri adilce paylaşmaları ve hâlâ birbirlerini önermeleri dostça bir ayrılığı gösteriyor: amicable (dostane).","why":["Correct. Amicable means friendly despite a difficult situation: they split the clients fairly and still recommend each other.","Acrimonious means full of anger and bad feeling. Years of disagreement might lead one to expect that, but 'although' and 'surprisingly' signal the opposite.","Clandestine means done in secret. Nothing suggests the split was hidden; the point is how friendly it was.","Ignominious means shameful and publicly disgraceful. Dividing clients fairly and recommending each other is nothing to be ashamed of."]},
{"id":"p5w15","t":5,"sec":"wic","p":"Viewers of a new film about an 1820s whaling voyage quickly spotted a glaring ___: in one scene the captain checks the time on a wristwatch, a device that did not come into common use until nearly a century later.","o":["paradox","precursor","relic","anachronism"],"a":3,"ex":"A wristwatch in a story set about a century before such watches were common is something placed in the wrong period: anachronism.","ex_tr":"Kol saatinin yaygınlaşmasından yaklaşık yüz yıl önce geçen bir sahnede kol saati görülmesi, yanlış döneme ait bir ayrıntıdır: anachronism (dönem hatası, anakronizm).","why":["A paradox is a statement that contradicts itself yet may be true. A wristwatch in an 1820s scene is not a puzzling truth; it is simply out of its time.","A precursor comes before the thing it leads to. The wristwatch belongs to a later time than the scene, not an earlier one.","A relic is an object that survives from an earlier time. The wristwatch comes from a later period than the 1820s, so it is not a relic in that scene.","Correct. An anachronism is something placed in the wrong historical period, like a wristwatch in a scene set a century before such watches were common."]},
{"id":"p5r1","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"The new library in Kesta is heated entirely by the ground beneath ___ pipes buried forty meters deep carry warmth up from the soil all winter.","o":["it;","it,","it","it, which"],"a":0,"ex":"Both halves are complete sentences ('The new library ... beneath it' and 'pipes ... all winter'), so a semicolon joins them.","ex_tr":"İki taraf da tam cümle; iki tam cümleyi noktalı virgül birleştirir. Tek başına virgül 'comma splice' olur.","why":["Correct. Each half is a complete sentence, and a semicolon can join two complete sentences.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together (a run-on).","'Which' cannot open a clause that is already complete: 'pipes ... carry warmth up from the soil' has its own subject and verb, so the sentence no longer makes sense."]},
{"id":"p5r2","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"After surveying a pine forest in the Tarn Hills for three years, ecologist Jonas Weller reached a surprising ___ the oldest trees were not the tallest but among the shortest, stunted by the thin soil on the ridges.","o":["conclusion:","conclusion,","conclusion","conclusion that:"],"a":0,"ex":"'Jonas Weller reached a surprising conclusion' is a complete clause, and what follows spells out that conclusion, so a colon introduces it.","ex_tr":"'... reached a surprising conclusion' kısmı tam bir cümle; sonrası bu sonucun ne olduğunu açıklıyor, bu yüzden iki nokta gelir. 'That'ten sonra iki nokta konmaz.","why":["Correct. A colon after a complete clause introduces an explanation, here what the surprising conclusion was.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together.","A colon must come after a complete clause. 'Reached a surprising conclusion that' is unfinished, so a colon cannot follow 'that'."]},
{"id":"p5r3","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Lake Orra ___ freezes from shore to shore only once every few decades; the last time it did, villagers drove trucks across the ice.","o":["(the deepest lake in the region),","(the deepest lake in the region,",", the deepest lake in the region)","(the deepest lake in the region)"],"a":3,"ex":"'The deepest lake in the region' is extra information, so it is set off by a matching pair of marks, two parentheses, with no comma between the closing parenthesis and the verb 'freezes'.","ex_tr":"'the deepest lake in the region' ek bilgidir; aynı türden bir çift işaretle, burada iki parantezle açılıp kapanmalıdır.","why":["The parentheses match, but the comma after them separates the subject 'Lake Orra' from its verb 'freezes'.","It opens with a parenthesis but closes with a comma; the marks must match.","It opens with a comma but closes with a parenthesis; the marks must match.","Correct. The extra information opens and closes with parentheses, a matching pair."]},
{"id":"p5r4","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"On the island of Sava, the only plants ___ had roots reaching more than two meters below the surface, so farmers there now favor deep-rooted crops.","o":["that survived the long drought,",", that survived the long drought,","that survived the long drought",", that survived the long drought"],"a":2,"ex":"'That survived the long drought' tells which plants are meant, so it is essential and takes no commas; a 'that' clause is not set off with commas.","ex_tr":"'that survived the long drought' hangi bitkilerin kastedildiğini belirtir; bu temel (essential) bilgidir ve virgülle ayrılmaz.","why":["The comma after 'drought' separates the subject 'plants' from its verb 'had'.","Commas would mark the clause as extra information, but it is essential, and a 'that' clause is not set off with commas.","Correct. The clause identifies which plants are meant, so it needs no commas.","A single comma before 'that' cuts an essential clause off from 'plants', and the comma is never closed."]},
{"id":"p5r5","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"The length of a swallow's wings and the shape of its tail ___ how sharply the bird can turn in midair, an ability it relies on to catch insects in flight.","o":["is determining","determines","determine","has determined"],"a":2,"ex":"Two things joined by 'and' ('the length ...' and 'the shape ...') make a plural subject, so the verb is 'determine'.","ex_tr":"'And' ile bağlanan iki özne ('the length ...' ve 'the shape ...') birlikte çoğul bir özne oluşturur; fiil de çoğul olmalı: determine.","why":["'Is' is singular, and the subject joined by 'and' is plural.","'Determines' is singular. It would agree with 'the shape of its tail' alone, but the subject is both things together.","Correct. 'The length ... and the shape ...' is a compound subject joined by 'and', which takes a plural verb.","'Has' is singular, and the subject joined by 'and' is plural."]},
{"id":"p5r6","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Built in 1874, the clock tower in Ferrand Square still keeps accurate time, although ___ original bells were replaced with lighter ones in 1950.","o":["its","their","it's","they're"],"a":0,"ex":"The bells belong to the clock tower, a single object, so the singular possessive 'its' (no apostrophe) is needed.","ex_tr":"Çanlar saat kulesine ait; kule tekil bir nesne: its. 'It's' = it is, 'they're' = they are.","why":["Correct. 'Its' is the singular possessive, and the bells belong to one clock tower.","'Their' is plural, but the only owner in the sentence is the single clock tower.","'It's' means 'it is', which makes no sense before 'original bells'.","'They're' means 'they are', which makes no sense before 'original bells'."]},
{"id":"p5r7","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"When the canal company was founded in 1820, its directors promised investors that the waterway ___ within five years; in fact, construction took eleven.","o":["will open","would open","has opened","opens"],"a":1,"ex":"The promise was made in the past about a time after it, so the future-in-the-past form is needed: 'would open'.","ex_tr":"Söz 1820'de, o tarihten sonrası için verilmiş; geçmişten bakılan gelecek 'would' ile anlatılır: would open.","why":["'Will' looks ahead from the present, but the promise belongs to 1820, so the verb must shift back to 'would'.","Correct. 'Would' expresses a future seen from a past moment: in 1820 the opening still lay ahead.","The present perfect describes something already done by now; in 1820 the canal had not opened.","The simple present does not fit a promise made in 1820 about an opening that was still in the future."]},
{"id":"p5r8","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"After reviewing all five ___ proposals for the new footbridge, the city's design panel chose the one submitted by a group of engineering students.","o":["team's","teams'","teams","teams's"],"a":1,"ex":"Five teams each submitted a proposal: plural 'teams' plus possession, so the apostrophe goes after the s: teams'.","ex_tr":"Beş takım (çoğul) + iyelik: kesme işareti s'den sonra gelir: teams'.","why":["'Team's' is the possessive of a single team, but there are five.","Correct. The proposals belong to five teams: plural, then an apostrophe after the s.","'Teams' is plural but shows no possession, and the proposals belong to the teams.","Adding 's to a plural that already ends in s is not standard; a plural possessive takes only an apostrophe."]},
{"id":"p5r9","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Born in a small fishing village on the Black Sea coast, ___","o":["the boats that came in each morning inspired Nehir Karaca to study marine biology.","Nehir Karaca's interest in marine biology began as she watched the boats come in.","marine biology became Nehir Karaca's passion as she watched the boats come in.","Nehir Karaca spent her childhood watching the boats come in and later became a marine biologist."],"a":3,"ex":"An opening modifier describes whatever comes right after the comma. Only a person is born in a village, so 'Nehir Karaca' must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade ('Born in a small fishing village ...') virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi niteler; köyde doğan kişi Nehir Karaca olduğu için virgülden sonra onun adı gelmelidir.","why":["This makes 'the boats' the ones born in a fishing village.","The subject is 'interest', not Nehir Karaca, so this says her interest was born in a fishing village.","This makes 'marine biology' the thing born in a fishing village.","Correct. Nehir Karaca is the one born in the village, and she comes right after the comma."]},
{"id":"p5r10","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Over the past thirty years, the Torva Glacier has retreated nearly a kilometer up its valley, ___ a lake of meltwater where the ice once stood.","o":["this leaves","it left","it has left","leaving"],"a":3,"ex":"After the comma, the participle 'leaving' adds the result without starting a new sentence; a subject and verb after the comma would make a comma splice.","ex_tr":"Virgülden sonra 'leaving' ile başlayan ortaç öbeği yeni bir cümle kurmadan sonucu ekler; özne ve fiille başlayan bir cümle virgülden sonra 'comma splice' olur.","why":["'This leaves' starts another complete sentence after a comma: a comma splice.","'It left' gives the second part its own subject and verb, so the comma joins two complete sentences: a comma splice.","'It has left' also makes a complete sentence after a comma: a comma splice.","Correct. 'Leaving' starts a participle phrase that adds the result to the sentence, so a comma is enough."]},
{"id":"p5r11","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"At the wildlife rescue center, volunteers feed the injured birds, clean their cages, and ___ each bird's weight in a logbook every morning.","o":["recording","record","to record","recorded"],"a":1,"ex":"Items in a series must share the same form: feed ..., clean ..., and record ....","ex_tr":"Listedeki üç öğe aynı yapıda olmalı: feed ..., clean ..., and record ....","why":["'Recording' is an -ing form, but the first two items are plain verbs, 'feed' and 'clean'.","Correct. 'Record' matches 'feed' and 'clean': three present-tense verbs for what the volunteers do.","'To record' adds 'to', which breaks the pattern of 'feed' and 'clean'.","'Recorded' is past tense, but 'feed' and 'clean' are present tense."]},
{"id":"p5r12","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Biologist Zhou Min spent three winters at a frozen mountain pond because she wanted to know ___","o":["could the frogs buried in its mud survive months without oxygen.","whether the frogs buried in its mud could survive months without oxygen?","could the frogs buried in its mud survive months without oxygen?","whether the frogs buried in its mud could survive months without oxygen."],"a":3,"ex":"A question reported inside a statement keeps statement word order ('whether the frogs could ...') and ends with a period.","ex_tr":"Düz bir cümlenin içine yerleştirilmiş soru, düz cümle sırasını korur ('whether the frogs could ...') ve nokta ile biter.","why":["'Could the frogs ...' is the word order of a direct question; inside a statement it should be 'whether the frogs could ...'.","The whole sentence is a statement about what she wanted to know, not a question, so it ends with a period.","This drops a direct question into the middle of a statement; a reported question uses 'whether' and statement word order, and ends with a period.","Correct. The question is reported inside a statement, so it keeps statement word order and ends with a period."]},
{"id":"p5r13","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"In 1911, a fire destroyed most of the town hall's records. ___ the town clerk spent ten years rebuilding the lost lists of births and marriages from church registers and family letters.","o":["Subsequently,","Previously,","Meanwhile,","For instance,"],"a":0,"ex":"The fire came first and the ten years of rebuilding came after it, so a word meaning 'afterward' fits: 'Subsequently'.","ex_tr":"Önce yangın çıkıyor ve kayıtlar yok oluyor; ardından memur on yıl boyunca listeleri yeniden oluşturuyor: Subsequently (daha sonra).","why":["Correct. The clerk rebuilt the lost lists after the fire destroyed them.","'Previously' would place the rebuilding before the fire, but records cannot be rebuilt before they are lost.","'Meanwhile' means at the same time, but the clerk spent ten years rebuilding the lists after the fire, not during it.","'For instance' introduces an example, but the clerk's work is a response to the fire, not an example of it."]},
{"id":"p5r14","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"The striped gecko of the Ombra dunes rarely drinks. ___ researchers who tracked fifty of the geckos for two years never once saw one drink; the animals get the moisture they need from the insects they eat.","o":["However,","In fact,","Similarly,","Otherwise,"],"a":1,"ex":"The second sentence goes further than the first ('rarely drinks' becomes 'never once saw one drink'), so it intensifies the point: 'In fact'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle ilkini güçlendiriyor: 'rarely drinks' (nadiren su içer) iddiası, 'never once saw one drink' (bir kez bile su içerken görülmedi) ile pekişiyor: In fact (hatta, aslında).","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but never seeing a gecko drink supports the claim that it rarely drinks.","Correct. 'In fact' introduces a stronger version of the claim: not just rarely, but never once in two years.","'Similarly' introduces a second, comparable case, but both sentences describe the same geckos.","'Otherwise' means 'if not' or 'in other respects', and neither meaning links the two sentences."]},
{"id":"p5r15","t":5,"sec":"wr","p":"Cork is light enough to float, which makes it useful for fishing nets. ___ it resists rot and insulates well, so it is also used for floors and wall panels.","o":["For example,","However,","Moreover,","Consequently,"],"a":2,"ex":"The second sentence adds more useful qualities of cork (it resists rot and insulates), and 'also' confirms the addition: 'Moreover'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle mantarın (cork) başka yararlı özelliklerini ekliyor: çürümez ve iyi yalıtım sağlar. 'Also' kelimesi de bir ekleme yapıldığını gösteriyor: Moreover (ayrıca, üstelik).","why":["'For example' introduces an instance of the first claim, but resisting rot is not an example of floating.","'However' signals a contrast, but both sentences list useful qualities of cork.","Correct. 'Moreover' adds further points: besides floating, cork resists rot and insulates.","'Consequently' signals a result, but cork's resistance to rot is not caused by its ability to float."]},
/* Practice 6 */
{"id":"p6w1","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"A museum label claims that a carved stone found on Tarsk Island links the island's first settlers to a culture on the distant mainland. The link is ___ at best, since it rests on a single faint symbol that some geologists believe is only a natural scratch in the rock.","o":["irrefutable","momentous","conspicuous","tenuous"],"a":3,"ex":"'At best' and evidence of one faint symbol that may be a natural scratch show the link is weak and may not exist at all: tenuous.","ex_tr":"Bağlantı, doğal bir çizik de olabilecek tek bir soluk işarete dayanıyor; yani çok zayıf, belki de hiç yok: tenuous (zayıf, temelsiz).","why":["Irrefutable means impossible to disprove. A link that rests on one faint mark, which may be a natural scratch, is easy to doubt.","Momentous means of great historic importance. 'At best' and the doubtful evidence point to weakness, not importance.","Conspicuous means easily noticed. The evidence is a single faint symbol, so nothing about the link stands out.","Correct. Tenuous means weak and possibly not real, which fits a link built on one faint, uncertain symbol."]},
{"id":"p6w2","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Within a week of taking power, the new government moved to ___ the mining treaty its predecessor had signed, declaring the agreement void and giving the foreign companies that had operated under it thirty days to leave the country.","o":["validate","abrogate","enumerate","embellish"],"a":1,"ex":"'Declaring the agreement void' restates the blank; the new government officially cancels the treaty: abrogate.","ex_tr":"'Declaring the agreement void' (anlaşmayı geçersiz ilan etmek) boşluğu açıklıyor; yeni hükümet antlaşmayı resmen iptal ediyor: abrogate (yürürlükten kaldırmak).","why":["Validate means to confirm officially. A government that declares the treaty void and orders the companies out is rejecting it, not confirming it.","Correct. Abrogate means to cancel officially, which is exactly what declaring the treaty void does.","Enumerate means to list items one by one. The government does not list the treaty's terms; it ends the treaty.","Embellish means to decorate or add detail. Declaring the agreement void removes it rather than adding to it."]},
{"id":"p6w3","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"The botanists Irina Kovac and Daniel Mensah began as close collaborators, sharing seeds and field notes for a decade. Their later letters, however, are strikingly ___, with each accusing the other of stealing credit for a discovery and several ending without even a polite signature.","o":["amicable","frivolous","acrimonious","nostalgic"],"a":2,"ex":"'However' turns away from the friendly early years, and accusations of stolen credit with no polite signature show anger and bad feeling: acrimonious.","ex_tr":"'However' dostça geçen ilk yıllarla bir karşıtlık kuruyor. Birbirlerini bir keşfin hakkını çalmakla suçlamaları ve bazı mektupların nazik bir kapanış bile olmadan bitmesi öfke ve kırgınlık gösteriyor: acrimonious (öfkeli, kin dolu).","why":["Amicable means friendly and pleasant. That describes the early collaboration, but 'however' signals that the later letters were different.","Frivolous means silly and not serious. Accusing a colleague of stealing credit for a discovery is a serious charge.","Correct. Acrimonious means full of anger and bad feeling, as the accusations and curt endings show.","Nostalgic means longing fondly for the past. Letters full of accusations show anger, not fond memories of their years together."]},
{"id":"p6w4","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"In forty years of records kept at the field station, the lake froze over by mid-January every winter but one. That ___, the ice-free winter of 1998, still puzzles the station's researchers, since none of their climate models can reproduce it.","o":["paradigm","corollary","anomaly","consensus"],"a":2,"ex":"The lake froze every winter 'but one', and that single winter breaks the pattern and still puzzles the researchers: anomaly.","ex_tr":"Kırk yıllık kayıtlarda göl, bir kış hariç her kış donmuş; o tek kış alışılmış düzenin dışında kalıyor ve araştırmacıları hâlâ şaşırtıyor: anomaly (düzensizlik, istisna).","why":["Paradigm means a model or typical example. The ice-free winter is the one case that does not fit the pattern, the opposite of typical.","Corollary means a natural consequence of something. The ice-free winter does not follow from anything in the records; no model can even reproduce it.","Correct. Anomaly means a deviation from the usual pattern, like the one winter in forty when the lake did not freeze.","Consensus means an opinion shared by a group. A winter is an event, and the researchers are puzzled, not in agreement."]},
{"id":"p6w5","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Merchants in the river town resented the heavy toll collected on every cart that crossed the stone bridge, and court records show that many found ways to ___ it, paying boatmen to ferry their goods across at night from a quiet landing a mile upstream.","o":["circumvent","abolish","tout","reinforce"],"a":0,"ex":"Paying boatmen to cross at night somewhere else is a clever way of avoiding the bridge toll without ending it: circumvent.","ex_tr":"Tüccarlar köprü geçiş ücretini ödememek için mallarını geceleri başka bir yerden kayıkla karşıya geçiriyor; yani ücreti kurnazca atlatıyorlar: circumvent (atlatmak, etrafından dolaşmak).","why":["Correct. Circumvent means to avoid something, especially cleverly or illegally, like ferrying goods across at night to skip the bridge.","Abolish means to end something officially. The merchants could not end the toll; it stayed in place and they simply avoided paying it.","Tout means to promote or praise. The merchants resented the toll, so they would not advertise it.","Reinforce means to strengthen. Merchants who resented the toll and crossed elsewhere were weakening it, not strengthening it."]},
{"id":"p6w6","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"When the drought-tolerant rice variety was released, some officials hailed it as a ___ for the region's food problems, as if a single seed could also fix its poor roads, costly fertilizer and crowded markets. Farmers, who knew how many separate problems they faced, were far more cautious.","o":["catalyst","panacea","malady","paradox"],"a":1,"ex":"'As if a single seed could also fix' roads, fertilizer prices and markets shows the officials treated the rice as a cure for every problem: panacea.","ex_tr":"'As if a single seed could also fix ...' ifadesi, yetkililerin bu pirinci her derde deva saydığını gösteriyor: panacea (her derde deva).","why":["Catalyst means something that speeds up change. A catalyst 'for the region's food problems' would make the problems grow faster, the opposite of what the officials hoped.","Correct. Panacea means a cure-all, which is how officials treated a seed they expected to fix roads, fertilizer prices and markets too.","Malady means a disease or serious problem. Officials who 'hailed' the rice were praising it, not calling it a problem.","Paradox means a statement that seems to contradict itself. Nothing contradictory is described; the officials simply expected too much of one seed."]},
{"id":"p6w7","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Sociologists studying a crowded apartment district found that shared community gardens can ___ trust among neighbors who had barely spoken before. After a single season of planting together, residents were twice as likely to lend one another tools or to watch one another's children.","o":["engender","gauge","undermine","relegate"],"a":0,"ex":"Neighbors who 'had barely spoken before' became twice as likely to help each other, so the gardens created trust that was not there: engender.","ex_tr":"Daha önce neredeyse hiç konuşmayan komşuların birbirine yardım etme olasılığı iki katına çıkıyor; yani bahçeler, önceden var olmayan bir güveni doğuruyor: engender (doğurmak, yol açmak).","why":["Correct. Engender means to give rise to, which fits trust appearing among neighbors who had barely spoken.","Gauge means to measure. The sociologists might measure trust, but the subject of the blank is the gardens, and gardens do not measure anything.","Undermine means to weaken. Residents became more willing to help each other, so trust grew instead of weakening.","Relegate means to lower in rank. Nothing is being demoted; trust is being created."]},
{"id":"p6w8","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"The council hoped to rename the town's main street in honor of a local inventor, but the old name, Market Lane, proved too ___ to dislodge; it appeared on two centuries of maps and property deeds, and residents went on using it long after the new signs went up.","o":["ephemeral","obscure","versatile","entrenched"],"a":3,"ex":"Two centuries of maps and deeds, and residents who kept using the old name, show it was too firmly established to remove: entrenched.","ex_tr":"Ad iki yüzyıldır haritalarda ve tapularda geçiyor, halk da yeni tabelalara rağmen eski adı kullanmayı sürdürüyor; yani ad, sökülüp atılamayacak kadar yerleşmiş: entrenched (kökleşmiş).","why":["Ephemeral means lasting a very short time. A name used for two centuries is the opposite of short-lived.","Obscure means unclear or hard to understand. 'Market Lane' is a plain name everyone used, and an unclear name would be easier to replace, not harder.","Versatile means useful in many different ways. Nothing in the text is about the name serving several purposes; its long history is what kept it in place.","Correct. Entrenched means deeply established, like a name fixed in two centuries of records and in residents' habits."]},
{"id":"p6w9","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Unlike the steady pulses recorded from the pulsars in the radio survey, the signal from the newly detected source was ___, sometimes arriving twice in a single minute and then vanishing for several hours before returning without warning.","o":["uniform","dormant","potent","erratic"],"a":3,"ex":"'Unlike the steady pulses' sets up a contrast, and a signal that comes twice in a minute and then disappears for hours is irregular: erratic.","ex_tr":"'Unlike the steady pulses' bir karşıtlık kuruyor; sinyal bazen dakikada iki kez geliyor, sonra saatlerce kayboluyor. Bu, düzensiz ve öngörülemez bir davranış: erratic (düzensiz).","why":["Uniform means constant and unvarying, which describes the steady pulses that the new signal is contrasted with.","Dormant means inactive. The signal goes quiet for hours, but it also arrives twice in a minute; the point is its irregular pattern, not inactivity.","Potent means powerful. The text describes when the signal arrives, not how strong it is.","Correct. Erratic means irregular and unpredictable, like a signal that comes twice in a minute and then disappears for hours."]},
{"id":"p6w10","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Rather than patent her low-cost water filter, the engineer Nadia Rahimi chose to ___ the royalties it might have earned her, publishing the full design online so that any village workshop could build the filter from local clay and sawdust.","o":["accrue","forgo","disclose","salvage"],"a":1,"ex":"By not patenting the filter and giving the design away, she gave up money she could have made: forgo.","ex_tr":"Filtrenin patentini almayıp tasarımı herkese açık yayımlaması, kazanabileceği lisans gelirinden vazgeçmesi demek: forgo (vazgeçmek, feragat etmek).","why":["Accrue means to accumulate over time. 'Rather than patent' shows she chose not to collect royalties, so there was nothing to build up.","Correct. Forgo means to give up something desirable, which is what she does with the royalties by publishing the design freely.","Disclose means to make something known. She disclosed the design, not the royalties, which were never earned.","Salvage means to rescue the usable part of something. Nothing was lost or damaged; she simply chose not to earn the money."]},
{"id":"p6w11","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"The poet Mira Castell's spelling was famously ___: she wrote 'nite' for 'night' and 'thru' for 'through,' capitalized any word she wished to stress, and followed habits that no other writer or editor of her era shared.","o":["idiosyncratic","orthodox","illegible","meticulous"],"a":0,"ex":"Odd spellings and capitals that 'no other writer or editor of her era shared' were peculiar to her alone: idiosyncratic.","ex_tr":"Döneminin başka hiçbir yazarının ya da editörünün paylaşmadığı yazım alışkanlıkları yalnızca ona özgü: idiosyncratic (kendine özgü, tuhaf).","why":["Correct. Idiosyncratic means peculiar to one person, which fits habits that no other writer or editor shared.","Orthodox means conventional. Writing 'nite' and 'thru' breaks the conventions of her time rather than following them.","Illegible means impossible to read. 'Nite' and 'thru' are unusual but easy to read; the point is that the habits were hers alone.","Meticulous means paying great attention to accuracy. Her spellings depart from the standard forms, and the text stresses how unusual they were, not how careful."]},
{"id":"p6w12","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Engineers designed the new breakwater to shelter the harbor, but coastal scientists warn that it will also ___ the natural drift of sand along the shore, starving the beaches to the south of the sediment that has kept them from shrinking.","o":["facilitate","impede","emulate","replenish"],"a":1,"ex":"'Starving' the southern beaches of sand means the breakwater will block the sand's natural movement: impede.","ex_tr":"Güneydeki plajların kumdan 'aç kalması' (starving), dalgakıranın kumun doğal hareketini engelleyeceğini gösteriyor: impede (engellemek).","why":["Facilitate means to make easier. If the breakwater helped the sand drift, the southern beaches would receive more sediment, not be starved of it.","Correct. Impede means to block or hold back, as a breakwater that stops sand from reaching the southern beaches does.","Emulate means to imitate. A breakwater does not copy the drift of sand; the scientists warn that it will interfere with it.","Replenish means to fill up again. The warning is that the beaches will lose sand, so the breakwater is not refilling anything."]},
{"id":"p6w13","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"The mold that damaged the library's map collection was especially ___ because it grew for years inside tightly folded sheets, where no one could see it. By the time staff noticed a faint musty smell in the archive, hundreds of maps were stained beyond repair.","o":["flagrant","benign","transient","insidious"],"a":3,"ex":"Harm that grew for years out of sight and was discovered only when it was too late is gradual and hidden: insidious.","ex_tr":"Küf yıllarca kimsenin göremediği yerde büyümüş ve fark edildiğinde iş işten geçmiş; zarar yavaş ve gizlice ilerlemiş: insidious (sinsi).","why":["Flagrant means shockingly obvious. This mold grew where no one could see it, the opposite of obvious.","Benign means harmless. Hundreds of maps were stained beyond repair, so the mold was far from harmless.","Transient means lasting only a short time. The mold grew for years before anyone found it.","Correct. Insidious means causing harm gradually and secretly, like mold spreading unseen for years."]},
{"id":"p6w14","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the physicist's research papers are notoriously dense, her book for general readers is remarkably ___, using a single everyday example and not one equation to explain how tiny particles can pass through solid barriers.","o":["lucid","convoluted","esoteric","melodramatic"],"a":0,"ex":"'Although' contrasts dense papers with a book that explains a hard idea through one everyday example and no equations, so the book is clear: lucid.","ex_tr":"'Although' yoğun makaleleri, zor bir konuyu tek bir gündelik örnekle ve hiç denklem kullanmadan anlatan kitapla karşılaştırıyor; kitap açık ve anlaşılır: lucid.","why":["Correct. Lucid means clear and easy to understand, which a single everyday example with no equations achieves.","Convoluted means extremely complex. That describes the dense papers, and 'although' says the book is different.","Esoteric means understood only by specialists. A book for general readers with no equations is written for everyone.","Melodramatic means showing exaggerated emotion. The contrast is between dense and clear writing, not about emotion."]},
{"id":"p6w15","t":6,"sec":"wic","p":"The company said it was closing its riverside factory in order to modernize the equipment, but internal emails released during a later lawsuit showed that the upgrade was only a ___; managers had planned for years to sell the land to a housing developer.","o":["concession","grievance","pretext","rebuttal"],"a":2,"ex":"The stated reason (the upgrade) hid the real one (selling the land), so the upgrade was a pretended reason: pretext.","ex_tr":"Açıklanan gerekçe (modernizasyon) asıl amacı (araziyi satmak) gizliyordu; yani modernizasyon yalnızca bir bahaneydi: pretext (bahane).","why":["Concession means something given up to settle a disagreement. No dispute is described; the emails show the upgrade hid a plan to sell.","Grievance means a complaint. The upgrade was the company's stated reason for closing, not something it complained about.","Correct. Pretext means a pretended reason that hides the real one, as the upgrade hid the plan to sell the land.","Rebuttal means a statement arguing that something is untrue. The upgrade was not an argument against anything; it was a cover story."]},
{"id":"p6r1","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The mountain town's first public library opened in 1902 with only four hundred ___ its collection fills a three-story building that also houses a recording studio for local musicians.","o":["books. Today","books, today","books today","books, today,"],"a":0,"ex":"Both halves are complete sentences, so a period must separate them; a comma alone makes a comma splice, and no punctuation makes a run-on.","ex_tr":"İki kısım da tam cümle, bu yüzden araya nokta gelmeli. Yalnızca virgül koymak 'comma splice', hiç işaret koymamak 'run-on' hatası olur.","why":["Correct. 'The library opened ... with only four hundred books' and 'Today its collection fills ...' are both complete sentences, and a period separates them.","A comma alone cannot join two complete sentences; this is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together (a run-on).","A second comma after 'today' does not help: two complete sentences are still joined by a comma alone, which is a comma splice."]},
{"id":"p6r2","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The prize for the year's most original experiment went to a researcher whom few people outside her own laboratory had ever heard ___ Mei Tanaka, a doctoral student who had built her equipment from salvaged parts.","o":["of;","of:","of","of, she was"],"a":1,"ex":"A complete sentence comes before the blank, and the name that follows tells us who the researcher is; a colon introduces it.","ex_tr":"Boşluktan önce tam bir cümle var; ardından gelen isim, araştırmacının kim olduğunu söylüyor. Böyle bir açıklamadan önce iki nokta (colon) kullanılır.","why":["A semicolon must have a complete sentence on both sides, and 'Mei Tanaka, a doctoral student ...' is not a sentence.","Correct. A colon after a complete sentence can introduce the name the sentence has been building up to.","Without punctuation the name becomes the object of 'of', but 'whom' already fills that role, so the sentence no longer makes sense.","'She was Mei Tanaka ...' is a complete sentence, and joining it to the first with only a comma is a comma splice."]},
{"id":"p6r3","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The Medlin ___ still carries barges of grain and timber to the coast every week.","o":["Canal which was dug by hand in the 1820s","Canal which was dug by hand in the 1820s,","Canal, which was dug by hand in the 1820s","Canal, which was dug by hand in the 1820s,"],"a":3,"ex":"The canal is already named, so the 'which' clause is extra information and needs a comma before and after it.","ex_tr":"Kanalın adı zaten verilmiş, bu yüzden 'which' ile başlayan kısım ek bilgidir ve iki virgül arasına alınmalıdır.","why":["With no commas the clause is treated as essential, but a named canal needs no clause to identify it; extra information must be set off with commas.","The extra information is closed with a comma but never opened.","The extra information is opened with a comma but never closed, so it runs straight into 'still carries'.","Correct. The name already identifies the canal, so the 'which' clause adds extra information and is set off by a pair of commas."]},
{"id":"p6r4","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The volunteers measured the height of every seedling in the test ___ recorded the results in a shared spreadsheet before they left the greenhouse each evening.","o":["plots; and","plots, and","plots and","plots, they"],"a":2,"ex":"'The volunteers' is the subject of two verbs, 'measured' and 'recorded'. Two verbs that share one subject are joined by 'and' with no comma.","ex_tr":"'The volunteers' öznesinin iki fiili var: 'measured' ve 'recorded'. Aynı özneyi paylaşan iki fiil, araya virgül koymadan 'and' ile bağlanır.","why":["A semicolon must join two complete sentences, but 'and recorded the results ...' has no subject of its own.","A comma before 'and' wrongly separates two verbs that share one subject; there is no new subject after 'and'.","Correct. 'Measured' and 'recorded' share the subject 'the volunteers', so 'and' joins them with no comma.","'They recorded the results ...' is a complete sentence, and joining it to the first with only a comma is a comma splice."]},
{"id":"p6r5","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Each of the twelve murals along the new subway platform ___ a different neighborhood of the city as it looked a century ago.","o":["have depicted","depict","are depicting","depicts"],"a":3,"ex":"The subject is 'each', which is singular; 'of the twelve murals' only describes it. So the verb is singular: 'depicts'.","ex_tr":"Özne 'each', yani tekil; 'of the twelve murals' yalnızca onu niteliyor. Fiil de tekil olmalı: depicts.","why":["'Have depicted' is plural and does not agree with the singular subject 'each'.","'Depict' is plural; it agrees with 'murals', but 'murals' sits inside the phrase 'of the twelve murals' and is not the subject.","'Are depicting' is plural and does not agree with the singular subject 'each'.","Correct. The subject 'each' is singular, so it takes the singular verb 'depicts'."]},
{"id":"p6r6","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The conservators who spent a year cleaning the cathedral's painted ceiling explained ___ methods at a public lecture, showing how centuries of soot had been removed without disturbing the original colors.","o":["its","their","his or her","there"],"a":1,"ex":"The methods belong to 'the conservators', a plural noun, so the pronoun must be plural: 'their'. 'Cathedral' and 'ceiling' come in between but do not own the methods.","ex_tr":"Yöntemler restorasyon uzmanlarına (the conservators, çoğul) ait, bu yüzden zamir de çoğul olmalı: their. Aradaki 'cathedral' ve 'ceiling' yöntemlerin sahibi değil.","why":["'Its' is singular and would point to the ceiling or the cathedral, which have no methods; the conservators do.","Correct. 'Their' is plural and refers back to 'the conservators', who own the methods.","'His or her' is singular, but 'the conservators' is plural.","'There' names a place; it is not a possessive pronoun and cannot come before 'methods'."]},
{"id":"p6r7","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Since 1994, the small observatory on Kale Hill ___ its telescope to local schoolchildren every clear Friday night, free of charge.","o":["opened","has opened","had opened","will open"],"a":1,"ex":"'Since 1994' describes a habit that started in the past and continues now, which takes the present perfect: 'has opened'.","ex_tr":"'Since 1994' geçmişte başlayıp bugün de süren bir durumu anlatır; bu da present perfect ister: has opened.","why":["The simple past describes a finished action and clashes with 'since 1994', which reaches up to the present.","Correct. 'Since 1994' marks a habit running from the past up to now, which takes the present perfect.","The past perfect needs a later past moment to look back from, and the sentence has none.","The future tense does not fit a habit that began in 1994."]},
{"id":"p6r8","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The ___ newest map, which took her four years to complete, shows every footpath, spring and stone wall on the island of Kesri.","o":["cartographer's","cartographers'","cartographers","cartographer"],"a":0,"ex":"'Her' shows there is one cartographer, and the map belongs to her, so use the singular possessive: 'cartographer's'.","ex_tr":"'Her' tek bir haritacı olduğunu gösteriyor ve harita ona ait; tekil iyelik gerekir: cartographer's.","why":["Correct. One cartographer ('her') owns the map, so the singular possessive adds an apostrophe and s.","An apostrophe after the s marks the possessive of several cartographers, but 'her' shows there is only one.","'Cartographers' is a plain plural and shows no ownership of the map.","'Cartographer' with no apostrophe shows no ownership, so it cannot tell us whose map it is."]},
{"id":"p6r9","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"After analyzing soil samples from forty farms in the valley, ___","o":["the researchers concluded that crop rotation had doubled the number of earthworms.","the conclusion was that crop rotation had doubled the number of earthworms.","crop rotation, the researchers concluded, had doubled the number of earthworms.","the number of earthworms, the researchers concluded, had been doubled by crop rotation."],"a":0,"ex":"An opening phrase describes whatever comes right after the comma. Only people can analyze soil samples, so 'the researchers' must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade, virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi anlatır. Toprak örneklerini inceleyenler araştırmacılardır, bu yüzden özne 'the researchers' olmalı.","why":["Correct. The researchers did the analyzing, and they come right after the comma.","This makes 'the conclusion' the one that analyzed the soil samples.","This makes 'crop rotation' the one that analyzed the soil samples.","This makes 'the number of earthworms' the one that analyzed the soil samples."]},
{"id":"p6r10","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"The bronze bell that hangs in the clock tower above the market square ___ cast in 1782 and still rings every hour.","o":["being","which was","was","having been"],"a":2,"ex":"'That hangs in the clock tower' is a relative clause, so the bell still needs a main verb to pair with 'and still rings': 'was cast'.","ex_tr":"'That hangs in the clock tower' bir yan cümle (relative clause); 'the bronze bell' öznesinin, 'and still rings' ile eşleşecek bir ana fiile ihtiyacı var: was cast.","why":["'Being cast' is a participle, not a main verb, so 'and' would join it to the main verb 'still rings' as if they were the same kind of verb; the bell needs 'was cast' to pair with 'rings'.","'Which was' starts another relative clause that swallows 'cast in 1782 and still rings every hour', so 'the bronze bell' never gets a main verb and the sentence is a fragment.","Correct. 'Was cast' gives the subject 'the bronze bell' its main verb, and 'still rings' follows as a second main verb joined by 'and'.","'Having been cast' is not a main verb either, so 'and' cannot pair it with the main verb 'still rings'; only 'was cast' gives the bell two matching main verbs."]},
{"id":"p6r11","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Before returning to its home museum in the autumn, the traveling exhibition of hand-drawn maps will visit three port cities: Valparaiso, ___ Mombasa, Kenya; and Porto, Portugal.","o":["Chile;","Chile,","Chile","Chile:"],"a":0,"ex":"The list items already contain commas (city, country), so semicolons separate the items, as after 'Kenya'.","ex_tr":"Liste öğelerinin içinde zaten virgül var (şehir, ülke); bu yüzden öğeler, 'Kenya'dan sonra olduğu gibi, noktalı virgülle ayrılır.","why":["Correct. Each item contains a comma, so a semicolon separates 'Valparaiso, Chile' from the next item, matching the one after 'Kenya'.","A comma here makes it unclear where one city-and-country pair ends and the next begins, and it breaks the semicolon pattern used after 'Kenya'.","With no punctuation, 'Chile' runs straight into 'Mombasa' and the first two items merge.","A colon cannot separate items within a list; the list has already been introduced by the colon after 'cities'."]},
{"id":"p6r12","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Every spring the valley fills with the same ___ of migrating cranes that stop to feed in the flooded rice fields.","o":["visitors. Thousands","visitors; thousands","visitors thousands","visitors — thousands"],"a":3,"ex":"The words after the blank explain who the 'same visitors' are, and a dash can introduce such an explanation at the end of a sentence.","ex_tr":"Boşluktan sonraki kısım, 'the same visitors' ifadesindeki ziyaretçilerin kim olduğunu açıklıyor. Cümle sonundaki böyle bir açıklama tireyle (dash) verilebilir.","why":["A period leaves 'Thousands of migrating cranes that stop to feed in the flooded rice fields' as a fragment; 'that stop' belongs to a relative clause, so there is no main verb.","A semicolon needs a complete sentence after it, but 'thousands of migrating cranes that stop to feed ...' has no main verb.","With no punctuation, 'visitors' and 'thousands of migrating cranes' run together and the sentence does not make sense.","Correct. The dash introduces the closing explanation of who the visitors are, thousands of migrating cranes."]},
{"id":"p6r13","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Critics have praised the novelist's debut for its vivid characters and inventive structure. ___ its final chapters feel rushed, as though the author were hurrying to meet a deadline. Even so, it remains one of the most ambitious first novels of the decade.","o":["For instance,","Consequently,","Admittedly,","Likewise,"],"a":2,"ex":"The second sentence admits a weakness in a book the passage otherwise praises, and 'Even so' then returns to the praise: 'Admittedly'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle, övülen kitabın bir kusurunu kabul ediyor; 'Even so' ise yeniden övgüye dönüyor. Bu bir kabul (concession) geçişidir: Admittedly.","why":["'For instance' would introduce an example of vivid characters or inventive structure, but a rushed ending is a weakness, not an example of either.","'Consequently' would mean the praise caused the rushed chapters, which makes no sense.","Correct. 'Admittedly' concedes a flaw, the rushed ending, before 'Even so' returns to the book's strengths.","'Likewise' signals a similar point, but a rushed ending is a criticism, unlike the praise before it."]},
{"id":"p6r14","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"Most lizards in the dry hills are active by day, basking on sunlit rocks to warm their bodies. ___ a small, pale skink found only in the valley's dunes hunts after sunset and spends the hottest hours buried in loose sand.","o":["In addition,","Similarly,","As a result,","By contrast,"],"a":3,"ex":"Most lizards are active by day, while this skink is active at night, so the two sentences describe opposite habits: 'By contrast'.","ex_tr":"Kertenkelelerin çoğu gündüz etkin, bu küçük kertenkele ise gün batımından sonra avlanıyor; iki cümle zıt alışkanlıkları anlatıyor: By contrast.","why":["'In addition' adds a point of the same kind, but the skink's habits are the opposite of those just described.","'Similarly' would mean the skink behaves like the other lizards, but it is active at night, not by day.","'As a result' would mean the other lizards' daytime habits cause the skink's night hunting, which the text does not suggest.","Correct. A skink that hunts after sunset and hides from the heat is set against lizards that are active by day."]},
{"id":"p6r15","t":6,"sec":"wr","p":"In many hot, dry regions, builders are reviving techniques their ancestors relied on. ___ rammed-earth walls, made by packing damp soil into wooden frames, are again appearing in inland towns because they keep rooms cool without air conditioning.","o":["However,","For example,","Nevertheless,","In other words,"],"a":1,"ex":"Rammed-earth walls are one specific case of an old technique being revived, so the second sentence gives an example: 'For example'.","ex_tr":"Sıkıştırılmış toprak (rammed-earth) duvarlar, yeniden canlandırılan eski tekniklerden somut bir örnek: For example.","why":["'However' signals a contrast, but the second sentence supports the first rather than opposing it.","Correct. Rammed-earth walls are a specific instance of the old techniques that builders are reviving.","'Nevertheless' signals that something happens despite the first sentence, but the revival of rammed earth is part of what the first sentence describes.","'In other words' restates the first sentence, but the second narrows it to one particular technique instead of rephrasing it."]},
/* Practice 7 */
{"id":"p7w1","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"When the linguist Tomas Reyes published his short study of whistled languages in 1958, few reviewers took notice. Within two decades, however, the study was widely regarded as ___, cited in nearly every later survey of the subject and credited with shaping the questions that researchers in the field still ask.","o":["peripheral","contentious","seminal","ephemeral"],"a":2,"ex":"'However' turns away from the quiet first reception, and a study cited everywhere and credited with shaping later research is highly influential: seminal.","ex_tr":"'However' ilk yıllardaki ilgisizliğin sonradan değiştiğini gösteriyor; sonraki hemen her derlemede kaynak gösterilen ve araştırmacılara hâlâ yön veren bir inceleme çok etkilidir: seminal (çığır açan).","why":["Peripheral means of minor importance. That may describe how reviewers treated the study in 1958, but 'however' marks a change, and a study cited in nearly every survey is central.","Contentious means causing disagreement. The passage reports wide citation and credit, not arguments over the study.","Correct. Seminal means full of new ideas and strongly influential on later work, like a study credited with shaping the questions researchers still ask.","Ephemeral means lasting a very short time. A study still cited in nearly every survey decades later had a lasting influence."]},
{"id":"p7w2","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Gu Yiran's paintings look as if they were dashed off in an afternoon, but their loose, careless-seeming brushstrokes ___ the months of planning behind each canvas; her notebooks contain dozens of careful studies for every finished work.","o":["belie","supplant","evince","embellish"],"a":0,"ex":"'But' sets the paintings' hasty look against the months of planning behind them, so the brushstrokes give a false impression of that planning: belie.","ex_tr":"'But' aceleyle yapılmış gibi görünen tablolarla aylarca süren hazırlığı karşılaştırıyor; özensiz görünen fırça darbeleri bu emeği gizliyor, yanlış bir izlenim veriyor: belie (olduğundan farklı göstermek).","why":["Correct. Belie means to give a false impression of something: strokes that look careless hide the long planning behind each canvas.","Supplant means to replace. The brushstrokes do not take the place of the planning; they hide it.","Evince means to show something clearly. Strokes that look careless and dashed off do not make months of planning obvious; only the notebooks reveal it.","Embellish means to decorate or exaggerate. Careless-looking strokes make the planning seem smaller, not grander."]},
{"id":"p7w3","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Because the ship's logbook was never recovered, the archaeologists had no written record from which to ___ where the vessel had been built. They eventually settled the question another way, matching the growth rings in its oak planks to trees felled near a river port on the Baltic coast.","o":["disclose","ascertain","forestall","stipulate"],"a":1,"ex":"'Settled the question' restates the blank: the team needed a way to find out where the ship was built, and the tree rings supplied it: ascertain.","ex_tr":"'Settled the question' boşluğu yeniden ifade ediyor: ekip geminin nerede yapıldığını kesin olarak öğrenmenin bir yolunu arıyordu ve ağaçların yıllık halkaları bu yolu sağladı: ascertain (tespit etmek, kesinleştirmek).","why":["Disclose means to make something known publicly. The archaeologists did not yet know where the ship was built, so they had nothing to reveal.","Correct. Ascertain means to find out or confirm, which is what the team later did when it 'settled the question' with tree rings.","Forestall means to prevent something by acting in advance. The ship was built long ago, and nothing here is being prevented.","Stipulate means to specify or require explicitly, as in a contract. The team was trying to discover a fact about the past, not set a requirement."]},
{"id":"p7w4","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Historians of ocean travel note how sharply the steamship Meridian, launched in 1911, broke with the older ships of its own line. Where those ships' passengers had eaten in cramped, low-ceilinged saloons, its ___ dining hall could seat four hundred people at once with room to spare between the tables.","o":["secluded","ostentatious","rudimentary","capacious"],"a":3,"ex":"'Where' sets the new hall against cramped saloons, and seating four hundred with room to spare describes a very roomy space: capacious.","ex_tr":"'Where' yeni salonu eski gemilerin dar salonlarıyla karşılaştırıyor; dört yüz kişiyi rahatça alan bir salon çok geniştir: capacious (geniş, ferah).","why":["Secluded means hidden away and private. A hall where four hundred passengers eat together is a shared public space.","Ostentatious means showy and meant to impress. The contrast in the passage is about space (cramped versus room to spare), not display.","Rudimentary means basic. The hall is presented as a clear advance over earlier ships, not as a simple, undeveloped one.","Correct. Capacious means roomy, the opposite of the cramped saloons, and fits a hall that seats four hundred with room to spare."]},
{"id":"p7w5","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Most new buildings in the region's hot climate depend on air conditioning, but the architect Folake Adeyemi prefers to ___ it altogether in the schools she designs, relying instead on thick earthen walls, shaded courtyards and high vents that draw warm air out of the classrooms.","o":["augment","advocate","eschew","calibrate"],"a":2,"ex":"'But' sets her apart from buildings that depend on air conditioning, and 'relying instead' shows she does without it: eschew.","ex_tr":"'But' ve 'relying instead' mimarın klimadan bilerek uzak durduğunu, onun yerine başka yöntemler kullandığını gösteriyor: eschew (kaçınmak).","why":["Augment means to increase. 'Relying instead' on walls and vents means she does not use air conditioning at all, let alone add more of it.","Advocate means to voice support for something. 'But' sets her apart from buildings that depend on air conditioning, so she is not promoting it.","Correct. Eschew means to avoid. She does without air conditioning 'altogether' and relies on walls, courtyards and vents instead.","Calibrate means to fine-tune something for accuracy. She does not adjust air conditioning; she replaces it with walls, courtyards and vents."]},
{"id":"p7w6","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"The company's handbook says nothing about working past six o'clock, yet new employees soon learn that the expectation to stay late is ___: managers praise those who remain at their desks into the evening and rarely promote anyone who leaves on time.","o":["implicit","tenuous","explicit","provisional"],"a":0,"ex":"The handbook 'says nothing', yet managers' praise and promotions send the message, so the expectation is understood without being stated: implicit.","ex_tr":"El kitabı bu konuda hiçbir şey söylemiyor, ama yöneticilerin davranışından herkes bu beklentiyi anlıyor; beklenti dile getirilmeden ima ediliyor: implicit (örtük, üstü kapalı).","why":["Correct. Implicit means implied though not plainly expressed: the handbook is silent, but praise and promotions carry the message.","Tenuous means weak and possibly not real. Praise for late workers and few promotions for the rest show a strong expectation, not a weak one.","Explicit means stated clearly and in detail. The handbook 'says nothing' about staying late, so the expectation is not stated.","Provisional means temporary and possibly changed later. Nothing suggests the expectation is about to change; the point is that it goes unstated."]},
{"id":"p7w7","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"For thirty years, mounted couriers carried letters between the valley's towns in a little over a day. Once a telegraph line reached the valley in 1869, however, messages arrived within minutes, and the courier service quickly became ___; the last riders were dismissed and their stables sold two years later.","o":["pivotal","dormant","volatile","obsolete"],"a":3,"ex":"The telegraph did the couriers' job far faster, and the service was then shut down for good, so it was no longer needed: obsolete.","ex_tr":"Telgraf aynı işi çok daha hızlı yapınca ulak hizmetine gerek kalmadı ve hizmet tamamen kapatıldı: obsolete (artık kullanılmayan, modası geçmiş).","why":["Pivotal means of crucial importance. 'However' marks the service's decline, and dismissing the riders shows it had lost its importance.","Dormant means temporarily inactive. Dismissing the riders and selling the stables ended the service for good, so it was not waiting to start again.","Volatile means liable to change rapidly and unpredictably. The service did not swing back and forth; it simply stopped being needed.","Correct. Obsolete means no longer used or useful: once messages arrived in minutes, riders taking a day were not needed."]},
{"id":"p7w8","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"The fuel subsidy remains popular with the region's fishing crews, but economists argue that its effect on the fishery itself has been ___. By lowering the cost of long voyages, it encouraged boats to stay at sea longer, and cod stocks have fallen by half since the subsidy began.","o":["benign","pernicious","inconsequential","salubrious"],"a":1,"ex":"'But' sets the subsidy's popularity against its results, and cod stocks falling by half show a very harmful effect: pernicious.","ex_tr":"'But' yakıt desteğinin sevilmesiyle sonuçlarını karşılaştırıyor; morina stoklarının yarıya inmesi çok zararlı bir etkiyi gösterir: pernicious (zararlı, yıkıcı).","why":["Benign means gentle and harmless. 'But' contrasts the subsidy's popularity with its damage, and halved cod stocks are not harmless.","Correct. Pernicious means having a very harmful effect, and cod stocks falling by half are serious harm.","Inconsequential means unimportant and not affecting the outcome. Losing half the cod is a major consequence.","Salubrious describes a pleasant, clean and healthy place. The subsidy damaged the fishery rather than making it healthier."]},
{"id":"p7w9","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"When the salt tax was raised for the third year in a row, the farmers of the province grew increasingly ___. Petitions gave way to marches on the governor's residence, and by the following spring officials were writing to the capital that they feared an open revolt.","o":["complacent","docile","restive","despondent"],"a":2,"ex":"Petitions turning into marches and officials fearing revolt show farmers who were impatient and becoming hard to control: restive.","ex_tr":"Dilekçelerin yerini yürüyüşlerin alması ve yetkililerin isyandan korkması, çiftçilerin huzursuzlaştığını ve kontrol altında tutulmalarının zorlaştığını gösteriyor: restive (huzursuz, sabırsız).","why":["Complacent means self-satisfied and unconcerned. Farmers marching against a rising tax are anything but unconcerned.","Docile means easy to control, the opposite of a population whose protests make officials fear revolt.","Correct. Restive means uneasy, impatient and hard to control, which fits farmers who move from petitions to marches until officials fear revolt.","Despondent means in low spirits from loss of hope. The farmers escalate from petitions to marches, which shows agitation and a hope of change, not hopelessness."]},
{"id":"p7w10","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"In redesigning the irrigation pump, the engineers removed every part they judged ___, testing each change on a working model. The finished pump has nineteen components instead of thirty-one, costs less to build, and moves just as much water as the original.","o":["superfluous","durable","integral","novel"],"a":0,"ex":"The pump moves just as much water with twelve fewer parts, so the parts that were removed were not needed: superfluous.","ex_tr":"Yeni pompada on iki parça daha az var, ama eskisi kadar su taşıyor; demek ki çıkarılan parçalar gereksizdi: superfluous (gereğinden fazla, gereksiz).","why":["Correct. Superfluous means more than is needed: the pump moves just as much water without those twelve parts.","Durable means sturdy and able to withstand damage. Engineers would have no reason to remove parts for being sturdy; the passage is about need, not strength.","Integral means necessary to make a whole complete. Removing necessary parts would weaken the pump, but it works just as well.","Novel means new and original. Nothing suggests the removed parts were new designs; the point is that the pump did not need them."]},
{"id":"p7w11","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"No census was taken in the river town of Marrowby before 1850, so historians have had to ___ its earlier population indirectly, counting the houses shown on old tax maps and multiplying by the average number of people in a household.","o":["validate","aggrandize","conflate","gauge"],"a":3,"ex":"With no census, historians count houses and multiply by an average household size, which is a way of estimating: gauge.","ex_tr":"Nüfus sayımı olmadığı için tarihçiler evleri sayıp bu sayıyı ortalama hane büyüklüğüyle çarpıyor; bu bir tahmin yöntemidir: gauge (ölçmek, tahmin etmek).","why":["Validate means to confirm officially that something is true. With no census there is no earlier figure to confirm; historians must produce an estimate.","Aggrandize means to make something seem grander than it is. The historians are trying to estimate the population, not inflate it.","Conflate means to wrongly treat two things as the same. Counting houses and multiplying is a method of calculation, not a mix-up of two things.","Correct. Gauge means to measure or estimate, and counting houses and multiplying by an average household size is an estimate."]},
{"id":"p7w12","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Ines Navarro did not pick up a cello until she was nineteen, an age at which most professional cellists have already been playing for well over a decade. Through hours of daily practice, however, she became ___ enough within six years to win a seat in a national orchestra.","o":["proficient","precocious","reticent","erratic"],"a":0,"ex":"'However' sets her late start against her progress, and winning a seat in a national orchestra shows she became highly skilled: proficient.","ex_tr":"'However' geç başlamasıyla sonraki ilerlemesini karşılaştırıyor; ulusal bir orkestraya girmek çok yetkin olmayı gerektirir: proficient (yetkin, becerikli).","why":["Correct. Proficient means skilled and competent, which a seat in a national orchestra requires.","Precocious means developed earlier than usual. She started at nineteen, late by the passage's own standard, so her skill came from practice, not early development.","Reticent means reserved and reluctant to speak. Shyness has nothing to do with winning an orchestra seat.","Erratic means irregular and unpredictable. Hours of daily practice leading to an orchestra seat show steady progress, and an erratic player would not win the seat."]},
{"id":"p7w13","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Unlike most diarists of her time, who wrote for their families or hoped to publish within their lifetimes, the botanist Aiko Mori kept her journals with ___ in mind: she sealed every volume and asked that none be opened until a century after her death.","o":["forebears","prosperity","posterity","expediency"],"a":2,"ex":"Sealing the journals until a century after her death means she was writing for readers not yet born: posterity.","ex_tr":"Günlüklerini ölümünden yüz yıl sonrasına kadar mühürlemesi, henüz doğmamış okurlar için yazdığını gösterir: posterity (gelecek kuşaklar).","why":["Forebears means ancestors, people who lived before her. Journals sealed for a hundred years are meant for people who come after her.","Prosperity means success and wealth. Nothing in the passage concerns money, and sealing the journals earned her nothing.","Correct. Posterity means future generations, the only readers who could open journals sealed until a century after her death.","Expediency means choosing what is convenient over what is right. Sealing every volume for a century is the opposite of the easy choice."]},
{"id":"p7w14","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"To ___ the bark beetles that had already killed a fifth of the island's pines, rangers released a native wasp that lays its eggs inside beetle larvae. Within three years, the number of newly damaged trees had fallen by more than half.","o":["bolster","thwart","track","emulate"],"a":1,"ex":"Releasing a wasp that attacks beetle larvae, after which new damage fell by more than half, is a way of stopping the beetles: thwart.","ex_tr":"Kabuk böceği larvalarının içine yumurta bırakan yerli bir yaban arısı salınıyor ve yeni hasar yarıdan fazla azalıyor; amaç böcekleri durdurmak: thwart (engellemek, önüne geçmek).","why":["Bolster means to support or strengthen. Releasing an enemy of the beetles weakens them rather than strengthening them.","Correct. Thwart means to stop someone or something from doing something: the wasp checked the beetles, and new damage fell by more than half.","Track means to follow or trace the path of something. Releasing a wasp that attacks the larvae does not follow the beetles; it fights them.","Emulate means to copy someone's achievement. Rangers have no wish to copy beetles that are killing the island's pines."]},
{"id":"p7w15","t":7,"sec":"wic","p":"Despite coming from four countries and sharing no single first language, the eight members of the research crew became remarkably ___ during their winter at the polar station, dividing chores without argument and settling disagreements in a single evening's conversation.","o":["fractious","heterogeneous","taciturn","cohesive"],"a":3,"ex":"'Despite' sets their different origins against how well they worked together; sharing chores and settling disagreements quickly describe a united group: cohesive.","ex_tr":"'Despite' farklı ülkelerden gelmeleriyle ne kadar uyumlu çalıştıklarını karşılaştırıyor; işleri tartışmadan paylaşmak ve anlaşmazlıkları hemen çözmek birlik içindeki bir grubu anlatır: cohesive (kenetlenmiş, uyumlu).","why":["Fractious means irritable and quarrelsome. A crew that divides chores without argument is the opposite.","Heterogeneous means made up of different kinds. The crew was mixed from the start; 'despite' needs a quality that contrasts with that mix, and 'became' needs a change.","Taciturn means habitually uncommunicative. A crew that talks its disagreements through in one evening is not silent.","Correct. Cohesive describes a group whose members are united, like a crew that shares chores without argument and settles disagreements quickly."]},
{"id":"p7r1","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The mapmakers surveyed the northern coastline in a single ___ the mountainous interior took them another nine years to chart.","o":["summer, but","summer,","summer but","summer, however"],"a":0,"ex":"Two independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction such as 'but' need a comma before the conjunction.","ex_tr":"İki tam cümle 'but' bağlacıyla birleşince bağlaçtan önce virgül konur; 'however' ise bağlaç değildir.","why":["Correct. A comma plus the coordinating conjunction 'but' properly joins two complete sentences.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","Without a comma, two long independent clauses joined by 'but' run together; standard punctuation puts a comma before the conjunction.","'However' is not a coordinating conjunction, so a comma before it creates a comma splice; it would need a semicolon before it and a comma after it."]},
{"id":"p7r2","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The plants that the restoration team will reintroduce to the wetland once the old dam is removed ___ cattails, blue flag iris, and water mint.","o":["include:","include;","include,","include"],"a":3,"ex":"The list is the object of the verb 'include', so no punctuation separates the verb from it; a colon must follow a complete sentence.","ex_tr":"Liste 'include' fiilinin nesnesidir; fiille nesnesi arasına noktalama işareti konmaz. İki nokta ancak tam bir cümleden sonra gelir.","why":["A colon must come after a complete sentence, and the sentence is not complete until 'include' has its object.","A semicolon joins two complete sentences, and the list after it is not a sentence.","A comma between a verb and its object breaks the sentence for no reason.","Correct. The list is the object of 'include', and nothing should separate a verb from its object."]},
{"id":"p7r3","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The lighthouse's original ___ now sits in a glass case at the entrance of the town museum.","o":["lens; cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters,","lens, cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters","lens cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters,","lens, cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters,"],"a":3,"ex":"'Cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters' is extra information about the lens, so it needs a comma on both sides.","ex_tr":"'cleaned and repaired by volunteers over two winters' mercek hakkında ek bilgidir; iki yanından virgülle ayrılmalıdır.","why":["A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'The lighthouse's original lens' is not one.","The phrase is opened with a comma but never closed, so 'winters' runs straight into the verb 'sits'.","The phrase is closed with a comma but never opened, which leaves a single comma between the subject and its verb.","Correct. The phrase adds extra information about the lens and is set off by a pair of commas."]},
{"id":"p7r4","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Working from a single surviving notebook, ___ spent a decade reviving a firing technique that had not been used in her region since the eighteenth century.","o":["glass artist, Yui Kondo,","glass artist, Yui Kondo","glass artist Yui Kondo","glass artist Yui Kondo,"],"a":2,"ex":"A descriptive title placed directly before a name ('glass artist Yui Kondo') belongs to the name and takes no commas.","ex_tr":"İsimden hemen önce gelen unvan ('glass artist Yui Kondo') isimle bir bütündür; araya virgül konmaz, özneyle fiil arasına da virgül girmez.","why":["Commas around the name treat it as extra information, but with no 'the' or 'a' before 'glass artist', the title and name form one unit that cannot be split.","A comma between 'glass artist' and the name splits the title from the name it belongs to.","Correct. 'Glass artist' works as a title before the name, so no comma separates them, and no comma comes between the subject and 'spent'.","A comma between the subject 'glass artist Yui Kondo' and its verb 'spent' wrongly separates them."]},
{"id":"p7r5","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Neither the original blueprints nor the builder's diary ___ how the bridge's central arch was raised without scaffolding, a puzzle that engineers still debate.","o":["explain","explains","have explained","were explaining"],"a":1,"ex":"With 'neither ... nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'diary', which is singular: 'explains'.","ex_tr":"'Neither ... nor' yapısında fiil yakındaki özneye uyar: 'diary' tekil olduğu için explains.","why":["'Explain' is plural; it would agree with 'blueprints', but 'diary' is closer to the verb.","Correct. The nearer subject, 'diary', is singular, so the verb takes the singular form.","'Have explained' is plural, so it cannot agree with the singular 'diary'.","'Were explaining' is plural, and the sentence states a present fact, not an action in progress in the past."]},
{"id":"p7r6","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The museum's collection of pocket watches is rarely shown in full, because ___ fills more cases than any single gallery can hold.","o":["they","them","it","those"],"a":2,"ex":"The pronoun refers to 'collection', which is singular (the verb 'fills' confirms it), not to 'watches': 'it'.","ex_tr":"Zamir 'watches'a değil 'collection'a gönderme yapıyor; 'collection' tekildir, 'fills' fiili de bunu gösterir: it.","why":["'They' is plural. It seems to match the nearby 'watches', but what fills the cases is the collection, and 'they fills' is not grammatical.","'Them' is an object pronoun and cannot be the subject of 'fills'.","Correct. 'It' refers to the singular 'collection' and agrees with the singular verb 'fills'.","'Those' is plural, so it agrees with neither 'collection' nor the singular verb 'fills'."]},
{"id":"p7r7","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"When archaeologist Selin Aksoy returned to the cave in 2015, she found that looters ___ several of the painted panels shown in her photographs from 2005.","o":["have removed","remove","will have removed","had removed"],"a":3,"ex":"The looting happened before she returned in 2015, an earlier past event, so it takes the past perfect: 'had removed'.","ex_tr":"Yağma, 2015'teki dönüşünden önce yaşanmıştı; geçmişteki bir olaydan daha önce olan olay için past perfect kullanılır: had removed.","why":["The present perfect 'have removed' connects to the present, but the sentence is set in 2015 and describes what she found then.","The present tense 'remove' clashes with the past-tense frame set by 'returned' and 'found'.","'Will have removed' looks ahead to the future, but the looting had already happened when she arrived.","Correct. The removal happened before her return in 2015, and the past perfect marks the earlier of two past events."]},
{"id":"p7r8","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Over the past decade, the number of community ___ in the city has tripled, and many now offer free workshops on composting and saving seeds.","o":["gardens'","garden's","gardens","garden"],"a":2,"ex":"'Gardens' is simply plural here; nothing belongs to the gardens, so it takes no apostrophe.","ex_tr":"Burada 'gardens' yalnızca çoğul bir isimdir; bahçelere ait bir şey yok, bu yüzden kesme işareti gerekmez.","why":["'Gardens'' is a plural possessive, but no noun follows that the gardens could own.","'Garden's' is a singular possessive, but the gardens own nothing in this sentence, and 'the number of' needs a plural.","Correct. 'The number of' needs a plural noun, and nothing here belongs to the gardens, so there is no apostrophe.","'Garden' is singular, but 'the number of' counts things, so it needs the plural."]},
{"id":"p7r9","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Natural dyes made from plants tend to fade quickly in sunlight. A weaver and dye chemist, ___","o":["Leyla Karimi developed a method for fixing plant dyes so that they no longer fade in sunlight.","Leyla Karimi's method for fixing plant dyes keeps them from fading in sunlight.","a method for fixing plant dyes so that they no longer fade in sunlight was developed by Leyla Karimi.","plant dyes that no longer fade in sunlight were the result of Leyla Karimi's new fixing method."],"a":0,"ex":"An opening phrase that names a role ('A weaver and dye chemist') must be followed by the person it describes: Leyla Karimi.","ex_tr":"Cümle başındaki 'A weaver and dye chemist' ifadesi virgülden hemen sonra gelen kişiyi tanımlamalı: Leyla Karimi.","why":["Correct. The weaver and dye chemist is Leyla Karimi, and her name comes right after the comma.","This makes 'Leyla Karimi's method' the weaver and dye chemist; the possessive name only describes the method, which cannot be a person.","The opening phrase must describe the noun right after the comma, and here that noun is 'a method', which cannot be a weaver and dye chemist.","The opening phrase must describe the noun right after the comma, and here that noun is 'plant dyes', which cannot be a weaver and dye chemist."]},
{"id":"p7r10","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Periodical cicadas, which spend thirteen or seventeen years feeding underground as nymphs, ___ in vast numbers over just a few weeks in late spring.","o":["to emerge","emerging","emerge","having emerged"],"a":2,"ex":"The subject 'Periodical cicadas' needs a main verb, and only 'emerge' is a finite verb.","ex_tr":"'Periodical cicadas' öznesinin bir ana fiile ihtiyacı var; seçenekler arasında çekimli fiil yalnızca 'emerge'.","why":["'To emerge' is an infinitive, which cannot serve as the main verb, so the sentence becomes a fragment.","'Emerging' cannot be a main verb on its own, so the sentence becomes a fragment.","Correct. 'Emerge' is a finite verb and gives the subject 'cicadas' the main verb the sentence needs.","'Having emerged' is a participle, not a main verb, so the sentence becomes a fragment."]},
{"id":"p7r11","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The new rail tunnel will not only shorten the trip between the two cities by an hour but also ___ thousands of trucks off the mountain highway each week.","o":["taking","take","it will take","takes"],"a":1,"ex":"'Not only' and 'but also' must be followed by matching forms: 'not only shorten ... but also take'.","ex_tr":"'Not only ... but also' yapısında iki taraf aynı biçimde olmalı: not only shorten ... but also take.","why":["'Taking' is an -ing form and does not match 'shorten'.","Correct. 'Take' matches 'shorten': both are base verbs that follow 'will'.","'It will take' adds a new subject and verb, which breaks the parallel with 'shorten'.","'Takes' cannot follow 'will', and it does not match 'shorten'."]},
{"id":"p7r12","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"In her review, critic Dana Holt described the novel ___ and urged readers to finish it in a single sitting.","o":["as, \"a house with every window open\"","as \"a house with every window open\"","as: \"a house with every window open\"","as; \"a house with every window open\""],"a":1,"ex":"The quotation completes 'described the novel as', so it is part of the sentence's grammar and takes no punctuation before it.","ex_tr":"Alıntı 'described the novel as' ifadesini tamamlıyor, yani cümlenin bir parçası; önüne noktalama işareti konmaz.","why":["A comma after 'as' separates the word from the phrase it introduces; a quotation woven into the sentence takes no comma.","Correct. The quoted phrase completes 'described the novel as', so it flows into the sentence with no punctuation before it.","A colon must follow a complete sentence, and 'critic Dana Holt described the novel as' is not complete.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and neither 'described the novel as' nor the quoted phrase is one."]},
{"id":"p7r13","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Elephants keep in touch across long distances with low rumbles, many of them too deep for human ears to hear. ___ blue whales use very low-pitched calls that can carry through the ocean to other whales hundreds of kilometers away.","o":["Similarly,","Therefore,","For instance,","However,"],"a":0,"ex":"Both sentences describe animals using very low sounds to communicate over long distances, so the second gives a parallel case: 'Similarly'.","ex_tr":"İki cümle de hayvanların çok alçak seslerle uzak mesafelerden iletişim kurmasını anlatıyor; ikincisi benzer bir durum: Similarly.","why":["Correct. Whales sending low calls across the ocean parallels elephants rumbling across long distances.","'Therefore' signals a result, but the elephants' rumbles do not cause whales to call.","'For instance' introduces an example of the previous claim, but whales are not an example of elephants; they are a second, parallel case.","'However' signals a contrast, but both sentences make the same point: these animals use very low sounds to reach others far away."]},
{"id":"p7r14","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's only ferry stopped running in March after its engine failed, and a replacement part will take months to arrive. ___ commuters on the far side of the lake now drive forty minutes around it to reach their jobs.","o":["Consequently,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,","In other words,"],"a":0,"ex":"The ferry's breakdown (cause) leads commuters to drive around the lake (result): 'Consequently'.","ex_tr":"Feribotun çalışmaması sebep, işe gidip gelenlerin gölün etrafından dolaşması sonuç: Consequently.","why":["Correct. The broken ferry and the slow repair explain why commuters now drive around the lake.","'Similarly' signals a parallel case, but the long drive is a consequence of the ferry stopping, not a similar event.","'Nevertheless' signals something that happens despite the first point, but driving around the lake follows naturally from losing the ferry.","'In other words' restates the previous sentence, but the long drive is a new point, not a rewording."]},
{"id":"p7r15","t":7,"sec":"wr","p":"Linguists describe Turkish as an agglutinative language. ___ it builds words by attaching a chain of suffixes to a root, with each suffix adding one piece of meaning.","o":["By contrast,","Similarly,","Nevertheless,","That is,"],"a":3,"ex":"The second sentence explains what 'agglutinative' means, so it restates the first in plainer words: 'That is'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle 'agglutinative' (eklemeli) teriminin ne demek olduğunu açıklıyor, yani ilk cümleyi başka sözlerle yeniden söylüyor: That is.","why":["'By contrast' signals an opposing idea, but the second sentence agrees with and explains the first.","'Similarly' introduces a second, parallel case, but the second sentence describes the same language, not another one.","'Nevertheless' signals something true despite the first point, but building words from suffixes is exactly what being agglutinative means.","Correct. The second sentence defines 'agglutinative', restating the first sentence in plainer words."]},
/* Practice 8 */
{"id":"p8w1","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"Although every character and event in Mariam Tahir's novel about a traveling circus in the 1890s is invented, reviewers praised the book's ___. Tahir spent two years reading old circus programs, payroll books, and performers' letters so that each detail of life on the road, from the food to the acrobats' slang, would ring true.","o":["anachronism","novelty","verisimilitude","austerity"],"a":2,"ex":"'Although ... invented' sets up a contrast, and the second sentence explains the praise: details researched so that they ring true. The invented story seems real: verisimilitude.","ex_tr":"'Although' bir karşıtlık kuruyor: roman tamamen kurgu, ama yazar her ayrıntı gerçek gibi dursun diye iki yıl eski sirk programlarını, maaş defterlerini ve sanatçıların mektuplarını okumuş. Övülen şey, kitabın gerçekmiş gibi görünmesi: verisimilitude (gerçeklik izlenimi).","why":["An anachronism is something placed in the wrong period, a flaw that two years of reading circus records was meant to prevent, not a quality reviewers would praise.","Novelty means newness. The second sentence explains the praise as details that ring true, which is about seeming real, not about being new.","Correct. Verisimilitude is the quality of seeming real: invented events that feel true because every detail was researched.","Austerity means extreme plainness or strict economy. Nothing describes a plain style; the praise is for convincing detail."]},
{"id":"p8w2","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"For generations, farmers in the river valley chose their planting dates by following old proverbs about the moon. The agronomist Zhang Hui took a more ___ approach, sowing test plots of rice on twelve different dates over six seasons and recording the harvest from each.","o":["haphazard","hypothetical","intuitive","empirical"],"a":3,"ex":"Sowing test plots and recording every harvest is learning by experiment and observation, in contrast with following proverbs: empirical.","ex_tr":"Atasözlerine uymak yerine deneme tarlaları ekip her hasadı kaydetmek, deney ve gözleme dayanan bir yöntemdir: empirical (deneysel, gözleme dayalı).","why":["Haphazard means random and unorganized. Twelve planned dates over six seasons, with every harvest recorded, is a careful system.","Hypothetical means based on speculation rather than fact. Zhang Hui replaced guesswork with measured harvests, the opposite of speculation.","Intuitive means based on instinct. Planting on twelve dates and recording each harvest is deliberate testing, not instinct.","Correct. Empirical means based on observation or experiment, exactly what six seasons of test plots provide."]},
{"id":"p8w3","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"When a storm cut the power halfway through her recital, the pianist Efua Boateng showed remarkable ___. She asked the audience to stay seated, waited while ushers brought candles, and then finished the sonata without missing a note.","o":["perturbation","animosity","jubilation","equanimity"],"a":3,"ex":"Her actions after the blackout (keeping the audience seated, waiting for candles, finishing without a mistake) show that she stayed calm after a sudden shock: equanimity.","ex_tr":"Elektrik kesilince seyirciden yerinde oturmasını istemesi, mumların getirilmesini beklemesi ve sonatı hatasız bitirmesi, ani bir şok karşısında sakin kaldığını gösterir: equanimity (soğukkanlılık).","why":["Perturbation means anxiety or disturbance. A blackout might cause it, but she kept the audience seated and finished without an error, so she showed the opposite.","Animosity is anger or hostility. She politely asked the audience to stay, which shows no anger at anyone.","Jubilation is great happiness, usually over a success. Nothing in the text is a celebration; she is coping with a problem.","Correct. Equanimity is calm after a shock, shown by how steadily she handled the blackout."]},
{"id":"p8w4","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"Until 2015, the island village saw few outsiders. Then a popular film was shot in its harbor, and the resulting ___ of tourists forced the ferry company to add four daily crossings and led residents to rent out their spare rooms.","o":["influx","attrition","dearth","diaspora"],"a":0,"ex":"Extra ferry crossings and rented spare rooms after the film show that large numbers of tourists suddenly arrived: influx.","ex_tr":"Film çekildikten sonra feribot şirketinin günde dört sefer eklemesi ve köylülerin boş odalarını kiraya vermesi, birden çok sayıda turistin geldiğini gösterir: influx (akın).","why":["Correct. An influx is a sudden arrival of large numbers, exactly what the extra ferries and rented rooms point to.","Attrition is a gradual loss through departures. The text describes a sudden rise in visitors after the film, not a decline.","Dearth means a shortage. A shortage of tourists would not force the ferry company to add four daily crossings.","A diaspora is a population scattered from its homeland. Tourists arriving for a visit are not a people dispersed from home."]},
{"id":"p8w5","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"When the factory announced longer night shifts, angry workers threatened to strike. To ___ them, the managers agreed to raise night pay by a fifth and to let each worker choose a schedule, and the strike was called off the next day.","o":["provoke","admonish","coerce","mollify"],"a":3,"ex":"The workers were angry; a pay raise and a free choice of schedule calmed them enough to call off the strike: mollify.","ex_tr":"İşçiler öfkeliydi; gece zammı ve vardiya seçme hakkı onları yatıştırdı ve grev ertesi gün iptal edildi: mollify (öfkesini yatıştırmak).","why":["Provoke means to stir up a reaction. Raising pay and offering choices calmed the workers, and the strike was called off.","Admonish means to tell someone firmly that they have done wrong. The managers made concessions instead of scolding anyone.","Coerce means to force through threats. A pay raise and a free choice of schedule are offers, not threats.","Correct. Mollify means to calm someone's anger, which the raise and the choice of schedules did."]},
{"id":"p8w6","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"Visitors to the town square admire the marble statue from every side, but conservators describe it as ___: one ankle is cracked through, and only a hidden iron rod keeps the figure from toppling.","o":["pristine","precarious","ostentatious","robust"],"a":1,"ex":"'But' sets the experts' view against the admiring visitors, and the colon explains it: with a cracked ankle and only a rod holding it up, the figure could fall. That is precarious.","ex_tr":"'But' ziyaretçilerin hayranlığını uzmanların görüşüyle karşılaştırıyor. İki noktadan sonrası nedenini söylüyor: bileği çatlamış heykeli yalnızca gizli bir demir çubuk ayakta tutuyor, her an devrilebilir: precarious (sağlam durmayan, tehlikede).","why":["Pristine means perfectly clean and unspoiled. A statue with an ankle cracked through is damaged, not unspoiled.","Correct. Precarious means in danger because not firmly held in place, like a figure that could topple.","Ostentatious means showy, made to impress. The conservators are worried about cracks, not about display.","Robust means strong and sturdy, the opposite of a figure that only a hidden rod keeps upright."]},
{"id":"p8w7","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"After years of debate, the island's language council approved a single standard spelling for the local language in 1962. To ___ the new rules, it printed them in every newspaper, mailed a booklet to every school, and broadcast a weekly radio lesson about them.","o":["annotate","rescind","disavow","promulgate"],"a":3,"ex":"Printing the rules in newspapers, mailing booklets and broadcasting lessons all spread the new rules to the public: promulgate.","ex_tr":"Kuralları her gazetede yayımlamak, her okula kitapçık göndermek ve radyoda haftalık ders yapmak, kuralları halka resmen duyurup yaymaktır: promulgate (resmen ilan etmek, yaymak).","why":["Annotate means to add notes to a text. Newspapers, booklets and radio lessons carry the rules to the public; nothing describes adding comments to them.","Rescind means to cancel a rule. The council had just approved the spelling and was spreading it, not canceling it.","Disavow means to deny any connection with something. The council is promoting its own rules, not distancing itself from them.","Correct. Promulgate means to spread or officially announce a rule, which newspapers, booklets and radio lessons do."]},
{"id":"p8w8","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"Engineers had worried that the soft clay beneath the river would ___ the tunnel project, and their worry proved justified; the boring machine kept sinking, and progress slowed to a few meters a week.","o":["facilitate","reinvigorate","stymie","delineate"],"a":2,"ex":"The worry 'proved justified' because the machine kept sinking and progress slowed to a crawl, so the clay held the project back: stymie.","ex_tr":"Endişe haklı çıktı: tünel açma makinesi sürekli batıyordu ve ilerleme haftada birkaç metreye düştü. Yani kil projenin önünü tıkadı: stymie (engellemek).","why":["Facilitate means to make easier. No one worries that clay will make work easier, and progress in fact slowed.","Reinvigorate means to give new energy. Slowing to a few meters a week is the opposite of new energy.","Correct. Stymie means to block or hinder progress, which a sinking machine and a few meters a week show.","Delineate means to describe precisely or mark an outline. Clay cannot describe or outline a project; the text is about it slowing the work."]},
{"id":"p8w9","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"For years, astronomers could find no reason why a distant star dimmed like clockwork every 300 days, and its behavior seemed ___. The puzzle was solved only when a new telescope revealed a cloud of dust orbiting the star and blocking part of its light on each pass.","o":["intermittent","inexplicable","ephemeral","inconsequential"],"a":1,"ex":"'Could find no reason' and 'the puzzle was solved' show that the dimming seemed impossible to explain until the dust cloud was found: inexplicable.","ex_tr":"'Hiçbir neden bulamadılar' ve 'gizem çözüldü' ifadeleri, yıldızın ışığının azalmasının o zamana kadar açıklanamaz göründüğünü gösterir: inexplicable (açıklanamaz).","why":["Intermittent means occurring at irregular intervals. The dimming came like clockwork every 300 days, and the point of the text is the missing reason, not the timing.","Correct. Inexplicable means impossible to explain: astronomers had no reason for the dimming until the dust cloud was found.","Ephemeral means lasting a very short time. The behavior went on for years.","Inconsequential means unimportant. The text calls the dimming a puzzle that needed solving, not something trivial."]},
{"id":"p8w10","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"A survey of the city found a striking ___ in life expectancy between two neighborhoods only two kilometers apart, with residents of the hillside district living eleven years longer, on average, than those near the port.","o":["parity","affinity","disparity","symbiosis"],"a":2,"ex":"An eleven-year gap in life expectancy between two neighborhoods is an inequality: disparity.","ex_tr":"Birbirine iki kilometre uzaklıktaki iki mahalle arasındaki on bir yıllık ortalama yaşam süresi farkı bir eşitsizliktir: disparity (eşitsizlik, uçurum).","why":["Parity means equality. An eleven-year gap is the opposite of equal.","Affinity is a natural liking or close connection. The survey reports a difference in numbers, not a bond between the neighborhoods.","Correct. Disparity means inequality: hillside residents live eleven years longer than those near the port.","Symbiosis is a relationship in which two organisms help each other. Life expectancy figures describe a gap, not a partnership."]},
{"id":"p8w11","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"For her exhibition, the photographer Paloma Ferrer chose to ___ portraits of textile workers taken in 1955 with new portraits of their grandchildren, hanging each pair side by side so that the changes in clothing, posture, and surroundings would stand out.","o":["conflate","juxtapose","efface","embellish"],"a":1,"ex":"Hanging each pair side by side so that the differences stand out is placing things together for contrast: juxtapose.","ex_tr":"Her çifti yan yana asıp kıyafet, duruş ve ortamdaki değişikliklerin göze çarpmasını sağlamak, karşıtlığı göstermek için yan yana koymaktır: juxtapose (karşılaştırmak için yan yana koymak).","why":["Conflate means to treat two things as the same by mistake. Ferrer wants viewers to see the differences, not to blur the two sets together.","Correct. Juxtapose means to place things side by side for contrast, exactly how the pairs are hung.","Efface means to erase. Hanging the portraits on a wall displays them; it does not wipe them out.","Embellish means to decorate or exaggerate. Hanging old and new portraits next to each other adds no decoration; it invites comparison."]},
{"id":"p8w12","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"As a student, the architect Selin Arslan filled her sketchbooks with floating towers and glass bridges, but her designs for a flood-prone river town are thoroughly ___; each house stands on concrete stilts, uses local brick, and can be repaired by residents with ordinary tools.","o":["pragmatic","abstract","profligate","equivocal"],"a":0,"ex":"'But' sets her dreamlike sketches against houses built for real conditions: stilts against floods, local brick, easy repairs. The design is practical: pragmatic.","ex_tr":"'But' hayalî çizimlerle gerçek koşullar için tasarlanmış evleri karşılaştırıyor: sele karşı beton ayaklar, yerel tuğla, kolay onarım. Tasarım uygulamaya dönük: pragmatic (pratik, gerçekçi).","why":["Correct. Pragmatic means practical rather than idealistic: stilts for floods, local brick and easy repairs.","Abstract means theoretical, existing only as an idea. That fits her student sketches, but 'but' sets the town designs against them, and those are for real, buildable houses.","Profligate means recklessly wasteful. Local brick and repairs with ordinary tools point to economy, not waste.","Equivocal means open to more than one interpretation. Nothing about the houses is unclear; the text lists their practical features."]},
{"id":"p8w13","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"Opening a small bookshop in the city's old quarter involved such ___ paperwork that Elif Tunca spent five months collecting eleven permits from seven different offices, several of which would accept a form only after another office had stamped it.","o":["rudimentary","efficient","byzantine","succinct"],"a":2,"ex":"Five months, eleven permits from seven offices, and offices that wait for one another's stamps describe paperwork that is complicated and hard to follow: byzantine.","ex_tr":"Beş ay süren uğraş, yedi ayrı kurumdan alınan on bir izin ve bir formu ancak başka bir kurum damgaladıktan sonra kabul eden daireler, evrak işinin çok karmaşık olduğunu gösterir: byzantine (karmaşık, içinden çıkılmaz).","why":["Rudimentary means basic. Eleven permits from seven offices, collected over five months, is far more than a basic process.","Efficient means achieving results with little waste. Five months of chasing stamps from office to office is the opposite of efficient.","Correct. Byzantine means complicated and difficult to understand, like eleven permits from seven offices that wait on one another's stamps.","Succinct means brief and clearly expressed. Eleven permits and five months of work make the paperwork long and tangled, not brief."]},
{"id":"p8w14","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"The hand-cranked adding machine that the clockmaker Greta Vogel built in 1874 could do nothing but add, and it jammed often. Historians nonetheless see it as a ___ of the office calculators that her family's firm sold in the 1920s, because those later machines used the same system of toothed wheels that she had designed.","o":["forerunner","successor","counterpart","relic"],"a":0,"ex":"Her 1874 machine was older and simpler, yet the calculators her family's firm sold in the 1920s used its wheel system, so it was an early, less advanced model of them: forerunner.","ex_tr":"1874'te yapılan makine hem daha eski hem daha basitti, ama aile şirketinin 1920'lerde sattığı hesap makineleri onun dişli çark sistemini kullandı. Yani o, bu makinelerin ilk ve daha ilkel örneğiydi: forerunner (öncü, ilk örnek).","why":["Correct. A forerunner is an early, less advanced model of what comes later: a simple, jam-prone machine whose wheel system the firm's later calculators used.","A successor comes after something and replaces it. Vogel's machine came about fifty years before the firm's 1920s calculators, not after them.","A counterpart is an equivalent of something else. A machine that could only add and jammed often was not the equal of the firm's later calculators; it came before them.","A relic is an object surviving from an earlier time. It cannot be a relic of the 1920s calculators, since it was built decades before them."]},
{"id":"p8w15","t":8,"sec":"wic","p":"In one species of reef fish, small males almost never win fights over nesting sites. Instead, they rely on ___ tactics, slipping into a larger male's nest to fertilize eggs while its owner is busy chasing other rivals away.","o":["surreptitious","ferocious","benevolent","conspicuous"],"a":0,"ex":"'Instead' of fighting, the small males slip in while the owner is distracted, acting secretly so as not to be noticed: surreptitious.","ex_tr":"Küçük erkekler kavga etmek yerine ('Instead'), yuvanın sahibi başka rakipleri kovalarken gizlice yuvaya süzülüyor: surreptitious (gizlice, fark ettirmeden yapılan).","why":["Correct. Surreptitious means done secretly to avoid notice, like slipping into a nest while its owner is busy.","Ferocious means violent. 'Instead' signals that the small males avoid fights rather than win them by force.","Benevolent means kind and wanting to help others. Fertilizing eggs in a rival's nest while he is busy is not kindness toward him.","Conspicuous means easily noticed. The small males succeed by slipping in while the owner is distracted, which depends on not being noticed."]},
{"id":"p8r1","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Engineers designed the bridge to carry 20,000 cars a ___ by its third year, it was carrying nearly twice that number.","o":["day; however,","day, however,","day however,","day, however"],"a":0,"ex":"Two complete sentences linked by 'however' need a semicolon before it and a comma after it.","ex_tr":"'However' bağlaç değildir; iki tam cümleyi tek başına bağlayamaz. Önüne noktalı virgül, arkasına virgül konur.","why":["Correct. Both halves are complete sentences; the semicolon joins them, and the comma after 'however' sets off the transition.","With only commas, the two complete sentences form a comma splice; 'however' is not a joining word.","With no punctuation before 'however', the two complete sentences run together.","A comma before 'however' still makes a comma splice, and the comma after 'however' is missing too."]},
{"id":"p8r2","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The village's apple harvest fell by half last ___ a hailstorm in June knocked most of the young fruit from the trees.","o":["year","year,","year:","year: and"],"a":2,"ex":"The second clause explains why the harvest fell. After a complete sentence, a colon can introduce a clause that explains it.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle hasadın neden yarıya düştüğünü açıklıyor. Tam bir cümleden sonra gelen iki nokta, onu açıklayan ikinci bir cümleyi başlatabilir.","why":["With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","Correct. The first part is a complete sentence, and the colon introduces the clause that explains it: the hailstorm.","A colon cannot be followed by 'and' to join two clauses; 'and' joins clauses after a comma, not after a colon."]},
{"id":"p8r3","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The festival's opening ___ follows three generations of one family as they run a small tea house in Hangzhou.","o":["film a documentary shot over eight years,","film, a documentary shot over eight years","film, a documentary shot over eight years,","film; a documentary shot over eight years,"],"a":2,"ex":"'A documentary shot over eight years' renames the film and is extra information, so it needs a comma on both sides.","ex_tr":"'a documentary shot over eight years' filmi tanımlayan ek bilgidir (appositive); iki virgül arasına alınır.","why":["The extra information is closed with a comma but never opened.","The extra information is opened with a comma but never closed, so it runs straight into the verb 'follows'.","Correct. The extra information about the film is opened and closed by a pair of commas.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'The festival's opening film' is not one."]},
{"id":"p8r4","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The volunteers who counted migrating hawks from the ridge last ___ more than 40,000 birds in a single month.","o":["autumn: recorded","autumn, recorded","autumn; recorded","autumn recorded"],"a":3,"ex":"The long subject 'The volunteers who counted ... last autumn' leads straight into its verb 'recorded'; no punctuation goes between a subject and its verb.","ex_tr":"Özne ('The volunteers who counted ... last autumn') ne kadar uzun olursa olsun, yüklemle ('recorded') arasına noktalama işareti konmaz.","why":["A colon must follow a complete sentence, and 'The volunteers who counted ... last autumn' is not one.","A single comma between a subject and its verb is an error, however long the subject is.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences; here it cuts the subject off from its verb.","Correct. Nothing separates the long subject from its verb, 'recorded'."]},
{"id":"p8r5","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The array of solar panels on the roofs of the school's three oldest buildings ___ enough electricity to power the whole campus on sunny days.","o":["produces","produce","are producing","have produced"],"a":0,"ex":"Cross out 'of solar panels on the roofs of the school's three oldest buildings': the subject is 'array', singular, so 'produces'.","ex_tr":"Araya giren öbeği ('of solar panels ... buildings') çıkar: özne 'array', tekil: produces.","why":["Correct. The subject is 'array', which is singular, so the verb is 'produces'.","'Produce' is plural; it agrees with 'panels', 'roofs' or 'buildings', but those sit inside the phrase that describes the array.","'Are producing' is plural and cannot agree with the singular subject 'array'.","'Have produced' is plural and cannot agree with the singular subject 'array'."]},
{"id":"p8r6","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Lina and I had joined the debate club only that autumn. At the end of the regional tournament, the judges gave the award for best rebuttal to ___, the only first-year students in the final round.","o":["her and me","she and I","her and I","she and me"],"a":0,"ex":"After the preposition 'to', both pronouns are objects: 'her and me'. Test each one alone: 'to her', 'to me'.","ex_tr":"'to' edatından sonra iki zamir de nesne hâlinde olur: her and me. Tek tek dene: 'to her', 'to me'.","why":["Correct. Both pronouns follow 'to', so both take the object form: 'to her' and 'to me'.","'She' and 'I' are subject forms; you would not write 'gave the award to she' or 'to I'.","'Her' is right, but 'I' is a subject form; you would not write 'gave the award to I'.","'Me' is right, but 'she' is a subject form; you would not write 'gave the award to she'."]},
{"id":"p8r7","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"In 1887, workers ___ the final section of the mountain railway, linking the two valleys by rail for the first time.","o":["complete","have completed","completed","will complete"],"a":2,"ex":"A specific past date, 'In 1887', calls for the simple past: 'completed'.","ex_tr":"Belirli bir geçmiş tarih ('In 1887') basit geçmiş zaman (simple past) ister: completed.","why":["The present tense does not fit an action completed in 1887.","The present perfect does not go with a specific past date such as 'In 1887'.","Correct. A finished action at a stated past time takes the simple past.","The future tense cannot describe an action that took place in 1887."]},
{"id":"p8r8","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Many students do their homework at the community center on Elm Street, mainly because ___ open until midnight and its study rooms stay quiet even during exam weeks.","o":["it","its","its'","it's"],"a":3,"ex":"The blank needs a subject and a verb: 'it is', shortened to 'it's'. 'Its' without an apostrophe shows possession.","ex_tr":"Boşluğa özne ve fiil gelmeli: 'it is', kısaca 'it's'. Kesme işaretsiz 'its' iyelik bildirir ('its study rooms').","why":["'It' supplies a subject but no verb, so 'because it open until midnight' is ungrammatical.","'Its' is a possessive, like 'its study rooms' later in the sentence; 'because its open' has no verb.","'Its'' is not a real English form.","Correct. 'It's' means 'it is', giving the clause its subject and verb: 'because it is open until midnight'."]},
{"id":"p8r9","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Carpenters joined the temple's beams with interlocking wooden joints that can shift slightly during an earthquake. Built entirely without nails, ___","o":["survival through three major earthquakes has been achieved by the temple's wooden frame.","three major earthquakes have not destroyed the temple's wooden frame.","the builders of the temple created a wooden frame that has survived three major earthquakes.","the temple's wooden frame has survived three major earthquakes."],"a":3,"ex":"An opening modifier describes whatever comes right after the comma. The frame is what was 'built entirely without nails', so the frame must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade ('Built entirely without nails') virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi niteler. Çivisiz yapılan şey çerçevedir: 'the temple's wooden frame'.","why":["This puts 'survival' right after the comma, saying the survival was built without nails.","This puts 'three major earthquakes' right after the comma, saying the earthquakes were built without nails.","This puts 'the builders' right after the comma, saying the builders were built without nails.","Correct. The frame is what was built without nails, and it comes right after the comma."]},
{"id":"p8r10","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Each spring, volunteers wade into the marsh ___ the eggs of a rare tree frog so that biologists can track how the population changes from year to year.","o":["counts","count","to count","they count"],"a":2,"ex":"The sentence already has its verb, 'wade'. 'To count' adds the purpose of wading in without adding a second main verb.","ex_tr":"Cümlenin yüklemi zaten var: 'wade'. 'To count' ikinci bir yüklem kurmadan amacı bildirir (saymak için).","why":["'Counts' is a singular main verb with no subject or joining word, so the sentence falls apart.","'Count' creates a second main verb with no 'and' to join it to 'wade'.","Correct. 'To count' is an infinitive that tells why the volunteers wade in; it does not need its own subject.","'They count' starts a new complete sentence with no punctuation or joining word: a run-on."]},
{"id":"p8r11","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"During the restoration of the old theater, workers removed the cracked plaster, repaired the roof beams, and ___ the ceiling in its original shade of blue.","o":["repainted","repainting","to repaint","the repainting of"],"a":0,"ex":"Items in a series must match: removed ..., repaired ..., and repainted ....","ex_tr":"Listedeki öğeler aynı yapıda olmalı: removed ..., repaired ..., and repainted ....","why":["Correct. 'Repainted' matches the other past-tense verbs, 'removed' and 'repaired'.","'Repainting' is an -ing form that breaks the pattern of past-tense verbs.","'To repaint' is an infinitive that breaks the pattern of past-tense verbs.","'The repainting of' is a noun phrase, not a verb like 'removed' and 'repaired'."]},
{"id":"p8r12","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Each June, the town's music festival takes place in Brannock ___ was built in 1790 as a wool merchant's home.","o":["Hall which","Hall, which","Hall; which","Hall. Which"],"a":1,"ex":"'Which was built in 1790 ...' adds extra information about a named building, so a comma sets it off.","ex_tr":"'which was built in 1790 ...' adı belli bir bina hakkında ek bilgi verir; önüne virgül konur.","why":["A 'which' clause that adds information about a named place must be set off with a comma.","Correct. The name already identifies the hall, so the 'which' clause is extra information and takes a comma before it.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'which was built in 1790 ...' cannot stand alone.","A period leaves 'Which was built in 1790 ...' as a fragment with no main clause."]},
{"id":"p8r13","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The new coastal hiking trail proved popular from its first week. ___ rangers counted nearly 60,000 walkers on it during its first summer, more than on all of the park's other trails combined.","o":["Previously,","However,","Similarly,","In fact,"],"a":3,"ex":"The second sentence makes the first claim much stronger (not just popular, but busier than all the other trails together), so it needs an intensifier: 'In fact'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle ilk iddiayı güçlendiriyor: yürüyüş yolu sadece sevilmekle kalmamış, ilk yazında parktaki diğer bütün yolların toplamından daha çok yürüyüşçü çekmiş. Vurgu bildiren geçiş: In fact.","why":["'Previously' points to an earlier time, but the count comes from the trail's first summer, after it opened.","'However' signals a contrast, but nearly 60,000 walkers agrees with the claim that the trail is popular.","'Similarly' introduces a parallel case of something else, but the second sentence is about the same trail and makes the same point more strongly.","Correct. 'In fact' introduces a stronger version of the first claim: not just popular, but busier than all the park's other trails combined."]},
{"id":"p8r14","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"Cast-iron pans hold heat for a long time, which helps meat brown evenly instead of turning gray. ___ they can last for generations; many cooks still use pans that once belonged to their grandparents.","o":["Nevertheless,","Moreover,","For instance,","In other words,"],"a":1,"ex":"The second sentence adds a second, separate advantage of cast-iron pans, so 'Moreover'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle, döküm tavaların ayrı bir avantajını daha ekliyor: Moreover.","why":["'Nevertheless' signals something that holds despite the first sentence, but both sentences describe advantages.","Correct. Lasting for generations is a second advantage added to holding heat well.","'For instance' introduces an example of the first claim, but lasting for generations is not an example of holding heat.","'In other words' restates the first sentence, but the second describes a different advantage."]},
{"id":"p8r15","t":8,"sec":"wr","p":"The composer Ayla Demir finished an opera in 1931 but put the score away when the theater that had ordered it closed. ___ in 1958, her daughter found the score in an attic and arranged the opera's first performance.","o":["Previously,","Later,","Meanwhile,","Similarly,"],"a":1,"ex":"The daughter's discovery in 1958 comes after the events of 1931, so the transition must move forward in time: 'Later'.","ex_tr":"Kızının 1958'deki keşfi 1931'deki olaylardan sonra geliyor; zamanda ileri giden bir geçiş gerekir: Later.","why":["'Previously' points to an earlier time, but 1958 comes after 1931.","Correct. 'Later' moves the story forward from 1931 to 1958.","'Meanwhile' means at the same time, but the discovery happened decades after the score was put away.","'Similarly' signals a comparison, but the second sentence continues the story rather than describing a similar event."]},
/* Practice 9 */
{"id":"p9w1","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Across most of the valley, the rock layers record a slow, steady buildup of river mud over thousands of years. One thin band near the top is ___, however; it contains volcanic glass and sea shells found nowhere else in the sequence, so the geologists are studying it on its own.","o":["anomalous","ubiquitous","dormant","superfluous"],"a":0,"ex":"'However' sets the thin band against the steady pattern, and its contents are 'found nowhere else in the sequence': it departs from the norm, so anomalous.","ex_tr":"'However' bu ince katmanı vadideki düzenli yapıyla karşılaştırıyor; içindekiler dizinin başka hiçbir yerinde bulunmuyor. Katman olağan düzenden sapıyor: anomalous (sıra dışı, alışılmışın dışında).","why":["Correct. Anomalous means deviating from the norm: unlike the steady layers of river mud, this band holds material found nowhere else.","Ubiquitous means seeming to be everywhere, but the band's contents are found nowhere else in the sequence.","Dormant means temporarily inactive. The volcanic glass may suggest it, but a rock layer is not resting; the point is that this band differs from every other layer.","Superfluous means more than is needed. The geologists think the band is worth studying on its own, and nothing in the text calls it unnecessary."]},
{"id":"p9w2","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"When the harbor town of Harrowmere opened its public library in 1791, lending books to anyone who could sign the register was ___ in the region; no earlier library there had let ordinary residents carry books home, and within a decade three neighboring towns had copied the idea.","o":["prevalent","obsolete","inconsequential","unprecedented"],"a":3,"ex":"'No earlier library there had let ordinary residents carry books home' says the practice had never been tried in the region before: unprecedented.","ex_tr":"'No earlier library there had let ordinary residents carry books home': bölgede daha önce hiçbir kütüphane bunu yapmamıştı. Uygulama daha önce hiç görülmemişti: unprecedented (eşi benzeri görülmemiş).","why":["Prevalent means widespread. No earlier library in the region lent books this way, so the practice could not have been common there.","Obsolete means outdated. The practice was brand new in 1791, and other towns were copying it, not abandoning it.","Inconsequential means unimportant. Three neighboring towns copied the idea within a decade, which shows it mattered.","Correct. Unprecedented means never done or known before, and no earlier library in the region had lent books to ordinary residents."]},
{"id":"p9w3","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"In Zhou Lan's novel, the ___ innkeeper cannot let a guest pass without a long story about the weather, his cousins, or the price of eggs, and travelers soon learn to leave by the back door.","o":["taciturn","garrulous","sullen","parsimonious"],"a":1,"ex":"Endless stories about the weather, cousins and egg prices describe someone who talks far too much about small things: garrulous.","ex_tr":"Hava durumu, kuzenleri ve yumurta fiyatları hakkında bitmeyen hikâyeler, önemsiz konularda durmadan konuşan birini anlatıyor: garrulous (geveze, çenesi düşük).","why":["Taciturn means habitually quiet, the opposite of a man who stops every guest for a long story.","Correct. Garrulous means excessively talkative about trivial matters, like the weather, his cousins and the price of eggs.","Sullen means silently ill-tempered. The innkeeper is not silent; travelers slip away because he talks too much.","Parsimonious means stingy with money. The price of eggs is only one of his topics of talk; nothing shows him refusing to spend."]},
{"id":"p9w4","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Most scholars of his era wrote only in Latin, but Johan Aldersen published his beekeeping manual in the ___ of the farming villages, so that readers who had never studied Latin could follow his advice.","o":["vernacular","anthology","rhetoric","typography"],"a":0,"ex":"'Only in Latin, but' sets up a contrast, and readers who never studied Latin needed the language they spoke every day: vernacular.","ex_tr":"'Only in Latin, but' bir karşıtlık kuruyor. Aldersen, Latince öğrenmemiş okurlar da anlayabilsin diye kitabını köylülerin her gün konuştuğu dilde yayımladı: vernacular (halk dili, yerel dil).","why":["Correct. The vernacular is the everyday language of a group, which readers who had never studied Latin could understand.","An anthology is a collection of works. A single manual is not a collection, and the contrast with Latin calls for a language.","Rhetoric is the art of persuasive speaking or writing. The contrast with Latin calls for a language, not a style of persuasion.","Typography is the style of printed letters. A new typeface would not help readers who could not understand Latin; the choice was about language."]},
{"id":"p9w5","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Because the island's farmers depend on a single export crop, vanilla, their incomes are ___: a new supplier abroad can halve the price within a season, while a poor harvest elsewhere can double it.","o":["volatile","durable","static","prohibitive"],"a":0,"ex":"The colon gives the evidence: the price can halve or double within a season, so incomes change quickly and unpredictably: volatile.","ex_tr":"İki noktadan sonrası kanıtı veriyor: fiyat bir mevsimde yarıya inebiliyor ya da iki katına çıkabiliyor. Gelir hızla ve öngörülemez biçimde değişiyor: volatile (oynak, istikrarsız).","why":["Correct. Volatile means liable to change rapidly and unpredictably, as a price that halves or doubles within a season does.","Durable means sturdy and lasting. Incomes that can be cut in half within a single season are anything but sturdy.","Static means lacking change, the opposite of a price that can halve or double in a season.","Prohibitive means so costly that it prevents something. The text is about incomes swinging up and down, not about a cost too high to pay."]},
{"id":"p9w6","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"The architect Mei Chen is famous for glass towers that dominate city skylines, yet her own house is surprisingly ___: a single-story wooden building, painted grey, that most neighbors walk past without a second glance.","o":["ostentatious","lofty","conspicuous","unassuming"],"a":3,"ex":"'Yet ... surprisingly' contrasts her towering designs with her home, and the colon describes a plain building nobody notices: unassuming.","ex_tr":"'Yet ... surprisingly' şehrin siluetine hâkim kulelerle mimarın kendi evini karşılaştırıyor; iki noktadan sonrası kimsenin dönüp bakmadığı sade bir evi anlatıyor: unassuming (gösterişsiz, mütevazı).","why":["Ostentatious means showy and meant to impress. A plain grey house that neighbors ignore is the opposite.","Lofty means very high. Her towers are tall, but the house is a single story, and 'yet' signals a contrast with the towers.","Conspicuous means easily noticed, but most neighbors walk past the house without a second glance.","Correct. Unassuming means modest and not showy, like a small grey wooden house that draws no attention."]},
{"id":"p9w7","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"When the town of Pellridge installed its first electric streetlamps in 1902, the lamplighters insisted that the new lights would never ___ gas lamps, which were cheaper to run. Within a decade, however, every gas lamp in town had been taken down and an electric one put up in its place.","o":["augment","supplant","replenish","adorn"],"a":1,"ex":"'However' shows the prediction failed: every gas lamp was taken down and an electric one put up 'in its place'. The new lights replaced the old: supplant.","ex_tr":"'However' tahminin tutmadığını gösteriyor: on yıl içinde her gaz lambası söküldü ve yerine elektrikli lamba kondu. Yeni lambalar eskilerin yerini aldı: supplant (yerini almak).","why":["Augment means to make greater or add to. The gas lamps were taken down, not added to, so the failed prediction was about replacement.","Correct. Supplant means to replace, and within a decade an electric lamp stood in the place of every gas lamp.","Replenish means to fill up again. Electric lights do not refill gas lamps; they took their place.","Adorn means to decorate. The text is about one kind of lamp replacing another, not about decoration."]},
{"id":"p9w8","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Asked about the resort planned for their bay, many residents of the fishing village said they felt ___, welcoming the jobs it would bring while fearing that its crowds would drive away the quiet their families had valued for generations.","o":["indifferent","sanguine","skeptical","ambivalent"],"a":3,"ex":"The residents hold two opposite feelings at once, welcoming the jobs but fearing the crowds: ambivalent.","ex_tr":"Köy sakinleri aynı anda iki zıt duygu taşıyor: işleri memnuniyetle karşılıyorlar ama kalabalıktan korkuyorlar: ambivalent (iki zıt duygu arasında kalmış).","why":["Indifferent means lacking interest or concern. The residents care a great deal: they welcome the jobs and fear the crowds.","Sanguine means optimistic. It fits their welcome for the jobs but ignores their fear of the crowds.","Skeptical means doubtful. It fits their fear of the crowds but not their welcome for the jobs.","Correct. Ambivalent means having two opposing feelings at the same time, here welcome for the jobs and fear of the crowds."]},
{"id":"p9w9","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"In the late 1800s, doctors often sent patients with lung disease to clinics in mountain villages. Unlike the crowded, smoky cities the patients came from, these villages were considered ___, with cool, clean air that was thought to help the body heal.","o":["noxious","salubrious","squalid","stifling"],"a":1,"ex":"'Unlike' sets the villages against the smoky cities, and cool, clean air that helps the body heal describes a healthy place: salubrious.","ex_tr":"'Unlike' köyleri kalabalık ve dumanlı şehirlerle karşılaştırıyor; iyileşmeye yardım eden serin ve temiz hava sağlıklı bir yeri anlatıyor: salubrious (sağlığa yararlı, temiz ve sağlıklı).","why":["Noxious means harmful. That describes the smoky cities, and 'unlike' sets the villages against them.","Correct. A salubrious place is clean and healthy to live in, as villages with cool, clean, healing air were thought to be.","Squalid means dirty and run-down. The villages are valued for their clean air, so this describes the opposite.","Stifling means extremely hot. The villages had cool air, and they were chosen to help patients heal."]},
{"id":"p9w10","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"The astronomer Farid Haddad's ___ ideas kept him out of the observatory's inner circle for years; while nearly all his colleagues dismissed the faint smudge in their photographs as a flaw in the lens, he argued that it was a galaxy far beyond our own.","o":["orthodox","antiquated","heterodox","lucid"],"a":2,"ex":"The second clause explains why he was kept out: he rejected what nearly all his colleagues believed, so his ideas were unconventional: heterodox.","ex_tr":"Noktalı virgülden sonrası neden dışlandığını açıklıyor: neredeyse bütün meslektaşlarının görüşüne karşı çıkmıştı. Fikirleri alışılmışın dışındaydı: heterodox (aykırı, alışılmadık).","why":["Orthodox means conventional. His ideas went against what nearly all his colleagues held, the opposite of orthodox.","Antiquated means outdated. The established view belonged to his colleagues; his idea was the new one.","Correct. Heterodox means unconventional: he rejected a view held by nearly all his colleagues.","Lucid means clear and easy to understand. Clarity would not keep him out of the inner circle; the text blames his disagreement with the majority."]},
{"id":"p9w11","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Although camera traps in the reserve have photographed nearly every mammal living there, the small striped cat that local guides call the 'shadow cat' remains ___; in ten years of surveys, researchers have recorded it only twice.","o":["ferocious","docile","elusive","rampant"],"a":2,"ex":"'Although' contrasts the many animals photographed with this cat, recorded only twice in ten years: it is hard to find, so elusive.","ex_tr":"'Although' iki durumu karşılaştırıyor: neredeyse her memeli fotoğraflanmış, bu kedi ise on yılda yalnızca iki kez kaydedilmiş. Kedi zor bulunuyor: elusive (bulunması, yakalanması zor).","why":["Ferocious means frightening and violent. The text says nothing about how the cat behaves, only how rarely it has been found.","Docile means easy to control. The contrast with nearly every mammal being photographed is about being found, not about temperament.","Correct. Elusive means difficult to find: in ten years of surveys the cat has been recorded only twice.","Rampant means spreading unchecked. A cat recorded only twice in ten years is the opposite of widespread."]},
{"id":"p9w12","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"To draw more families during the quiet winter months, the national park will ___ its usual entrance fee on weekends, so that visitors pay nothing to enter.","o":["defer","stipulate","enact","waive"],"a":3,"ex":"'Visitors pay nothing to enter' explains the blank: the park gives up a fee it normally has the right to charge, so waive.","ex_tr":"'Visitors pay nothing to enter' (ziyaretçiler giriş için hiç ödeme yapmıyor) boşluğu açıklıyor: park normalde aldığı giriş ücretinden vazgeçiyor: waive (bir hakkından vazgeçmek, talep etmemek).","why":["Defer means to delay. A delayed fee would still be paid later, but visitors pay nothing at all.","Stipulate means to specify or require. Requiring the fee is the opposite of letting visitors pay nothing.","Enact means to make a proposal into law. The park is dropping a fee, not making one official.","Correct. Waive means to give up something you have a right to, here the fee the park usually charges."]},
{"id":"p9w13","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"For years, the company's managers were ___ about its lead in the market, assuming that customers would always prefer its phones; they paid little attention to rivals until a cheaper competitor took a third of their sales in a single year.","o":["complacent","vigilant","reticent","despondent"],"a":0,"ex":"The managers assumed their lead was safe and ignored rivals until it was too late, which is self-satisfied unconcern: complacent.","ex_tr":"Yöneticiler pazardaki liderliklerini garanti sandılar ve iş işten geçene kadar rakiplerle ilgilenmediler. Bu, kendinden memnun bir rehavettir: complacent (rehavete kapılmış).","why":["Correct. Complacent means self-satisfied and unconcerned: they assumed customers would always stay and ignored rivals.","Vigilant means watchful for danger. The managers paid little attention to rivals, the opposite of watchful.","Reticent means reluctant to speak. The problem in the text is what the managers assumed and ignored, not how much they said.","Despondent means hopeless. The managers were confident their lead was safe, not in low spirits."]},
{"id":"p9w14","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Even critics who had dismissed Ana Ruiz's early symphonies as noisy and shapeless were quick to ___ her final work, calling its slow movement one of the most moving pieces of the decade.","o":["deride","laud","abridge","emulate"],"a":1,"ex":"Calling the slow movement 'one of the most moving pieces of the decade' is high praise, a turn from the critics' earlier dismissal: laud.","ex_tr":"Yavaş bölüm için 'on yılın en etkileyici eserlerinden biri' demek büyük bir övgüdür; eleştirmenler önceki küçümseyici tavırlarını bırakmış: laud (övmek, göklere çıkarmak).","why":["Deride means to mock. That matches how the critics treated her early symphonies, but they call the final work deeply moving.","Correct. Laud means to praise highly, as calling the slow movement one of the decade's most moving pieces does.","Abridge means to shorten. Nothing in the text is about cutting the work; the critics are praising it.","Emulate means to copy someone's achievement. The critics are judging her work, not trying to compose like her."]},
{"id":"p9w15","t":9,"sec":"wic","p":"Each dandelion seed hangs from a tiny parachute of fine hairs. When the wind catches these parachutes, the seeds can drift far from the parent plant, so a single dandelion is able to ___ its offspring across a whole field rather than crowding them into one small patch.","o":["confine","cultivate","disperse","consolidate"],"a":2,"ex":"Seeds that drift far on the wind, 'rather than crowding them into one small patch', are spread over a wide area: disperse.","ex_tr":"Rüzgârla uzaklara sürüklenen tohumlar 'tek bir küçük alana yığılmak yerine' bütün tarlaya yayılıyor: disperse (geniş bir alana yaymak, dağıtmak).","why":["Confine means to limit. Keeping the seeds in one place is the 'crowding into one small patch' that the text rules out.","Cultivate means to grow crops. A plant does not farm its seeds; the wind carries them away.","Correct. Disperse means to spread over a wide area, as wind-borne seeds spread across a whole field.","Consolidate means to combine and make stronger. The seeds are scattered apart, not gathered together."]},
{"id":"p9r1","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"The volunteers planted two hundred oak seedlings along the riverbank last ___ they hope the young trees will one day keep the bank from washing away.","o":["spring, which","spring,","spring","spring;"],"a":3,"ex":"Both halves are complete sentences ('The volunteers planted ...' and 'they hope ...'), so join them with a semicolon; a comma alone makes a comma splice.","ex_tr":"İki taraf da tam cümle ('The volunteers planted ...' ve 'they hope ...'), bu yüzden noktalı virgülle bağlanır. Yalnızca virgül kullanmak 'comma splice' hatası olur.","why":["'Which' needs a role in its clause, but 'they hope the young trees will ...' is already complete, so the sentence falls apart.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice; 'they' begins a new sentence.","With no punctuation, the two complete sentences run together.","Correct. A semicolon joins two complete sentences, and 'they hope the young trees will ...' is a complete sentence."]},
{"id":"p9r2","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"The restored observatory now offers visitors three ___ a solar telescope, a planetarium dome, and a garden laid out as a giant sundial.","o":["attractions","attractions;","attractions, which are:","attractions:"],"a":3,"ex":"'The restored observatory now offers visitors three attractions' is a complete sentence, so a colon can introduce the list that follows.","ex_tr":"'The restored observatory now offers visitors three attractions' tam bir cümle; ardından gelen listeyi iki nokta tanıtır.","why":["Without a mark, 'three attractions a solar telescope' jams two noun phrases together; a colon is needed to introduce the list.","A semicolon must be followed by a complete sentence, and the list is not one.","A colon cannot come right after a verb such as 'are'; it must follow a complete sentence.","Correct. A colon after a complete sentence introduces a list that spells out the three attractions."]},
{"id":"p9r3","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Basalt ___ when a thick flow of lava cools slowly and cracks into regular shapes as it shrinks.","o":["columns, mostly six-sided pillars of dark rock — form","columns — mostly six-sided pillars of dark rock, form","columns — mostly six-sided pillars of dark rock — form","columns; mostly six-sided pillars of dark rock — form"],"a":2,"ex":"The phrase about six-sided pillars is extra information that interrupts the sentence; an interruption opened with a dash must close with a dash.","ex_tr":"Ara söz (mostly six-sided pillars of dark rock) ek bilgidir; uzun çizgiyle (—) açılan ara söz yine uzun çizgiyle kapanmalıdır.","why":["It opens with a comma but closes with a dash; the two marks must match.","It opens with a dash but closes with a comma; the two marks must match.","Correct. The interruption opens and closes with matching dashes.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and 'Basalt columns' alone is not one."]},
{"id":"p9r4","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"After months of tests, the engineers finally ___ caused the turbine to shake at high speeds.","o":["identified, the loose bolt that","identified the loose bolt that","identified the loose bolt, that","identified: the loose bolt that"],"a":1,"ex":"'The loose bolt' is the object of 'identified', and 'that caused ...' tells which bolt is meant; no punctuation belongs between a verb and its object or before an essential 'that' clause.","ex_tr":"'The loose bolt', 'identified' fiilinin nesnesi; 'that caused ...' hangi cıvatanın kastedildiğini belirtiyor. Fiil ile nesnesi arasına ve gerekli 'that' cümleciğinin önüne noktalama işareti konmaz.","why":["A comma between the verb 'identified' and its object 'the loose bolt' wrongly separates them.","Correct. The verb runs straight into its object, and the essential 'that' clause follows with no comma.","The 'that' clause tells which bolt is meant, so it is essential, and an essential 'that' clause never takes a comma before it.","A colon must follow a complete sentence, and 'the engineers finally identified' is not complete without its object."]},
{"id":"p9r5","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Each June, the planning committee, which includes teachers from five schools, ___ its recommendations for the coming year to the school board.","o":["present","presents","were presenting","have presented"],"a":1,"ex":"The subject is 'committee', a collective noun acting as one body (note 'its'), so the verb is singular: 'presents'.","ex_tr":"Özne 'committee': tek bir grup olarak davranan bir topluluk ismi ('its' de bunu gösteriyor). Fiil tekil olmalı: presents.","why":["'Present' is plural; it agrees with 'teachers' or 'schools', which sit inside the 'which' clause and are not the subject.","Correct. 'Committee' is a single body, as 'its' confirms, so it takes the singular 'presents', and the present tense fits a yearly habit.","'Were presenting' is plural, but the subject 'committee' is singular, and the past tense does not fit a yearly habit.","'Have presented' is plural, but the subject 'committee' is singular, as 'its' shows."]},
{"id":"p9r6","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"The prize for local history went to Tobias Wren, a retired bus driver ___ spent twelve years recording the memories of the city's oldest residents.","o":["whom","which","who","whose"],"a":2,"ex":"The relative pronoun refers to a person and is the subject of 'spent', so 'who'.","ex_tr":"Zamir bir kişiyi (a retired bus driver) gösteriyor ve 'spent' fiilinin öznesi: who.","why":["'Whom' is an object form, but the pronoun here is the subject of 'spent'.","'Which' is used for things and animals, not for a person such as a bus driver.","Correct. 'Who' refers to a person and acts as the subject of 'spent'.","'Whose' shows possession and must be followed by a noun, as in 'whose memories', not by a verb such as 'spent'."]},
{"id":"p9r7","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Because air pressure is lower at high altitudes, water ___ at a lower temperature in mountain towns than at sea level, so pasta takes longer to cook there.","o":["boils","boiled","had boiled","will have boiled"],"a":0,"ex":"The sentence states a general truth, and its other verbs ('is', 'takes') are in the present, so the present tense 'boils' fits.","ex_tr":"Cümle genel bir bilimsel gerçeği anlatıyor; diğer fiiller de ('is', 'takes') geniş zamanda: boils.","why":["Correct. A general scientific fact takes the simple present, matching 'is' and 'takes'.","The past tense suggests something that happened once, but this is a fact that is always true.","The past perfect needs a later past event to come before, and the sentence describes no past event.","The future perfect describes something completed by a future time, but water boils this way now and always."]},
{"id":"p9r8","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Researchers are tracking a sea turtle ___ journey across the Pacific has already covered more than 9,000 kilometers since it was tagged.","o":["whom","who's","who is","whose"],"a":3,"ex":"The journey belongs to the turtle, so the possessive 'whose' is needed; 'who's' means 'who is'.","ex_tr":"Yolculuk kaplumbağaya ait, bu yüzden iyelik bildiren 'whose' gerekir. 'Who's' ise 'who is' demektir.","why":["'Whom' is an object pronoun and cannot show that the journey belongs to the turtle.","'Who's' is short for 'who is', and 'who is journey' makes no sense.","'Who is journey across the Pacific' makes no sense; the sentence needs a possessive.","Correct. 'Whose' shows possession: the journey belongs to the turtle."]},
{"id":"p9r9","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Known for her detailed drawings of beetles and moths, ___","o":["the museum asked the artist Rosa Lindqvist to illustrate its new guide to insects.","the artist Rosa Lindqvist was asked to illustrate the museum's new guide to insects.","Rosa Lindqvist's drawings were chosen for the museum's new guide to insects.","the museum's new guide to insects was illustrated by the artist Rosa Lindqvist."],"a":1,"ex":"An opening phrase describes whatever comes right after the comma. The one known for her drawings is Rosa Lindqvist, so she must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade, virgülden hemen sonra gelen ismi niteler. Çizimleriyle tanınan kişi Rosa Lindqvist olduğu için virgülden hemen sonra o gelmeli.","why":["This makes 'the museum' the one known for its detailed drawings of beetles and moths.","Correct. 'The artist Rosa Lindqvist' comes right after the comma, and she is the one known for her drawings.","This puts 'drawings' right after the comma, so the drawings, not the artist, are said to be known for their drawings.","This makes 'the museum's new guide' the thing known for detailed drawings of beetles and moths."]},
{"id":"p9r10","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"A new ferry route opened in March, ___ the trip between the two islands from three hours to forty minutes.","o":["it cut","cutting","this cut","it has cut"],"a":1,"ex":"After the comma, the participle 'cutting' begins a phrase that describes the result; a subject plus a verb there would make a second sentence and a comma splice.","ex_tr":"Virgülden sonra -ing biçimindeki 'cutting', yeni hattın sonucunu anlatan bir öbek başlatır. Özne ve fiil gelirse ikinci bir tam cümle oluşur ve 'comma splice' hatası çıkar.","why":["'It cut' gives the second part its own subject and verb, so the comma joins two sentences: a comma splice.","Correct. 'Cutting' starts a participle phrase that adds the result without creating a new sentence.","'This cut' also makes a complete second sentence after a comma: a comma splice.","'It has cut' gives the second part its own subject and verb, so the comma joins two complete sentences: a comma splice."]},
{"id":"p9r11","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"In a greenhouse study, the leaves of the desert shrubs were much smaller than ___ grown under the same lights.","o":["those of the rainforest plants","that of the rainforest plants","the rainforest plants","the rainforest plant"],"a":0,"ex":"The comparison must be between like things, leaves and leaves. 'Those of' stands for 'the leaves of', and it is plural like 'leaves'.","ex_tr":"Karşılaştırma aynı tür şeyler arasında olmalı: yapraklarla yapraklar. 'Those of' = 'the leaves of'; 'leaves' çoğul olduğu için 'that' değil 'those' kullanılır.","why":["Correct. 'Those of the rainforest plants' means 'the leaves of the rainforest plants', so leaves are compared with leaves.","'That' is singular, but it has to stand for 'leaves', which is plural.","This compares the shrubs' leaves with whole rainforest plants, an illogical comparison.","This compares the shrubs' leaves with a single whole plant rather than with other leaves, an illogical comparison."]},
{"id":"p9r12","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Although the stone bridge was designed for horse-drawn ___ still carries hundreds of cars and buses across the river every day.","o":["carts. It","carts; it","carts, it","carts, and it"],"a":2,"ex":"'Although ... carts' is a dependent clause; a comma separates it from the main clause 'it still carries ...'.","ex_tr":"'Although ... carts' bir yan cümle; ana cümleden ('it still carries ...') virgülle ayrılır.","why":["A period leaves 'Although the stone bridge was designed for horse-drawn carts' as a fragment.","A semicolon must join two complete sentences, and the 'although' clause cannot stand alone.","Correct. A comma ends the introductory 'although' clause and leads into the main clause.","'Although' already links the two clauses, so adding 'and' gives the sentence two joining words for one join."]},
{"id":"p9r13","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Most moths fly only at night and rest during the day. ___ the hummingbird hawk-moth feeds in bright sunshine, hovering in front of flowers much like a small bird.","o":["By contrast,","Similarly,","Therefore,","For example,"],"a":0,"ex":"Most moths are active at night, but this one feeds in sunshine. The second sentence is the opposite of the first: 'By contrast'.","ex_tr":"Çoğu gece kelebeği yalnızca geceleri uçar; bu tür ise parlak güneşte beslenir. İkinci cümle birincinin tersini söylüyor: By contrast.","why":["Correct. A moth that feeds in bright sunshine is the opposite of moths that fly only at night.","'Similarly' would mean this moth also flies only at night, but it feeds in sunshine.","'Therefore' would make its daytime feeding a result of other moths flying at night, which makes no sense.","'For example' would introduce a moth that flies only at night, but this one breaks the pattern."]},
{"id":"p9r14","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"Languages often borrow the names of foods along with the foods themselves. ___ the English word 'tomato' comes from the Spanish 'tomate', which was itself borrowed from Nahuatl, a language of central Mexico.","o":["Consequently,","However,","For instance,","Instead,"],"a":2,"ex":"The second sentence gives one case of the general pattern in the first, a food name borrowed along with the food: 'For instance'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle, birinci cümledeki genel durumun bir örneğini veriyor (yiyecekle birlikte adı da ödünç alınmış): For instance.","why":["'Consequently' signals a result, but the tomato story illustrates the pattern rather than being caused by it.","'However' signals a contrast, but the tomato story agrees with the first sentence.","Correct. 'Tomato' is one specific case of a food name borrowed along with the food.","'Instead' signals a replacement for something just mentioned, but nothing is being replaced."]},
{"id":"p9r15","t":9,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's new weekly market, held in the restored train station, draws nearly twice as many shoppers as the old one did. ___ the station has only a small parking lot, but most visitors arrive on foot or by tram.","o":["Similarly,","Therefore,","For instance,","Admittedly,"],"a":3,"ex":"The writer grants a weakness (little parking) and then answers it ('but most visitors arrive on foot or by tram'): a concession, so 'Admittedly'.","ex_tr":"Yazar önce bir eksikliği kabul ediyor (otopark küçük), sonra 'but' ile buna cevap veriyor. Bu bir kabul (concession) geçişi: Admittedly.","why":["'Similarly' would present the small lot as a point like the market's success, but it is a drawback, not a similar point.","'Therefore' would make the small parking lot a result of the market's popularity, but the lot's size does not follow from the number of shoppers.","'For instance' would make the small lot an example of the market drawing more shoppers, which it is not.","Correct. 'Admittedly' grants a drawback, the small parking lot, which the 'but' clause then answers."]},
/* Practice 10 */
{"id":"p10w1","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Only eleven short inscriptions in the ancient Kethic language have survived, all of them scratched on broken pottery. Because of this ___ of evidence, scholars who study the language's grammar must often rely on informed guesses, and some basic questions, such as how its verbs marked the past, may never be answered.","o":["dearth","glut","synthesis","repository"],"a":0,"ex":"Only eleven short inscriptions survive, so there is too little evidence, which is why scholars must guess: dearth.","ex_tr":"Yalnızca on bir kısa yazıt kalmış; kanıt bu kadar az olduğu için araştırmacılar çoğu zaman tahmine dayanmak zorunda: dearth (yetersizlik, kıtlık).","why":["Correct. Dearth means a supply that is not large enough, which is exactly why scholars must rely on guesses.","Glut means an excessive amount. Eleven short inscriptions are far too few to be a glut, and a surplus of evidence would not force scholars to guess.","Synthesis means combining ideas into a whole. The passage is about how little evidence exists, not about combining it.","Repository means a place where things are stored. The problem described is a shortage of evidence, and a storage place would not explain the guesswork."]},
{"id":"p10w2","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Although the new hull coating made fishing boats in Port Velmora far easier to clean, it proved ___ to the harbor's shellfish, whose numbers fell by half within three years of the coating's introduction.","o":["innocuous","integral","amenable","deleterious"],"a":3,"ex":"'Although' sets the coating's benefit against a drawback, and shellfish numbers falling by half show that the coating was harmful: deleterious.","ex_tr":"'Although' kaplamanın faydasını bir zararla karşılaştırıyor; kabuklu deniz hayvanlarının sayısının üç yılda yarıya inmesi kaplamanın zararlı olduğunu gösteriyor: deleterious (zararlı).","why":["Innocuous means harmless. Shellfish numbers falling by half show the coating did real harm, and 'Although' calls for a drawback.","Integral means essential to a whole. Nothing suggests the shellfish depended on the coating; their numbers fell after it arrived.","Amenable means open and responsive to suggestion. Nothing in the text responds to anything, and the halved shellfish population shows the passage is about harm.","Correct. Deleterious means harmful, and the shellfish population fell by half after the coating was introduced."]},
{"id":"p10w3","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"For generations, farmers on the slopes of Mount Kerem assumed that the volcano above them had died out for good. Rising gas levels beneath its crater now tell a different story: the mountain is not extinct but ___, quiet for the moment yet fully capable of erupting again.","o":["volatile","obsolete","dormant","desolate"],"a":2,"ex":"'Not extinct but ___' calls for a contrast with 'died out for good', and 'quiet for the moment yet fully capable of erupting again' defines the word: dormant.","ex_tr":"'Not extinct but ___' bir karşıtlık kuruyor: yanardağ sönmüş değil. Ardından gelen 'şimdilik sessiz ama yeniden patlayabilir' ifadesi kelimeyi tanımlıyor: dormant (uykuda, geçici olarak etkisiz).","why":["Volatile means liable to change rapidly and unpredictably. The passage calls the volcano 'quiet for the moment', not unstable.","Obsolete means outdated and no longer used, a word for tools or ideas. It cannot describe a mountain that may still erupt.","Correct. Dormant means temporarily inactive: quiet for now, but able to erupt again.","Desolate means empty and without people, but farmers live on these slopes, and the word says nothing about whether the volcano can erupt."]},
{"id":"p10w4","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"For three centuries, the Haldane pottery workshop kept the recipe for its deep blue glaze a closely guarded secret. Apprentices swore never to ___ it, not even to their own families, and the master potter wrote the formula down only once, in a coded notebook that was kept under lock and key.","o":["disparage","divulge","embellish","abridge"],"a":1,"ex":"Secrecy runs through the passage (a 'closely guarded secret', a coded notebook), so the oath was a promise never to reveal the recipe: divulge.","ex_tr":"Metnin her yerinde gizlilik vurgulanıyor ('sıkı korunan bir sır', şifreli bir defter); çıraklar tarifi kimseye açıklamayacaklarına yemin etmiş: divulge (sırrı açığa vurmak).","why":["Disparage means to belittle. The oath is about keeping a secret, and speaking badly of the recipe would not give it away.","Correct. Divulge means to make a secret known, which is exactly what the apprentices swore never to do.","Embellish means to decorate or exaggerate. The passage is about secrecy, not about adding to the recipe.","Abridge means to shorten a text while keeping its meaning. Shortening the recipe has nothing to do with keeping it from outsiders."]},
{"id":"p10w5","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"The dispute between two railway companies over the shared station at Linford was expected to be settled within a week. Instead, because both firms remained ___, each refusing to give up even a single platform, the case dragged on for three years until a court finally imposed a timetable.","o":["ambivalent","receptive","intransigent","taciturn"],"a":2,"ex":"The phrase right after the blank defines it: each firm refused to give up even one platform, so neither would compromise: intransigent.","ex_tr":"Boşluktan hemen sonraki ifade kelimeyi tanımlıyor: iki şirket de tek bir perondan bile vazgeçmedi, yani hiçbiri uzlaşmaya yanaşmadı: intransigent (uzlaşmaz).","why":["Ambivalent means having mixed feelings. Each firm held its position firmly; neither was unsure.","Receptive means willing to consider new ideas. Firms that refused to give up a single platform were not open to anything, which is why the case dragged on.","Correct. Intransigent means unwilling to compromise, as firms that would not give up even one platform were.","Taciturn means habitually quiet. The problem was a refusal to give ground, not a refusal to talk."]},
{"id":"p10w6","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"No written rule or public notice assigns the stalls in Kalsar's riverside market, yet for generations traders have rarely argued over who sets up where. Anthropologist Rosa Ferreira contends that the market runs on ___ agreements, which every trader follows but which no one has ever needed to put into words.","o":["explicit","tacit","provisional","fractious"],"a":1,"ex":"There is no written rule and no one has ever put the agreements into words, yet every trader follows them: tacit.","ex_tr":"Yazılı bir kural yok, anlaşmaları kimse dile getirmemiş; yine de her satıcı onlara uyuyor: tacit (söze dökülmeden anlaşılan, örtük).","why":["Explicit means stated clearly. These agreements are the opposite: no rule is written and no one has ever put them into words.","Correct. Tacit means understood without being stated, like agreements no one has ever needed to put into words.","Provisional means temporary. Agreements that have held for generations are anything but temporary.","Fractious means quarrelsome. The traders have rarely argued, so the agreements are the opposite of fractious."]},
{"id":"p10w7","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"The claim that the star Kestra-9 has an orbiting planet first rested on a single faint dip in the star's brightness, which might have been an instrument error. A year of observations from three separate telescopes did much to ___ the claim, recording the same dip every 41 days, exactly the pattern that a planet passing in front of the star would produce.","o":["undermine","postulate","disavow","buttress"],"a":3,"ex":"One uncertain dip became a regular pattern seen by three telescopes, the pattern a planet would produce, so the new evidence strengthened the claim: buttress.","ex_tr":"Tek ve belirsiz bir parlaklık düşüşü, üç teleskobun gördüğü düzenli bir örüntüye dönüştü; yeni gözlemler iddiayı güçlendirdi: buttress (desteklemek, sağlamlaştırmak).","why":["Undermine means to weaken. A regular dip every 41 days is exactly what a planet would produce, so the observations support the claim.","Postulate means to propose an idea that is not yet proven. The claim already existed; the new observations supplied evidence for it.","Disavow means to deny knowing about or being responsible for something. Telescope data cannot disown a claim, and this data backs it up.","Correct. Buttress means to strengthen support for an argument, which the repeated, regular dip does."]},
{"id":"p10w8","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"The ___ of the ballpoint pen, now found in nearly every home, office and classroom, makes it easy to forget how recently it became common; for much of the early twentieth century, most people still wrote with fountain pens or by dipping steel nibs into bottles of ink.","o":["ubiquity","novelty","paucity","efficacy"],"a":0,"ex":"'Now found in nearly every home, office and classroom' restates the blank, and being everywhere is what makes the pen seem as if it had always existed: ubiquity.","ex_tr":"'Neredeyse her evde, ofiste ve sınıfta bulunan' ifadesi boşluğu açıklıyor; tükenmez kalemin her yerde olması, onun hep varmış gibi görünmesine yol açıyor: ubiquity (her yerde bulunma).","why":["Correct. Ubiquity means being everywhere, which the passage restates as 'found in nearly every home, office and classroom'.","Novelty means newness. If the pen still seemed new, it would be easy to remember how recent it is, not easy to forget.","Paucity means too little of something. A pen found in nearly every home and classroom is plentiful, not scarce.","Efficacy means effectiveness. The passage describes how widespread the pen is, not how well it works, and effectiveness would not hide its recent origin."]},
{"id":"p10w9","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Historians still disagree about whether the painter Marta Kovacs supported the uprising that swept her city in 1848. Her only surviving remark on it, a single line in a letter to her brother, is so ___ that some scholars read it as quiet praise while others hear in it a note of mockery.","o":["equivocal","verbose","facetious","unequivocal"],"a":0,"ex":"'So ___ that' leads to a result: the same line is read as praise by some and as mockery by others, so it is open to more than one interpretation: equivocal.","ex_tr":"'So ___ that' bir sonuca bağlanıyor: aynı satırı kimi övgü, kimi alay olarak okuyor. Yani söz birden fazla anlama açık: equivocal (iki anlamlı, muğlak).","why":["Correct. Equivocal means open to more than one interpretation, which is why scholars read the same line in opposite ways.","Verbose means using too many words. The remark is a single line, and wordiness would not explain the two opposite readings.","Facetious means joking about a serious matter. That would make the line plainly mocking, so it would not explain why some scholars read it as praise.","Unequivocal means clear and leaving no doubt. A clear remark would not be read as praise by some and as mockery by others."]},
{"id":"p10w10","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"In 1898 the chemist Selim Arkan announced that he had isolated a new metallic element from ore mined near his hometown. When three other laboratories repeated his procedure and found only a mixture of known metals, Arkan chose to ___ the claim in a short published letter, admitting that his sample had been contaminated.","o":["proclaim","vindicate","recant","extol"],"a":2,"ex":"Other laboratories could not repeat the result, and Arkan admitted his sample was contaminated, so he publicly took back his claim: recant.","ex_tr":"Diğer laboratuvarlar sonucu tekrarlayamadı ve Arkan numunesinin kirlendiğini kabul etti; yani iddiasını herkesin önünde geri aldı: recant (sözünü geri almak).","why":["Proclaim means to announce publicly. Arkan had already announced the element; a letter admitting contamination withdraws the claim rather than announcing it.","Vindicate means to prove right. The other laboratories' results and the contaminated sample show the claim was wrong.","Correct. Recant means to say publicly that one's earlier statement was wrong, which a letter admitting contamination does.","Extol means to praise enthusiastically. A chemist admitting his sample was contaminated is not praising his own claim."]},
{"id":"p10w11","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Unlike writers who draft a novel in a few frantic weeks, Leyla Ozkan prefers to ___ on an idea for a year or more, turning it over in her mind during long walks before she commits a single sentence to paper.","o":["dawdle","ruminate","digress","expound"],"a":1,"ex":"'Unlike' contrasts frantic drafting with slow thought, and 'turning it over in her mind' for a year describes deep thinking over time: ruminate.","ex_tr":"'Unlike' aceleyle yazan yazarlarla yavaş düşünen bir yazarı karşılaştırıyor; bir fikri bir yıl boyunca yürüyüşlerde zihninde evirip çevirmek, onu uzun süre derin derin düşünmektir: ruminate (uzun uzun düşünmek).","why":["Dawdle means to move slowly and waste time. Turning an idea over for a year is purposeful thinking, not wasted time.","Correct. Ruminate means to think deeply about something over time, as she does on her long walks.","Digress means to wander off the main topic while speaking or writing. She has not written anything yet; she is thinking the idea through.","Expound means to explain something in detail to others. She turns the idea over privately in her mind, before saying or writing anything."]},
{"id":"p10w12","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"While cutting her documentary about the cleanup of Alder Creek from three hours to ninety minutes, editor Lin Zhao removed every interview she considered ___, keeping only those that bore directly on how the creek had been restored.","o":["salient","contentious","extraneous","candid"],"a":2,"ex":"The interviews she kept were the ones that bore directly on the restoration, so the ones she removed were off the subject: extraneous.","ex_tr":"Filmde kalan röportajlar, derenin nasıl eski hâline getirildiğiyle doğrudan ilgili olanlar; demek ki çıkarılanlar konu dışıydı: extraneous (konu dışı, gereksiz).","why":["Salient means most noticeable or important. An editor keeps the most important interviews; she would not cut them.","Contentious means causing argument. The passage gives relevance as her only test ('bore directly on'), not whether an interview was controversial.","Correct. Extraneous means irrelevant, the opposite of the interviews she kept, which bore directly on the restoration.","Candid means frank and honest. Nothing suggests she cut interviews for being honest; she cut the ones that were off the subject."]},
{"id":"p10w13","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Music historians often point out that the composer Ilse Brandt's best-known ___ was her habit of writing every tempo marking in green ink, a practice that no other composer in her circle shared and that her publishers never managed to explain.","o":["idiosyncrasy","paradigm","aversion","grievance"],"a":0,"ex":"A personal habit that no other composer in her circle shared, and that even her publishers could not explain, is a peculiar personal trait: idiosyncrasy.","ex_tr":"Çevresindeki hiçbir bestecide görülmeyen ve yayıncılarının bile açıklayamadığı kişisel bir alışkanlık, kişiye özgü bir tuhaflıktır: idiosyncrasy (kendine özgü huy, tuhaflık).","why":["Correct. Idiosyncrasy means a peculiar personal habit, like writing in green ink when no one else did.","Paradigm means a model or typical example. A habit that no other composer shared is the opposite of typical.","Aversion means a strong dislike. Writing every marking in green ink was something she did, not something she disliked.","Grievance means a cause for complaint. Her habit was a personal quirk, not a complaint she made."]},
{"id":"p10w14","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"When the bridge's night watchmen first reported faint cracking sounds, the engineers were inclined to ___ the reports, blaming the noises on cold weather shrinking the steel. Only after a support beam failed the following spring did they begin to treat such reports as serious warnings.","o":["scrutinize","corroborate","aggrandize","discount"],"a":3,"ex":"The engineers explained the noises away and only later treated the reports as serious, so at first they dismissed them: discount.","ex_tr":"Mühendisler sesleri soğuğa bağlayıp üzerinde durmadılar; raporları ancak bir destek kirişi kırıldıktan sonra ciddiye aldılar. Yani başta onları önemsiz saydılar: discount (önemsememek).","why":["Scrutinize means to examine closely. Blaming the sounds on the cold, and taking the reports seriously only later, shows the engineers did not examine them closely.","Corroborate means to confirm with evidence. The engineers explained the noises away instead of confirming the reports.","Aggrandize means to make something seem greater than it is. The engineers made the reports seem smaller, not greater.","Correct. Discount means to dismiss as unimportant, which is what blaming the noises on the cold did."]},
{"id":"p10w15","t":10,"sec":"wic","p":"Genetic evidence suggests that the two lizard species on Marune Island shared a single ancestor until about two million years ago, when rising seas split the island in two. Isolated on separate halves, the populations began to ___, and today they differ in color, body size and diet.","o":["converge","diverge","dwindle","intersect"],"a":1,"ex":"One ancestral population was split by the sea, and the two groups now differ in color, size and diet, so they grew apart: diverge.","ex_tr":"Deniz, ortak bir atadan gelen topluluğu ikiye böldü; bugün iki tür renk, boy ve beslenme bakımından farklı. Yani zamanla birbirlerinden ayrıştılar: diverge (ayrışmak, farklı yönlere gitmek).","why":["Converge means to come together. Populations kept apart that now differ in color, size and diet have grown apart, not together.","Correct. Diverge means to separate and go in different directions, as the two isolated populations did.","Dwindle means to shrink gradually. The passage says nothing about the populations getting smaller; it describes them becoming different.","Intersect means to cross or meet. The populations were isolated on separate halves of the island and could not meet."]},
{"id":"p10r1","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"Honeybees can see ultraviolet ___ use it to spot patterns on petals that are invisible to people but lead a bee straight to the flower's nectar.","o":["light; they","light, they","light they","light, which they"],"a":0,"ex":"Both halves are complete sentences ('Honeybees can see ultraviolet light' and 'they use it ...'), so a semicolon joins them.","ex_tr":"İki taraf da tam cümle; ikisini noktalı virgül bağlar. Tek virgül 'comma splice' olur, 'which they use it' ise dilbilgisi açısından bozuktur.","why":["Correct. Each side is a complete sentence, and a semicolon can join two complete sentences.","A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together into a run-on.","'Which they use it' is ungrammatical: 'which' already stands for the light, so 'it' repeats the object."]},
{"id":"p10r2","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The engineers who designed the Tolva Bridge faced an unusual ___ the river beneath it freezes solid for four months each year and then floods every spring.","o":["challenge,","challenge:","challenge","challenge, which"],"a":1,"ex":"A complete sentence ('The engineers ... faced an unusual challenge') is followed by an explanation of what the challenge was, so a colon introduces it.","ex_tr":"Tam bir cümleden ('... faced an unusual challenge') sonra zorluğun ne olduğu açıklanıyor; bu açıklamayı iki nokta başlatır.","why":["A comma between two complete sentences is a comma splice.","Correct. A colon after a complete sentence introduces an explanation, here what the challenge was.","With no punctuation, the two sentences run together, and 'challenge the river' misreads as if the challenge were aimed at the river.","'Which' would have to stand for the challenge, but 'the river ... freezes solid' already has its own subject, so the sentence falls apart."]},
{"id":"p10r3","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The ___ can regrow a lost leg, bones and all, within a few months, an ability that has made it a favorite subject of scientists who study how injured tissue heals.","o":["axolotl (a salamander from the lakes of central Mexico)","axolotl (a salamander from the lakes of central Mexico,","axolotl, a salamander from the lakes of central Mexico)","axolotl (a salamander from the lakes of central Mexico"],"a":0,"ex":"Extra information inside a sentence must open and close with the same kind of mark: an opening parenthesis needs a closing one.","ex_tr":"Cümle içindeki ek bilgi aynı işaretle açılıp kapanır: açılan parantez, parantezle kapanmalıdır.","why":["Correct. The extra information is opened and closed with parentheses, a matching pair.","It opens with a parenthesis but closes with a comma; the marks must match.","It opens with a comma but closes with a parenthesis; the marks must match.","The parenthesis is opened but never closed, so the rest of the sentence seems to belong inside it."]},
{"id":"p10r4","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"In a small greenhouse experiment, the tomato ___ no faster than the plants kept in a quiet room next door.","o":["plants, that heard music every day, grew","plants that heard music every day grew","plants that heard music every day, grew","plants, that heard music every day grew"],"a":1,"ex":"The 'that' clause tells which plants are meant, so it is essential and takes no commas, and no comma separates the subject from its verb 'grew'.","ex_tr":"'that' cümleciği hangi bitkilerin kastedildiğini belirtir; temel bilgidir, virgülle ayrılmaz. Özneyle fiil ('grew') arasına da virgül girmez.","why":["A 'that' clause is never set off by commas; these commas treat the information that identifies the plants as if it were extra.","Correct. The 'that' clause identifies which plants grew no faster, so it is essential and takes no commas.","A single comma between the subject 'plants' (with its clause) and the verb 'grew' separates them for no reason.","A comma before an essential 'that' clause is wrong; it cuts the plants off from the clause that identifies them."]},
{"id":"p10r5","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"At the bottom of Lake Kivra, nearly forty meters below the surface, ___ the wooden hulls of three cargo boats that sank in a storm more than a century ago.","o":["rest","is resting","rests","has rested"],"a":0,"ex":"The sentence is inverted: the subject, 'the wooden hulls', comes after the verb. 'Hulls' is plural, so the verb is 'rest'.","ex_tr":"Cümle devrik: özne ('the wooden hulls') fiilden sonra geliyor. 'Hulls' çoğul olduğu için fiil de çoğul olmalı: rest.","why":["Correct. The subject is 'the wooden hulls', which is plural, so the plural verb 'rest' agrees with it.","'Is resting' is singular and cannot agree with the plural subject 'hulls'.","'Rests' is singular, but the subject 'hulls' is plural; 'Lake Kivra' and 'the surface' are not the subject.","'Has rested' is singular and cannot agree with the plural subject 'hulls'."]},
{"id":"p10r6","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The Halvard Seed Bank keeps ___ underground vaults at minus eighteen degrees Celsius, a temperature at which most stored seeds stay alive for decades.","o":["their","it's","its","they're"],"a":2,"ex":"'The Halvard Seed Bank' is a single organization, so the possessive is the singular 'its' (no apostrophe).","ex_tr":"'The Halvard Seed Bank' tek bir kurumdur; iyelik tekil olmalı: its. 'It's' = it is, 'they're' = they are.","why":["'Their' is plural, but the vaults belong to the seed bank, a single organization.","'It's' means 'it is', which makes no sense before 'underground vaults'.","Correct. The seed bank is one organization, and 'its' is the singular possessive.","'They're' means 'they are', which makes no sense here, and the seed bank is singular."]},
{"id":"p10r7","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"When the first settlers reached the valley in 1840, they quickly ___ that the river flooded almost every spring, so they built their houses on the higher ground to the east.","o":["learn","have learned","will learn","learned"],"a":3,"ex":"The other verbs ('reached', 'flooded', 'built') are in the simple past, and the events happened in 1840, so the simple past 'learned' keeps the tense consistent.","ex_tr":"Diğer fiiller ('reached', 'flooded', 'built') geçmiş zamanda ve olay 1840'ta geçiyor; tutarlı seçenek: learned.","why":["The present tense clashes with the past-tense verbs around it and with the date 1840.","The present perfect cannot be used with a finished past time like 'in 1840', and it clashes with 'built'.","The future tense cannot describe what settlers did in 1840.","Correct. The simple past matches 'reached', 'flooded' and 'built' and fits an event in 1840."]},
{"id":"p10r8","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The exhibition traces how ___ games changed between 1850 and 1950, from hoops and marbles played in the street to board games printed by the thousands in factories.","o":["childrens'","children's","childrens","child's"],"a":1,"ex":"'Children' is already plural and does not end in s, so its possessive adds an apostrophe and s: children's.","ex_tr":"'Children' düzensiz bir çoğuldur ve s ile bitmez; iyelik için kesme işareti ve s eklenir: children's.","why":["'Childrens'' adds an s that 'children' does not have: the word is already plural, so its possessive is 'children's', with the apostrophe before the s.","Correct. 'Children' is an irregular plural that does not end in s, so the possessive adds 's.","'Childrens' is not a word, and the games belong to the children, which needs an apostrophe.","'Child's' is the possessive of one child, and 'how child's games changed' would need an article; the exhibition is about the games of children in general."]},
{"id":"p10r9","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"While mapping the seafloor off the island's northern coast, ___","o":["a chain of underwater hills that no chart had ever recorded was discovered by a team of geologists.","no chart had ever recorded the chain of underwater hills that a team of geologists discovered.","a team of geologists discovered a chain of underwater hills that no chart had ever recorded.","the discovery of a chain of underwater hills was made by a team of geologists."],"a":2,"ex":"An opening modifier describes whatever comes right after the comma. Only the geologists can be mapping the seafloor, so they must come first.","ex_tr":"Baştaki ifade ('While mapping the seafloor') virgülden hemen sonra gelen özneyi niteler; deniz tabanının haritasını çıkaranlar jeologlardır.","why":["This makes 'a chain of underwater hills' the thing doing the mapping.","This makes 'no chart' the thing doing the mapping.","Correct. The geologists are the ones mapping the seafloor, and they come right after the comma.","This makes 'the discovery' the thing doing the mapping."]},
{"id":"p10r10","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The clay tablet that archaeologists recovered from the riverbank in 2021 ___ a list of grain deliveries written about four thousand years ago.","o":["recording","which records","having recorded","records"],"a":3,"ex":"The subject 'The clay tablet' (with its 'that' clause) still needs a main verb, and 'records' supplies it.","ex_tr":"Özne ('The clay tablet ...') bir ana fiil bekliyor; 'recovered' fiili 'that' cümleciğine aittir. Ana fiil: records.","why":["'Recording' cannot be a main verb on its own, so the sentence is left as a fragment.","'Which records' starts another relative clause and leaves the subject with no main verb: a fragment.","'Having recorded' is not a main verb, so the sentence has no main verb and is a fragment.","Correct. 'Records' is the main verb the subject 'The clay tablet' needs; 'recovered' belongs to the 'that' clause."]},
{"id":"p10r11","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"Training to become a mountain guide in the Taral Range takes three seasons and involves reading avalanche reports, practicing rescues on steep ice, and ___ groups of beginners up easy routes under an instructor's eye.","o":["to lead","led","they lead","leading"],"a":3,"ex":"The things the training involves form a list, and each item is an -ing form: reading ..., practicing ..., and leading ....","ex_tr":"Eğitimin içerdiği işler bir liste oluşturuyor ve hepsi -ing biçiminde olmalı: reading ..., practicing ..., and leading ....","why":["'To lead' is an infinitive, which breaks the pattern set by 'reading' and 'practicing'.","'Led' is a past-tense verb, which does not match 'reading' and 'practicing' and clashes with the present tense of 'takes' and 'involves'.","'They lead' is a full clause with a new subject, so the last item no longer matches the two -ing forms before it.","Correct. 'Leading' matches 'reading' and 'practicing', so all three things the training involves share the same -ing form."]},
{"id":"p10r12","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"Last spring, hoping to plan new routes, the town's transit office sent out a survey that asked residents ___","o":["how often they rode the town's buses?","how often did they ride the town's buses?","how often they rode the town's buses.","how often did they ride the town's buses."],"a":2,"ex":"The question is reported inside a statement ('asked residents how often ...'), so it keeps statement word order and ends with a period.","ex_tr":"Soru bir cümlenin içinde aktarılıyor (dolaylı soru); düz cümle sırası ('they rode') kullanılır ve cümle nokta ile biter.","why":["The word order is right, but the whole sentence is a statement, so it ends with a period, not a question mark.","This uses direct-question word order and a question mark, but the question is reported inside a statement.","Correct. An embedded question uses statement word order ('they rode') and ends with a period, because the whole sentence is a statement.","'Did they ride' is direct-question word order, which does not belong in a reported question."]},
{"id":"p10r13","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The town's new reservoir holds enough water to supply every household for two full years without rain. ___ officials saw no need to limit water use during last summer's three-month drought.","o":["Consequently,","Nevertheless,","For instance,","In contrast,"],"a":0,"ex":"A two-year supply (cause) explains why officials did not limit water use in a three-month drought (result): 'Consequently'.","ex_tr":"İki yıllık su stoku sebep, üç aylık kuraklıkta kısıtlamaya gerek görülmemesi sonuç: Consequently.","why":["Correct. The large water supply is the cause, and the decision not to limit water use is the result.","'Nevertheless' signals something surprising despite the first sentence, but not limiting water use is exactly what a two-year supply would lead you to expect.","'For instance' introduces an example, but the officials' decision is a result of the reservoir's size, not an example of it.","'In contrast' signals an opposite idea, but the two sentences agree."]},
{"id":"p10r14","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"The museum's new climate system is designed to keep conditions in the galleries stable all year. ___ it holds the temperature at 20 degrees Celsius and the humidity at 50 percent, day and night, in every season.","o":["In contrast,","Instead,","Nonetheless,","Specifically,"],"a":3,"ex":"The second sentence gives the exact settings behind 'stable' (20 degrees, 50 percent), so it spells out the first sentence in detail: 'Specifically'.","ex_tr":"İkinci cümle 'stable' (sabit) sözünü somut ayrıntılarla açıklıyor (20 derece, yüzde 50): Specifically.","why":["'In contrast' signals an opposite idea, but steady settings are exactly what a stable system provides.","'Instead' signals a replacement for something rejected, but nothing in the first sentence is rejected.","'Nonetheless' signals something surprising despite the first sentence, but the second simply spells it out.","Correct. The second sentence gives the precise settings that make the conditions 'stable', which is what 'Specifically' introduces."]},
{"id":"p10r15","t":10,"sec":"wr","p":"Desert foxes lose excess body heat through their unusually large ears, which are thin and full of blood vessels. ___ African elephants cool off by flapping their enormous ears, which carry many blood vessels close to the skin.","o":["In contrast,","Similarly,","Instead,","As a result,"],"a":1,"ex":"Both animals use large ears full of blood vessels to lose heat, so the second sentence describes a similar case: 'Similarly'.","ex_tr":"İki hayvan da kan damarlarıyla dolu büyük kulakları sayesinde ısı atıyor; ikinci cümle benzer bir durumu anlatıyor: Similarly.","why":["'In contrast' signals a difference, but the two animals cool off in the same way.","Correct. Elephants, like desert foxes, use large ears full of blood vessels to lose heat.","'Instead' signals a replacement for something rejected, but the elephants' method does not replace the foxes'; it parallels it.","'As a result' would mean the foxes' ears cause what the elephants do, and there is no such link."]}
];
